KANT_TR['reflm/guyer'] = {"reflm:3489":["expresses *propositiones tavtologicas*.",["*propositiones tavtologicas*: “tautological propositions”, in Kant's spelling."]],"reflm:3490":["Boring pastime is *contradictio in adiecto*.\nAmusing pastime is tautology.",["The wordplay is lost: langweilig (“boring”, literally “long-while”) and kurzweilig (“amusing”, literally “short-while”) against Zeitvertreib (“pastime”, literally “driving away time”)."]],"reflm:3491":["visible darkness",[]],"reflm:3492":["Above and \n*pia fraus*",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","*pia fraus*: “pious fraud”."]],"reflm:3493":["In the right margin \nApparent contradictions in the government of the world. In the mysteries. |\nIn the riddles. In the witty notions. Which are resolved. A book would be shorter etc. etc. In the writing of a great man the contradictions are held to be apparent.",["“In the mysteries … In the riddles … In the witty notions” render bare datives (Den Geheimnissen, Den Rätzeln, Den witzigen Einfällen), which seem to continue “in der Regirung”.","“apparent contradictions” renders Scheinwidersprüche; “apparent” at the end renders scheinbar."]],"reflm:3494":["*identitas et contradictio explicita et implicita*.\nOn M §. 13 »patens«:\n*indemonstrata*.\nOn M §. 13 »latens«:\n*demonstrata*.",["*identitas et contradictio explicita et implicita*: “explicit and implicit identity and contradiction”; *indemonstrata* / *demonstrata*: “undemonstrated” / “demonstrated”."]],"reflm:3495":["*pia fraus*.",["*pia fraus*: “pious fraud”."]],"reflm:3496":["*latens* is *pia fraus*.",["*latens*: “latent”; *pia fraus*: “pious fraud”."]],"reflm:3497":["Also tautology.",[]],"reflm:3498":["The ground is something other than the consequence.\nNothing contains the ground in itself.\nPrior, posterior. Antecedent and consequent.",["“ground” renders ratio, “consequence” rationatum.","“the ground in itself” renders rationem in se ipso, which can also be read “its own ground in itself”."]],"reflm:3499":["Opposition logical, real.",["Latin: Oppositio Logica, realis."]],"reflm:3500":["In the right margin \nThe real ground of actuality is the efficient cause.",["“real ground” renders realgrund; “actuality” Wirklichkeit; “efficient cause” Wirkende Ursache."]],"reflm:3501":["Connected are those of which, one being posited, another is posited.",["“Connected” renders connexa."]],"reflm:3502":["That which not being posited, another is not posited, is a condition.",[]],"reflm:3503":["the consequence being posited, the ground is not posited, but required.",["“consequence” renders rationatum, “ground” ratio."]],"reflm:3504":["ground either analytic (logical) or synthetic (real ground).",["The words in parentheses are Kant's German in a Latin line: logisch, Realgrund."]],"reflm:3505":["that which being posited another is necessarily posited, is a ground.",[]],"reflm:3506":["the consequence not being posited, the ground is not posited.",["The Latin non rationato posito may also be read “a non-consequence being posited”."]],"reflm:3507":["That which being posited (if I posit it) another is posited (s determinately) according to a rule, is a ground.\nfrom the consequence to the ground the inference is not valid.\ncondition: that which not being posited another is not posited.",["“(if I posit it)” renders Kant's German gloss wenn ich es setze.","“inference” renders consequentia."]],"reflm:3508":["ideal and real.",[]],"reflm:3509":["*nexus* is also of coordinated things, not always of subordinated things.",["“coordinated things”, “subordinated things” render coordinatorum, subordinatorum."]],"reflm:3510":["Something, Nothing, no thing.",["“no thing” renders kein Ding."]],"reflm:3511":["In the left margin \nOf possibility. Of the something. Of the thing.",[]],"reflm:3512":["in certain, in all regard (*respectu*).",["“regard” renders Betracht."]],"reflm:3513":["connected (g mutually) either through agreement or opposition.",["“connected” renders connexa; “mutually” inuicem; “agreement” consensum."]],"reflm:3514":["In a series (g of coordinated things and) of subordinated things.",[]],"reflm:3515":["Primitive and subaltern ground.",["“ground” renders ratio."]],"reflm:3516":["primitive or subaltern ground.",[]],"reflm:3517":["a ground which is itself a consequence.",["“ground” renders ratio, “consequence” rationatum."]],"reflm:3518":["Of condition in general — limiting.",["“limiting” renders einschränkende."]],"reflm:3519":["Above and \nthe ground either suffices for the consequence or [sibim] also for itself; if the former: it is *secundum quid* sufficient, if the latter: it is *simpliciter*.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source; [sibim] is a deleted, unfinished sibimet.","*secundum quid*: “in a certain respect”; *simpliciter*: “absolutely”."]],"reflm:3520":["logical predicate. Determines:",[]],"reflm:3521":["He who is a human being is determined with regard to learnedness, but not as human being. I.e. not the logical *subiect* human being, but the real one, namely the one who is thought through the concept of humanity.",["“human being” renders Mensch, “humanity” Menschheit; “learnedness” Gelehrsamkeit."]],"reflm:3522":["that in which there is the relation of ground to consequence is called determining.\nwhat is of itself undetermined is determined by another.",["“relation” renders respectus."]],"reflm:3523":["logical predicate or determination.",["Latin and German mixed: praedicatum logicum oder determination."]],"reflm:3524":["See p.12.\nOn M §. 36 »realitas — negatio«:\nof itself, not relative to another concept, which is logical, not metaphysical or transcendental.",["“transcendental” renders transscendentalis."]],"reflm:3525":["Absolute and respective concepts.\nas those of the ground and of the consequences. are respective. Only the respective ones can be made distinct.",["“consequences” renders folgen."]],"reflm:3526":["A book in the library\n*deest* — negation: is missing. What is not in its place. (g *abest*: what is not in its time.)\n*deficit* — when there is missing what ought to be, i.e. negation opposed to perfection (g is lacking).\n*abest* — when [it] one knows where it otherwise is (g is not present).",["“is missing”, “is lacking”, “is not present” render Kant's German fehlt, Mangelt, ist nicht zugegen.","*deest*, *deficit*, *abest*: “is wanting”, “is deficient”, “is absent”."]],"reflm:3527":["This is a logical *respectus*.\n(s *realis*.)",["*respectus*: “relation”; *realis*: “real”."]],"reflm:3528":["The essence of a thing (adjectivally).\nA being: *ens*.",["“essence” and “being” both render Wesen."]],"reflm:3529":["proper\nFurther, from σ-ψ:\nare those whose concepts can be reciprocated with the subjects.",["“proper” renders eigenthümliche (also “peculiar”); “subjects” renders Subiecten."]],"reflm:3530":["From the assertoric to the problematic, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:3531":["ψ−χ?? M 16.\nFrom the negation of the assertoric (g antecedent) not to the negation of the problematic.",[]],"reflm:3532":["*nullo*\nBetween §. 59 and §. 60 stand the words:\nfor if the upper proposition is true, then from a particular nothing at all can follow.",["“upper proposition” renders obere Satz, i.e. Obersatz (major premise); “true” renders war (wahr)."]],"reflm:3533":["i.e. from the concept neither of thoroughgoing determination nor of a reality can one not infer to existence.",["The double negative (weder … noch … nicht) is kept as in the German.","“existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:3534":["»*omni, non tollitur possibilitas*« one cannot say; what does not exist at all must also not be possible in every respect.",["*omni, non tollitur possibilitas*: “by all, possibility is not removed”.","muß auch nicht can be read “must not” or “need not”; “in every respect” renders in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:3535":["that which contains reality",[]],"reflm:3536":["Thing and its predicates",[]],"reflm:3537":["chimera, fantasy, ideal.",[]],"reflm:3538":["determination of a being, insofar as what is posited or how many times something is posited is considered.",["“being” renders ens."]],"reflm:3539":["The difference of one thing from another through the plurality of the homogeneous that is contained in [it] both is magnitude. The difference of the heterogeneous is quality.",["“plurality” renders Vielheit; “magnitude” Größe."]],"reflm:3540":["that which is possible through one thing posited several times is a *qvantum*.",[]],"reflm:3541":["better *quanta*; that something is a *Qvantum* can be cognized absolutely, but how great (*qvantitas*), only relatively.",["“cognized” renders erkennen."]],"reflm:3542":["*quantum* and *quantitas*.",[]],"reflm:3543":["In the right margin, \nTaken substantively.\nThe adjectival is hypothetical.\nFurther, likewise in the right margin, above line 3, beside M §. 74 »Quicquid — est«, probably also on »unitas categorica«:\nquantitative and qualitative",[]],"reflm:3544":["That of which there are not several of the same kind is unique.",["“kind” renders Art; “unique” einzig."]],"reflm:3545":["Nonsense in which there is method.",[]],"reflm:3546":["On »Sectio VII. Perfectum.«, or \nthe perfection of a being in general is the totality of reality.",["The editorial note “bzw.” (“or”) is cut off in the source.","“totality” renders omnitudo."]],"reflm:3547":["they agree either logically, insofar as they do not contradict, or really, insofar as they are not really opposed to one another.",[]],"reflm:3548":["On »Sectio VII«:\nperfection taken adjectivally or substantively. likewise unity, truth, possibility.",[]],"reflm:3549":["In every way contingent",["Latin: Omnimode contingens."]],"reflm:3550":["Something is contingent either to a thing (i.e. it is not necessary to it), e.g. composition to the elements, or breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3551":["There are contingent essences, namely the essences of all contingent things, e.g. motions.",["“essences” renders Wesen, which can also be read “beings”."]],"reflm:3552":["Unconditioned necessity is both with regard to the external and internal det breaks off.",["“det” renders the unfinished Best (Bestimmung, “determination”)."]],"reflm:3553":["Thoroughly necessary.",["“Thoroughly” renders Durchaus (also “absolutely”)."]],"reflm:3554":["Either with regard to one determination or to all: 1. the opposite is internally or absolutely not thinkable, i.e. there cannot be more necessary beings.\nFurther:\ni.e. under the condition under which something necessarily belongs to a thing, this thing cannot be determined in another way; but it can be subjected to another condition. e.g. A bell cannot be otherwise than [int] internally hollow and open; but the same metal can nevertheless be melted into a bar.",["“[int]” renders the deleted, unfinished [inn].","“more necessary beings” can be read “further necessary beings” or “more than one necessary being”."]],"reflm:3555":["After the opening word of M §. 125 Kant wants inserted:\n*perdurabilis*",["*perdurabilis*: “perdurable”."]],"reflm:3556":["the subject is altered, the determinations are varied, the state breaks off.",["“state” renders status."]],"reflm:3557":["Beside and \nWhether matter, motion is reality.\nWhether vice is *realitas* or *negatio*.\nnegations in objects are limitations.\n*realitas affecta negationibus*.\nPlurality of reality and degree.\nSpace and time have no degree.\nExtensive, protensive and intensive magnitude.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","“objects” renders Obiekten; “limitations” Schranken; *realitas affecta negationibus*: “reality affected with negations”."]],"reflm:3558":["*opposito realis*",["*opposito* is probably a slip for oppositio: “real opposition”."]],"reflm:3559":["In the lower margin, \nlimitation.",["“limitation” renders limitatio."]],"reflm:3560":["Occamists (g Thomists) nominalists. Scotists realists.",[]],"reflm:3561":["nominalists and realists.",[]],"reflm:3562":["not 2/3 + 3/4 = 1. Of the 21 quarters of the city of Paris.",["“quarters” renders viertheilen, “fourth parts”, which also means city quarters."]],"reflm:3563":["Kant alters the definition at the beginning of M §. 157 so that it reads:\nA composite altogether the same as its parts is a whole.",[]],"reflm:3564":["That which cannot exist except either as part or as consequence or as accident.",["“consequence” renders rationatum."]],"reflm:3565":["totality either of a *quantum* or of a composite as such. Part of a whole either of a *quantum* or of a composite.",["“as such” renders qva talis."]],"reflm:3566":["*qvantum* of *qvantitas* — — indeterminate.\ndeterminate is *qvantitas*.\n[assignable parts are]\na givable *qvantum* is that which [whole] as complete can be actual. assignable parts are those which can in themselves be clearly cognized. [whose parts] that in which the multitude of assignable parts is indeterminate (whose parts cannot be given in every way except through the whole), is continuous.",["“givable” renders dabile; qvantum dabile est can also be read “a qvantum is givable”.","“indeterminate” renders indeterminatae, which agrees with qvantitatis."]],"reflm:3567":["It is false; for that which is greater than nothing, of it a part is equal to nothing; therefore nothing is part of something, which is contradictory.",["“is contradictory” renders repugnat."]],"reflm:3568":["Absolutely (i.e. in a certain respect) possible.\nIn a certain respect also possible.",["The gloss “h.e. respectu qvodam” after Absolute is kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3569":["determines strictly, broadly.\nlatitudinarian, who admits exceptions.",[]],"reflm:3570":["the more it approaches universality and lacks restrictions.",[]],"reflm:3571":["The intensive magnitude of laws rests on necessity, i.e. the lack of exceptions. No rule without exception, but a law is without exception.",["The second sentence is Latin: Nulla regula sine exceptione, sed lex est absque exceptione."]],"reflm:3572":["*substratum*, stable.",[]],"reflm:3573":["The real subject is substance.",[]],"reflm:3574":["an accident can be a logical subject.",[]],"reflm:3575":["*Phaenomenon substantiatum*",["“substantiated phenomenon”."]],"reflm:3576":["If these were true, the state of no being would be immutable.",[]],"reflm:3577":["On the heading: »Sectio VIII. Status«:\nWhether it is all the same to say: a thing has altered, or: its state (if need be inner) has altered.",["“has altered” renders habe sich verändert; “if need be” allenfalls."]],"reflm:3578":["Properly it is not a *modus* but the substance that is altered. For that which is altered remains; alteration is only the change of its determinations.",["“alteration” renders Veranderung, “change” Wechsel."]],"reflm:3579":["On the last two sentences of M §. 210:\nThe [human] body (s the lifeless,) works; the living (g makes) acts (s *operatur*); the free does (s *facit*).",["“[human]” renders the deleted, unfinished [Menschl].","“works” renders wirkt, “makes” machet, “acts” handelt, “does” thut."]],"reflm:3580":["substance actuating substance (g producing) creates*.\n(g as cause of duration conserves.)\nsubstance adding a complement to an acting substance concurs.\n*(s as cause of the origin of a substance creates.)",["“acting” renders actrici; “origin” ortus."]],"reflm:3581":["If all undergoing of the substance is properly the determination of the activity of the undergoing subject, then no substance can be determined internally even by God to other and greater *accidentibus* than are possible from its nature; otherwise another fundamental force and substantiality would be produced in it, and thus it would be recreated, and not be the identity of the subject.",["“undergoing” renders Leiden and leidenden (passio, also “suffering”); “fundamental force” Grundkraft; “recreated” umgeschaffen."]],"reflm:3582":["The inner possibility of a force (s of acting) is the faculty. e.g. Someone can have a great faculty for science, but has as yet no force of acuity etc. etc.",["“force” renders Kraft, “faculty” Vermögen, “acuity” Scharfsinnigkeit."]],"reflm:3583":["The internally sufficient ground for it is force; if it is also externally sufficient, then it is living force; if it is externally insufficient, then it is dead force. Dead force on account of the hindrance is *conatus*.",[]],"reflm:3584":["Faculty and force,\n*potentia* — *actus*.\nForce acts, the faculty does not.",[]],"reflm:3585":["The inner principle of the possibility of acting is the faculty*, e.g. machine**.\n*(g The inner determining ground of the faculty for acting is striving (*conatus*). The faculty with a striving to act is force. Force, insofar as it is internally sufficient for the actuality of the action, but without effect, is dead force; this always proves a hindrance. (s Force sufficient internally as well as externally is living.))\n**(s The faculty together with a determining ground of it (e.g. an applied lever) is force. Force (thus faculty and determining ground of it together) [is brings] is for itself sufficient for the actuality of the action. Thus *posita vi* (g *per se*) *ponitur actio*. But another can conflict with the determining ground [of the] in the force.[This] The state of a force in this conflict is striving (*conatus*) and that which contains the ground of the action's not arising, the hindrance. Every *conatus* is an action, against which, however, something counteracts. Hence it is dead force. Living force is not greater than the *conatus*, but brings about a desired effect.\nNB. For the *conatus* a time is required, if it is to become living force. For if the support were taken from a weight and gravity were not to act further, then the one impression would give no motion.)",["“determining ground” renders Bestimmungsgrund, “striving” Bestrebung, “lever” Hebebaum, “impression” Eindruk.","*posita vi … ponitur actio*: “the force being posited … the action is posited”.","“proves a hindrance” renders beweiset … Hindernis, which can also be read “proves (the presence of) a hindrance”."]],"reflm:3586":["To act means: to contain the sufficient ground of the *accidentis*.\nThe possibility of the action is the faculty.\nThe internally sufficient ground of the action is force.\nIt is dead if [externally] a ground of the opposite is there.\nThe opposed ground is *impedimentum*; if it is another substance: *resistentia*.",["*impedimentum*: “hindrance”; *resistentia*: “resistance”."]],"reflm:3587":["Concordant forces: Of these one adds to the other something homogeneous, e.g. solar attraction to the moon. uncombined: there the accession is 0. Opposed: –a; take away just as much, thus the difference of the adding from the taking away is double the amount of the *accessorii*. Hence the danger of diminution is always greater than the gain of the addition.",["“uncombined” renders nicht verbundene; “accession” Hinzukunft; “adding” Hinzuthuung."]],"reflm:3588":["The substance, insofar as it contains the sufficient ground of a determinate *accidentis*, acts; insofar as it contains the ground of accidents in general, has a force. This is either internally sufficient or insufficient; in the latter case it is a mere faculty, in the former it is either externally insufficient, i.e. a *conatus*, or externally sufficient: *vis viva*.\nWith every dead force another opposed one is also dead.",["“accidents” renders accidentzen; *vis viva*: “living force”."]],"reflm:3589":["Where there is force, there is also action (in lifeless beings), but not therefore at once effect, because an inner or outer resistance [a] is the counteraction of a force which cancels the effect of the former. In living beings faculty and force are distinguished; in lifeless ones e.g. a rope has as much faculty to bear a load held on it as it exercises actual force of cohesion.",["“cohesion” renders Zusammenhang (elsewhere “interconnection”); “cancels” aufhebt."]],"reflm:3590":["The real relation of a substance as cause to [its] the [there] through it possible effect is called force. This before the effect is called *conatus*; without it, because a resistance occurs, *vis mortua*.",["“[there]” renders the deleted [da], apparently the start of dadurch.","*vis mortua*: “dead force”; “without it” (ohne dieselbe) may refer to the effect or to the force."]],"reflm:3591":["At the very top of the page:\nBecause all *nexus* is logical or real. so is breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3592":["origin is existence which follows non-existence.",["“origin” renders ortus."]],"reflm:3593":["Arising and perishing, beginning and ceasing. The latter goes to accidents.",["“Arising and perishing” renders Entstehen und vergehen, “beginning and ceasing” anheben und aufhören."]],"reflm:3594":["The substance *phaenomenon* is perdurable.",["Latin: Substantia phaenomenon est perdurabile."]],"reflm:3595":["Above the final word of M §. 233:\nComposite said quantitatively, qualitatively.",[]],"reflm:3596":["simile of the bow.",[]],"reflm:3597":["tensed.",["“tensed” renders angespannt (also “drawn”, “strained”)."]],"reflm:3598":["Space and time have boundaries.",["“boundaries” renders terminos."]],"reflm:3599":["Sameness and difference belong to the relation of comparison.",["“Sameness” renders Einerleyheit, “difference” Verschiedenheit."]],"reflm:3600":["Generically, specifically and numerically identical.",[]],"reflm:3601":["totally the same —\nnumerically the same\n(g congruent) (g as to internal things —\nin different relations)\nas to quantity —\nare also as to quality, because only homogeneous things can be equal,\nas to quality —\ncan be also as to quantity, and then would be congruent.",[]],"reflm:3602":["the boundary of space is distinguished from limits.\nA spherical surface is properly not bounded anywhere. but is bounding.",["“boundary” renders terminus, “limits” limitibus; “bounded”, “bounding” terminata, terminans."]],"reflm:3603":["Because the boundary is a ground of the limitations, it is no part, in that it adds nothing to the magnitude, but rather cuts off; the cutting off, however, belongs to both that is cut off; thus the boundary is common.",["“boundary” renders Grentze / Grenze, “limitations” Schranken."]],"reflm:3604":["The quality of the boundary of the extended is the figure.",[]],"reflm:3605":["The community of boundaries. Two points do not touch, therefore *distant*.",["*distant* is Latin: “they are distant”."]],"reflm:3606":["Two points lie always in one line, three points [in a plane and 4 in a corporeal space.], through which no common straight line can be drawn, lie in a plane, and four points, of which through three no straight line can go, lie in corporeal space. Yet from these *data* two bodies are possible, namely according to two opposed regions.\nThis means as much as this. If one point is given, then from a given distance another point can be given in infinitely many positions (g i.e. it lies in a spherical surface). If two points are given, then the third point lies in a circle*. If three are given, then the fourth point lies in two positions on opposite sides**. All position of the points can be determined geometrically from certain *data*, but the last position of the fourth point only with regard to our body.\nOne must therefore be acquainted with the whole corporeal space in order to draw from a given point with a given distance a line; for this can be drawn only to a determinate point in the surface.\n*(g the third point is determined, but not its position.)\n**(g for then no rotation is possible.)",["\"regions\": Gegenden; \"position\": Stelle; \"position\" in \"All position\": Lage; \"distance\": Weite.","\"plane\": Fläche; \"surface\": Oberfläche; \"must be acquainted with\": kennen."]],"reflm:3607":["*Continua* have not only limitations, but also boundaries.",["\"limitations\": Schranken; \"boundaries\": Grentzen."]],"reflm:3608":["The mathematical concepts are unity, plurality, number, infinity, (the relations) [equality, inequality], composition of one magnitude out of another one or several times or out of its parts, or also out of many magnitudes *per synthesin vel positivam vel negativam* (through synthesis either positive or negative); hence the idea of equality, inequality and relations. The objects of mathematics are space, time, motion or any other ground either as a *totum* or as a ground estimated, the former through the many that is in it, the latter through that which is posited by it.",["\"objects\": obiecte (the Latinate word).","\"estimated\": geschätzt; \"relations\": Verhältnisse."]],"reflm:3609":["If [magnitudes of] the quality of a magnitude is of such a kind that the action [the *coordinatio*] of raising one of them, which has a certain extensive magnitude, intensively to the second degree is a multiplication, then there are no more than 3 *dimensiones*. But where the magnitude |\ngrows not intensively, *multiplicando*, but extensively, *addendo*, there is only one *dimensionem*. Of things that are at the same time one can represent the generation of the multitude out of unity as in flux.",["The German construction \"die Handlung ..., die eine derselben ... zu erheben\" is irregular (a relative \"die\" followed by an infinitive); rendered as \"of raising\".","\"multitude\": Menge; \"generation\": Erzeugung."]],"reflm:3610":["It is remarkable that with the point, line and plane, when they move (g perpendicularly), the motions of the elements do not coincide with one another, but they do with the body. It is also not necessary that it happen perpendicularly, if only it is not in the direction in which the motion of the previous elements would meet. The question is: why then do they meet with the fourth dimension, namely that after the generation of a body. Does the ground of this not perhaps lie in the combinations in general?",["\"coincide with one another\": in einander fallen; \"meet\": zusammentreffen.","\"plane\": Fläche."]],"reflm:3611":["as if rest were an accident.",["Latin: qvasi qvies esset accidens."]],"reflm:3612":["Only in *phaenomenis*.",["Latin ablative plural: \"in appearances\"."]],"reflm:3613":["as quantity\nforgot, however, to strike out the second word of the text, »continuatio«, which with the new definition must of course drop away.",["Latin: ut quantitas.","The second line is the AA editor's remark; its subject (Kant) is unexpressed in the German."]],"reflm:3614":["in all parts of time",["Latin: omnibus partibus temporis (ablative without preposition)."]],"reflm:3615":["*ens a se* is not changed. For it is in no determination through others, hence not to something else.",["*ens a se*: a being from itself. Latin \"non mutatur\" is passive.","\"determination\": Bestimmung."]],"reflm:3616":["*respectus* and relation; the former: of comparison, the latter: of influences.",["\"relation\": relation (the Latinate German word), set against Latin *respectus*."]],"reflm:3617":["An accident, insofar as it is a cause, is not called efficient.\nA substance, insofar as its accident is a cause, is likewise not efficient.",[]],"reflm:3618":["either efficient or a *conditio sine qva non*",["*conditio sine qva non*: condition without which not."]],"reflm:3619":["Büffon seeks *nexus effectivos*, others final ones. Epicurus the former in the organization.",["Latin: nexus effectivos (effective connections); \"final ones\" renders \"finales\"."]],"reflm:3620":["On the concluding sentence of M §. 332:\nThe causality in the cause is not nobler than the effect.",["Latin: non est nobilior effectu.","\"causality\": causalitaet."]],"reflm:3621":["Estimable (worthy of estimation)\nInestimable (not capable of estimation),\njust as \"incorrigible\" has a twofold sense.",["\"Estimable\": Schätzbar; \"Inestimable\": Unschätzbar, which can mean both \"priceless\" and \"not capable of being estimated\".","\"incorrigible\": unverbesserlich, which can mean both \"incorrigible\" and \"not capable of improvement\"."]],"reflm:3622":["objective",["German: obiectiv."]],"reflm:3623":["Idea. Archetype. Model. Example. Similarity, analogy are different.",["\"Archetype\": Urbild; \"Model\": Muster; \"Example\": Beyspiel."]],"reflm:3624":["absolute universality of the concept.\nabsolute universality of the whole, i.e. the metaphysical universe of things. this would prove that there is only one single world, that the cause is the highest.",["Latin: universitas (\"universe\", also \"totality\") beside universalitas (\"universality\").","\"that the cause is the highest\": causam esse summam."]],"reflm:3625":["Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:3626":["*casus* respective. absolute.",["Latin *casus*: chance, or case; respectivus, absolutus."]],"reflm:3627":["Nature is here taken only adjectivally.",[]],"reflm:3628":["On the heading »Mundus optimus«:\nThe world as *summum bonum derivativum*.",["*summum bonum derivativum*: derivative highest good."]],"reflm:3629":["[All coercion is either a] All necessitation (g is either a necessitation) unwillingly (g or willingly,) either aesthetic or physical, a necessitation of the will itself breaks off?",["\"coercion\": Zwang; \"necessitation\": Nöthigung.","\"unwillingly\": wiederwillen; \"willingly\": Mit Willen.","\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:3630":[") M 277'.\nThe action, insofar as it arises from the faculty of desire in such a way that I can become conscious that I am not determined to it breaks off.",["\"faculty of desire\": Begehrungsvermögen; \"am not determined\": sey ... nicht bestimmt (subjunctive).","\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:3631":["Separation of the soul from the body.",[]],"reflm:3632":["removal of the *nexus* either virtual or local.",["Latin: remotio nexus vel virtualis vel localis; \"virtual\": virtualis, pertaining to force (vis)."]],"reflm:3633":["The separation of the soul from the body is either local or virtual.",[]],"reflm:3634":["Beside the second-to-last sentence of M §. 784:\nMetempsychosis of Michel Angelo, Gallilaei and Newton. (by Nelli in Florence.)",["\"by Nelli\": von Nelli, which could also be read \"from Nelli\"."]],"reflm:3635":["Internal predicate of God\nexternal\n{\nindependence dependence of all things on him",["Latin: Dei praedicatum internum / externum; independentia, dependentia omnium ab illo. The brace is the AA's layout."]],"reflm:3636":["logical perfection (*sufficientia*) or practical.",[]],"reflm:3637":["*nexus* with contingent things",["Latin: nexus cum contingentibus."]],"reflm:3638":["He is not changeable.\nBeing existence is not changeable; persistent.",["\"Seyn Daseyn\": \"Seyn\" can be read as the noun \"being\" or as the possessive \"his\" (sein); it may be a first word left uncorrected.","\"existence\": Daseyn; \"persistent\": beharrlich."]],"reflm:3639":["Because things subsist through God, it seems as if they subsisted in him.\nHe is not the soul of the world.",["Latin: Non est anima Mundi; the subject (\"he\" or \"it\") is unexpressed."]],"reflm:3640":["*Causatum maximum*. The highest created good.",["*Causatum maximum*: the greatest thing caused."]],"reflm:3641":["as active being he has nature.",["\"active being\": thatig Wesen."]],"reflm:3642":["On the word »theologia«, which he underlined\nectypal: from worldly affections. accommodated through reduction: analogical.",["Latin: ectypa: e mundanis affectionibus. per reductionem accomodata: analogica."]],"reflm:3643":["The cognition of the good (g of that which is good in the whole (in every actual relation)) from the idea of the whole as a ground of choice is wisdom.\nWhat is to be good in every respect must be derived from the idea of the most perfect whole.\n(g Prudence is the knowledge of the means to ends that are not completely in our power. Thus with regard to the opinion and freedom of other human beings.)\nThe wisdom of the human being consists in his doing without the ends that are not in his power.",["\"in every respect\": in aller absicht; \"relation\": Beziehung.","\"Prudence\": Klugheit; \"knowledge\": Kentnis; \"choice\": Wahl.","\"doing without\": entbehre."]],"reflm:3644":["Wisdom is directed to the good.\nPrudence to the useful.",["\"is directed to\": geht auf."]],"reflm:3645":["The faculty of cognizing the relation of all things* to the highest good** is wisdom; to the (g good in appearance) particular: prudence (not of God.).\n(s The adequacy of cognition to the highest good and its possibility is either merely theoretical — science, or moral: wisdom.)\n*(s The faculty of the cognition of the [means] best means to it is art-wisdom; the faculty of the cognition of the end together with the willing of it: moral wisdom.)\n**(s either to it itself or to the derived highest good. The former is the ground of the latter, but only in the divine understanding.)",["\"relation\": Verhaltnis; \"adequacy\": Angemessenheit; \"art-wisdom\": Kunstweisheit.","\"the willing of it\": dem Willen desselben, also \"the will for it\".","\"to it itself\": ihm selbst, which may refer to the highest good or to God (\"to him himself\")."]],"reflm:3646":[") M 359.\nWisdom in general is the perfection of cognition insofar as it is adequate to the whole of all ends (either to the physical or moral whole).",["\"is adequate\": angemessen ist."]],"reflm:3647":["Wisdom is the (g subjective) *principium* of the unity of all ends [a pr] according to reason, consequently happiness united with morality.",["\"happiness\": Glükseeligkeit; \"morality\": Sittlichkeit.","The deleted \"[a pr]\" is left as the AA prints it (probably \"a priori\" begun)."]],"reflm:3648":["The highest good has two parts. Which is the condition, which is the conditioned. Morality or happiness?",["\"Morality\": Sittlichkeit; \"happiness\": Glükseeligkeit."]],"reflm:3649":["Whether we can fathom the ends of the divine wisdom in nature and whether that is physicotheology? — He is the only Holy — Blessed — Wise.",["\"fathom\": ergründen; \"Blessed\": Seelige."]],"reflm:3650":["The divine understanding derives all cognition of the particular from the whole of all possibility and every particular end from the whole of all ends. Everything from itself.",[]],"reflm:3651":["M359':\n[Kl] Skill is the faculty of reason with regard to means to arbitrary ends, prudence with regard to the sum of all subjective ends.\nWisdom: the faculty of determining every particular end through the whole of all objective ends. |\nM 359: |\nHence »a human being« and »wise« is a contradiction, except in morality as a model.",["The deleted \"[Kl]\" is the beginning of \"Klugheit\" (prudence).","\"Skill\": Geschiklichkeit; \"arbitrary\": beliebigen (ends one pleases); \"sum\": Summe."]],"reflm:3652":["It is all the same whether many or few means lead to the end, if only the end is attained; superfluous means are in the human being only blameworthy because they hinder his other ends. Yet because the sufficient ground of the means is only in the end, nothing more can be in the former than what is required for the latter.",["\"bey den Menschen\" can be singular or plural; \"seine\" (his) suggests the singular.","\"only blameworthy because\": the \"nur\" may attach to \"tadelshaft\" or to the \"weil\" clause."]],"reflm:3653":["As means in and for itself (g in the execution) there can surely never be more than in the end, i.e. there is not more in the cause than in the effect. but as means in the intention there can be more than what is required for the end, from error. But there can be much present besides what fulfills the whole end, and the law of parsimony is not for God.",["\"in and for itself\": an und vor sich selbst; \"vor Gott\" can be read \"for God\" (vor = für) or \"before God\".","\"parsimony\": Sparsamkeit; \"be present\": daseyn."]],"reflm:3654":["The measuredness in means.",["\"measuredness\": Abgemessenheit (proportioning, exact measure)."]],"reflm:3655":["Not everything is ends or means, at least not everything good in the sequels of certain causes.",["\"sequels\": Folgen."]],"reflm:3656":["The divine wisdom is the ground of the agreement of his choice with itself without exceptions and collision from one plan.",["\"agreement\": Einstimung; \"choice\": Wahl."]],"reflm:3657":["The parsimony of the divine decree in the choice [of the] of that alone which is useful as a means to the highest represented good is no property of the divine wisdom. It is the property of the impotent one, who, if he does more in one case than what contributes something to some use, neglects other use.",["\"parsimony\": Sparsamkeit; \"decree\": Rathschluß; \"property\": Eigenschaft.","\"use\": Nutzen (also \"benefit\"); \"impotent one\": ohnmächtigen."]],"reflm:3658":["The objectively antecedent will is that whose [object] motive is what is universal in the objects.\nThe objectively consequent will is that whose motive is what is individual in the objects.",["Latin: voluntas obiective antecedens / conseqvens; \"motive\": motivum."]],"reflm:3659":["The best will determines [perfection] the particular volition in the totality, i.e. according to the human manner of speaking: in the universal, insofar as it is restricted through particular conditions.",["\"totality\": omnitudine; \"volition\": volitionem."]],"reflm:3660":["The divine will, insofar as it is directed to that which the object has in common with others that [not ge] he does not will, is called *antecedens*; insofar as it is directed to that whereby it distinguishes itself from all others, is called *conseqvens*.",["The deleted \"[not ge]\" breaks off inside a word (\"nicht ge...\"); its end is left untranslated.","\"it distinguishes itself\": \"er\" may refer to the will or to the object (Gegenstand)."]],"reflm:3661":["*voluntas antecedens* is the one that is directed to a universal concept of an object (as undetermined). *conseqvens*: to the determinate concept of the object.",["\"the one that\": der, masculine, agreeing with \"Wille\" rather than Latin voluntas.","\"object\": obiect."]],"reflm:3662":["The object of the highest intelligence is the highest good according to cognition and as author according to will.\nThe highest good in the whole and also the relation of each individual to it:\n1. the relation to the universal concept of the good. *voluntas antecedens*.\n2. to the thoroughly determined cognition of this world. *voluntas conseqvens*.",["\"object\": Gegenstand; \"relation\": relation.","\"thoroughly determined\": durchgangig bestimmten."]],"reflm:3663":["One could still ask whether reward is a matter of justice or of benevolence.",["\"benevolence\": Gütigkeit."]],"reflm:3664":["On M §. 907 Kant has made a sign after the words »bonum morale«, which he underlined, to which in the right margin the same sign before the word\n*meritum*\ncorresponds, which is evidently to take the place of the two words of the text. For Kant adds:\nFor whether *bonum morale* in general is merely [wisdo] wise goodness.",["All but the last line is the AA editor's description.","The last line is syntactically incomplete in the German (\"Denn ob ... ist\").","\"[wisdo]\" renders the deleted fragment \"[Weishei]\"."]],"reflm:3665":["Rewards are either gifts: gratuitous rewards\n—\n—\n—\nwages: — — owed.\nmoral: of wisdom,\npragmatic: of prudence.",["Latin: praemia, munera, mercedes, debita; \"owed\": debita (due)."]],"reflm:3666":["Punitive justice is distinct from vindictive justice; the former is exercised by him whose right, the other by him whose person has been injured.",["\"Punitive justice\": Strafgerechtigkeit; \"vindictive justice\": Rachgerechtigkeit (justice of revenge)."]],"reflm:3667":["God does not punish the offense to his person (*laesae maiestatis*), but, because the human being cognizes God only according to the analogy with human beings, the malice that the human being expresses, that he would be capable [it] of it in relation to other human beings. [Hence] Otherwise we would become confused in the infinity of the crime.",["\"[it] of it\": the deleted accusative \"sie\" is replaced by the genitive \"ihrer\", referring to Bosheit (malice).","*laesae maiestatis*: of injured majesty."]],"reflm:3668":["Punitive justice belongs to wisdom.",[]],"reflm:3669":["On the opening word of M §. 910:\ndistributive or vindicative",["Latin: distributiva vel vindicativa."]],"reflm:3670":["distributive justice is not instrumental, but final.",["Latin: iustitia distributiva non est instrumentalis, sed finalis."]],"reflm:3671":["Kant alters the first sentence of M §. 910 so that it reads:\nThe justice of God is not remunerative, but punitive; it is either pragmatic: of the ruler, or moral: of the judge, because it is sin.",["\"because it is sin\": quia peccatum est, which can also be read \"because sin has been committed\".","\"of the ruler\": des Regenten; \"of the judge\": des Richters (German in the Latin sentence)."]],"reflm:3672":["Whether there is a crime of injured divine majesty.",["Latin: Crimen laesae majestatis divinae."]],"reflm:3673":["All these properties are the moral [which], chiefly on the ground that God demands the interior of the human heart [conformable] in agreement with him and his presence.",["\"das Gott\" stands for daß Gott; \"die moralische\" lacks the plural ending.","\"conformable\": Gemaß (deleted); \"in agreement\": einstimmig; \"interior\": das innere."]],"reflm:3674":["He demands long-suffering among human beings in punishing.\nThe benevolence that postpones the punishment of the offender for his improvement.",["\"long-suffering\": Langmuth; \"benevolence\": Gütigkeit."]],"reflm:3675":["He punishes according to the order of nature in the whole only after a long time.\nHe punishes only in conformity with the infinite *progressus*.",[]],"reflm:3676":["He [wills] loves impartiality in the judgment of human beings.\nHe has no favorites. e.g. the Jewish people.",["\"in the judgment of human beings\": im Urtheil der Menschen, either judgment passed by or on human beings."]],"reflm:3677":["The human being cannot always derive the particular good that God distributes from the universal provision; hence he is entitled to regard it as a particular provision and to thank God for it, yet also to believe that in a way unknown to him this is nevertheless universal.",["\"provision\": Vorsorge (not Vorsehung, providence)."]],"reflm:3678":["He administers equity, i.e. before his court a transgression of equity is a violation of strict right.",["\"equity\": Billigkeit; \"strict right\": strengen rechts."]],"reflm:3679":["He judges from the standpoint of a human being, who is a limited being. The human being can regard many a thing as a preference that God gives him over others.",["\"preference\": Vorzug (also advantage); \"over others\": für andere, also \"for others\"."]],"reflm:3680":["He demands sincerity, which with regard to God is commonly very much lacking, in that one affects praises and devotions that one does not feel, out of fear pretends to believe much and applies oneself to flattery.\n(s God is open in his revelations necessary to us, not hidden, not enigmatic and ambiguous. He does not lead us into temptation.)",["\"sincerity\": Aufrichtigkeit; \"devotions\": Ergebenheiten."]],"reflm:3681":["He wills truthfulness among human beings.\n(s one must not say of God what is completely identical with the predicates through which we think his moral perfections.)",["\"truthfulness\": warhaftigkeit."]],"reflm:3682":["*beatus*. Self-sufficiency in well-being.",["\"Self-sufficiency\": Selbstgnugsamkeit; \"well-being\": Wohlseyn."]],"reflm:3683":["He is above the [nature] world as the author (nature) and as the lawgiver (freedom).",[]],"reflm:3684":["end without motives.",["Latin: finis absqve motivis."]],"reflm:3685":["actuation of the world and government.",["Latin: actuatio mundi et regimen."]],"reflm:3686":["On the last two sentences of M §. 950:\nThe preservation of its form conforming to the final end in time is called gubernation: administration.",["\"final end\": Endzwek; \"administration\": Verwaltung; \"its\": derselben, which may also be \"their\"."]],"reflm:3687":["not influx, which is [action] modification, not actuation of substance.",["Latin: non influxus, qvi est [actio] modificatio, non actuatio substantiae."]],"reflm:3688":["A continued beginning is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3689":["*Concursus* to nature or freedom.\nProvidence with respect to laws (determining them) is general, with respect to events is special.",["Latin: Providentia respectu legum (illas determinans) est generalis, eventuum est specialis."]],"reflm:3690":["He does not concur in the formal element of actions. He is not the author of evil according to the ordinary direction.",["Latin: Non concurrit ad formale actionum; the subject is unexpressed."]],"reflm:3691":["Permission. Allowing is not permission.",["\"Permission\": Erlaubnis; \"Allowing\": Zulassung."]],"reflm:3692":["Lawgiver at the beginning,\nRuler in the duration,\nJudge at the end. properly in the infinity of the progression.",["\"progression\": Fortganges."]],"reflm:3693":["who is not subjected to the power of another.",[]],"reflm:3694":["397. E I 689.\nIs he who [s] binds the creature in conformity with an absolute will, without * taking their use under the laws into consideration. Thus there are despotic and patriotic laws.\n*(g taking into consideration the agreement of their own will governed through reason, i.e. as machines.)",["\"binds\": verbindet (also \"obligates\").","\"their\": ihren, plural, though \"das Geschöpf\" is singular."]],"reflm:3695":["The will that, in regard to the general grounds [of the same] of willing, is determined to a certain action is *antecedens*. That which is determined in regard to the *individui*: *consequens*.",["*antecedens* / *consequens*: the antecedent and consequent will (voluntas antecedens, consequens), Kant's Latin kept.","*individui* is a Latin genitive (\"of the individual\"), kept as Kant wrote it after \"in Ansehung des\"."]],"reflm:3696":["Here God is represented as if he resolved upon everything possible, which part he would take out for his choice.",["\"he would\" is not in the German, which has only \"welchen theil ... herausnehme\"; the clause depends loosely on \"beschlösse\" (resolved, decided)."]],"reflm:3697":["A decree is consequent will, hence with respect to an individual.\nAntecedent will is that whose impelling cause is only something general, which is common to all objects of choice.\nThe decree [none] of founding this world is a decree of an individual, hence it is not universal. There is no universal decree. but a decree entire or total, different not from a [partial] particular one, but from a partial one.",["\"decree\" renders decretum; \"consequent/antecedent will\" voluntas consequens/antecedens; \"impelling cause\" caussa impulsiva; \"objects of choice\" obiecta arbitrii.","\"entire or total\" renders universum sive totale, as against universale (universal)."]],"reflm:3698":["is the highest good of the world, insofar as it is opposed to the highest good of an individual.",["\"highest good\" renders summum bonum; the subject is missing in the source."]],"reflm:3699":["Unconditioned without qualification the divine choice never is, but perhaps unconditioned in regard to the moral quality of the person, as despotism; for God's aim could after all be [and d] directed to the world's best in the whole, and this be the condition.* God cannot sacrifice a (g rational) creature to the world-whole, but may well permit that it sacrifice itself.\n*(g Here it is assumed that God uses the rational creature merely as means to the perfection of the whole. But there is no moral worth in the world where rational beings are used merely as means.)",["\"choice\" renders Willkühr (arbitrium); \"world's best\" renders weltbeste; \"world-whole\" Weltganzen.","\"as despotism\" (als despotism) may attach to \"unconditioned\" or to \"choice\"; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:3700":["Whether the divine will is in itself determined by motives, or is merely thought by us according to this analogy?",["\"motives\" renders Kant's foreign word motiven, not Bewegungsgrund."]],"reflm:3701":["Fate.",["\"Fate\" renders Verhängnis (fatum); it can also mean dispensation, doom."]],"reflm:3702":["as the end of the will",["tanquam fine voluntatis: fine is ablative (\"as by/with the end\"); the construction it depended on is missing."]],"reflm:3703":["You ask: [is] who is happier in the world, the virtuous or the vicious? if one investigates it, then among the advantages of the [virtuous] wicked there will always be something mixed in that the [vicious] virtuous does not desire and on account of which he would not want to exchange his state for that of the other; thus he is in fact more content with himself than one thinks. The ills that befall the virtuous do not properly befall virtue, but are common to all. If the general laws were to be restricted only to the relation of the pious and the godless, then say: which, then, are the pious. Will not the one take this one for deserving of punishment, but the other another |\nfor such, and would God's justice then be cognized by all. Bread is the reward not of virtue but of industry. If, finally, you give the human being everything good, say: are you then content, do you not always desire more, and will God well be able to find an end to your wishes. The true prize of virtue is the inner stillness of the soul, the other goods overthrow or corrupt it. Learning, posthumous fame, wealth: all do not have the true good with them. Thus virtue alone makes the true happiness, which in abundance as well as in want, in weeping as well as in gladness finds something that satisfies it. Since virtue thus finds no want, wishing counts for nothing.\nSelf-love, which combines itself with the love of God and of one's neighbor, constitutes the happiness of human beings. The greater the love, the further extended: the greater is the happiness. God begins with love at the whole and extends it to the parts, but human love begins from itself and spreads little by little over the whole. On such a one the earth smiles from all sides, and the godhead itself sees its image in his soul.",["\"the vicious\" renders Lasterhafte, \"the wicked\" Boshaften; \"ills\" Übel; \"prize\" Preis (also price).","\"overthrow or corrupt it\": sie may be the stillness or the soul (Seelen, old singular); the ambiguity is kept.","\"Thus virtue alone makes\": nur may also qualify \"makes\" or \"the true happiness\"."]],"reflm:3704":["S. I:\nOutline of optimism.\nOptimism is that doctrinal system [which from the presup|position\nof a supremely benevolent, wise and almighty original being] the [presence] ills of the world [with the concept] from the presupposition of an infinitely perfect, benevolent and almighty original being to [unite] justify, in that one convinces oneself that [of such nothing but that] notwithstanding all apparent contradictions, what has been chosen by this infinitely perfect being must nevertheless be the best among everything possible, and ascribes the presence of evil not to the choice of the divine good pleasure, but to the unavoidable necessity of the essential defects of finite things, [which] that, in that they have been brought along into the plan of creation without its fault through the decree of permission, are nevertheless through its wisdom and goodness so turned to the best of the whole that the displeasure which [they them before] the sight of them in particular can arouse is, through the compensation that the divine goodness knows how to arrange, perfectly made good in the whole, since thus this world is, among all that were possible through the divine power, the best, and that which piecemeal could be better, [the without] it might not through the bricht ab.\n[Herr von Leibnitz] One will, if one examines the outline that Leibniz gives of his principles [with] precisely, find that this idea expresses his opinion as precisely as possible. One need only consider this division into the necessary or metaphysical and into the contingent or hypothetically physical and hypothetically moral ills. One must consider his distinction between the antecedent and consequent will, of which the first [everything without] strives to exclude all evil, the second [through the] includes it in its plan as unavoidable consequence of the [finitude] eternal nature of things, then one will be convinced of the |\n[correctness] truthfulness of our portrayal. Leibniz represents the goodness of God, spreading itself without measure, as the pull of a stream that drives everything found in its sum total to motion with equal force, but carries along the heavy cargo ships, which possess more natural force of inertia than those of less mass, more slowly than these.\nS. II:\nHe represents God as he is ready to reveal himself with [his] the whole infinity of all properties in the works of creation, [but] and how he has a true displeasure at the ills, the sight of which so greatly disquiet the mind of the well-disposed. but he lets them hope, from the goodness, wisdom and power of this Lord, sufficiently cognized from other undeniable grounds, that the defects will be compensated in interconnection with advantages and that, [so much] though the ills may in the end also disfigure the [plan] best plan, [it] they could nevertheless in the whole [not] without still greater deviation not have been prevented. To be sure, freely acting beings could have avoided many an evil action, to the great good pleasure of God; but it was [not in relation to] an unavoidable necessity, among two ills, of which one is the lack of freedom, the other of the morally best, to choose the least, and there were also in the best plan other hindrances that could move God, out of apprehension of still greater absurdities, [to] to omit the arrangement of certain moving causes that could prevent some kinds of ill. In short. It was not possible otherwise, there had to be evil. Thanks be to the eternal wisdom, that he permitted the least and has carried everything in the whole out most gloriously to his praise. It is true, it seems to me, we do not yet see where the compensation will properly turn out that is to indemnify the whole for its |\npartial defects. but [getra] are you not assured that God is (g so), (g as he) must be in order to be able to expect everything good from him. In this case you can expect nothing other than, in the sequel, a complete satisfaction, at the least a complete justification of the divine justice and goodness.\nS. III:\nComparison of the doctrinal concept of Pope with optimism, and advantage of the former.\nLeibniz admitted that the deviations and defects which, as true ills, put the well-disposed in distress, were so in fact; but he reserved for himself to excuse the highest wisdom [of] (g which he cognized from other grounds, on account of) the permission. [The best] Thus the properties of God were put in safety for the benefit of those who have insight and docility enough to give assent to the metaphysical proofs of the divine existence. The remaining part of those who [on account of] at the sight of the world would gladly recognize the trace of God. remains in distress. Pope chose a way which, in order to make the beautiful proof of God [to] perceptible to all human beings, the most apt [is] among all possible is, and which, what precisely constitutes the perfection of his system, (g even) subjects all possibility to the dominion of an all-sufficient original being, under which things can have no other properties, not even such as one calls essentially necessary, that do not agree together perfectly for the ex|pression\nof its perfection. He goes through creation piecemeal, chiefly there where it seems most to lack agreement; yet he shows that every thing which we would gladly wish away out of the plan of the greatest perfection is also, considered by itself, good, and that one need not (g beforehand) have an advantageous prejudice about the wisdom of the ordering being in order [it as] to win assent for it. The essential and necessary determinations of things, the general laws, which are put in relation to one another through no [foreign, extorted from them] (g forced) union into a harmonizing plan, will adapt themselves as it were of themselves to the maintenance of perfect ends. Self-love, which has only one's own enjoyment as its aim and which evidently seems to be the cause of the moral disorder that we observe, is the origin of that beautiful [property] agreement that we admire. [Wealth can itself] Everything that benefits itself finds itself in the necessity of being at the same time useful to others. The general bonds which connect the whole together in an unexplored |\nS. IV: |\nway make it that the single advantages always relate to the advantage of the other things, and indeed through a natural sequence. Thus a general law of nature sets that love [fast through the drives] which preserves the whole fast, and indeed through such moving causes as naturally also produce that ill whose sources we would gladly see annihilated.\nCan one well, when one sees the essential constitutions in so general a natural harmony, suppose that what so adapts itself to an excellent whole is at the same time the cause of such evil bricht ab.\nOf the general perfection of the world-constitution in the physical and moral sense.\nThe foremost rule of the perfection of the world is that it [so] be complete in the highest degree, that everything be there that is possible and that neither in the chain of beings nor in the manifoldness of their variation anything be lacking that is only capable of existence; for there is for the world in general no greater infirmity than the nothing in any one of its parts. Hence the [revelation] field of the revelation of the divine power comprehends all genera of finite things and [through] with a kind of richness it extends itself even to the defects and loses itself not otherwise than through all stages of diminution from the highest degrees of perfection down to the nothing.\nLet us, upon this presupposition, hear with contempt the complaints of those to whom heaven, in their opinion, has allotted no pleasing lot of perfections. I would gladly, says one, have less [over] darkness in the understanding and less vehemence in the sensible inclinations, if I were only so fortunate as to have no [inclination to sin] other inclination than that for virtue. If most considered that, when they wish themselves such properties as presuppose an alteration of their nature, it means just as much as to desire that they not be at all and that in their place another being of the desired constitution might be present, |\nthen one would go into oneself, for self-love has an abhorrence of annihilation and is rather content with the state, as it may be allotted to it, than that it bricht ab mit der Seite.",["\"ills\" renders Übel, \"evil\" das Böse; \"defects\" Mängel; \"moving causes\" Bewegungsursachen; \"properties\" Eigenschaften, \"constitution(s)\" Beschaffenheit(en).","\"bricht ab\" and \"bricht ab mit der Seite\" (breaks off with the page) are the AA's notes, kept in German.","\"One need only consider\" renders \"Man darf ... erwegen\" (darf = may, or need); \"sets ... fast\" keeps the split verb setzt ... fest around the deletion."]],"reflm:3705":["S. I:\nDefects of optimism.\nLeibniz was right that he named his doctrinal edifice a theodicy or defense of the good cause of God. For in fact it is nothing other than a justification of God against the imputation that he might perhaps be the author of evil, through the assurance that, so far as it lies with him, everything is good and that it is at least not his fault if [everything] not every thing turns out as perfectly as it ought to turn out according to the wish of the upright.\nThe errors of this doctrinal concept are far too important for one to be able to stop at it. Leibniz [represents] represents the rules of (g having as their end) perfection as they conflict with one another in their exercise. He regards the exceptions as necessary defects and perceives the action befitting the highest wisdom in this, that it makes the choice on the side of the best. just as a seafarer throws a part of his wares overboard in order to save the ship and the rest. It is true, the wisdom and goodness of God triumph here over all reproaches. But what will one say of infinity (g and) of independence. From what causes may the essential determinations of things have that discord which makes the perfection, each of which in particular would increase the satisfaction of God, incompatible in one combination. What sort of [strange] inscrutable conflict is it between the universal will of God, which aims solely at the good, and the (g metaphysical) necessity[of the |\nmetaphysical properties], which will not adapt itself to it with complete agreement (g without exceptions). If the evils, through a fatality necessary in I know not what way, extort from God their permission, without having aroused [on their account] satisfaction in him, then they put this most blessed being in a certain kind of displeasure, which can indeed be mitigated to some degree by the justification of innocence on his side, but not quelled. If everything was good in the whole or is still good in the parts, then unfailingly the sight from all sides is the source of a true gratification. Why then must it be so arranged that everything in the parts |\nS. II: |\nbe unpleasant, only in order to arouse satisfaction in the whole. If God abhors vices and torments, [why] if he does not desire them, but only permits them: why then was it necessary that they must be present, [since they the] granted even that they cannot be excluded, in order not to make room for still greater defects; this excuse serves, to be sure, to acquit God of the fault, but it will never resolve the important doubt why the [necessary propert] essential necessity has something which [God against his] conflicts with the universal will of God and extorts from him the permission, without having gained his satisfaction. The whole error rests in this, Leibniz places the plan of the best world partly in a kind of independence, partly in a dependence on the will of God. [All] All possibility is spread out before God. God sees, reflects on, examines it; he is steered to one side by the determinations inherent in them according to the measure of their (g particular) perfections and to the other side according to that which they make up in combination; this comparison occasions his decree. [The world is really not so because God wants to have it so, but because it cannot be done, to make it |\notherwise. The independence of the eternal natures comes first. The dependence consists only in the plan, in which God strives to order them as well according to the rules of the best as their essential determinations permit.]\nThe world's essence [good] is not [merely good because] solely so because God wants to have it so [and], but because it was not possible otherwise, only ei breaks off, but still leaves room for 7 lines.\nS. III:\nThe second chief error of [doctr] optimism is that the evils and absurdities which are perceived in the world are excused only from the presupposition of the existence of God. and that one must therefore (g beforehand) believe that there is an infinitely benevolent and infinitely perfect being, before one can assure oneself that the world, which is assumed to be his work, is beautiful and regular, instead of the universal agreement [and] of the arrangements of the world, if they can be cognized in and for themselves, offering the most beautiful proof of the existence of God and of the universal dependence of all things on him. The surest and easiest proof, therefore, of the actuality of an all-sufficient, infinite[ being] benevolent and infinitely wise being, which is cognized from the consideration of the excellent arrangements that the world shows everywhere, is invalidated by the doctrinal edifice of Herr v. Leibniz. It seems to me that an Epicurus would answer him who built on this proof: If the agreement that you perceive in the world seems to you to prove [to] an ordering wisdom as its author, then you must admit that [that part] the world for the greatest part does not depend on it, because everywhere for more than half it contains absurdities and repugnant deviations within itself. I do not accept your subterfuge |\nthat of that wisdom which has perhaps ordered some parts [with] to prudent ends it is to be presumed that it has likewise hidden in the rest proofs of its supervision. I would rather, therefore, conclude: if[you] the wise higher cause has not been able to bring all things into a plan of harmonizing beauty, then at least not all things were, according to their properties, subject to its satisfaction, and [that] the eternal fate, which limits the [intention] power [of this deity] of the capable cause [only room to in] so greatly and extorts from it consent to gross evils, thereby robs it of all-sufficiency |\nS. IV: |\nand [in that it weighs it with the rest, sub] subjects it to that very same necessity.",["\"in die Schantze Schlagt\" (in die Schanze schlagen): to put at stake, sacrifice; rendered \"throws overboard\". Wohlgefallen = satisfaction, Vergnügen = gratification; Lehrgebäude = doctrinal edifice, Lehrbegriff = doctrinal concept.","\"unendlich[en Wesens] gütigen\": with the deleted ending the text read \"infinite being\"; with it struck it reads \"infinitely benevolent\".","\"nur ei bricht ab, läßt aber noch Raum für 7 Zeilen übrig\": \"ei\" is Kant's broken-off word; the rest reads as the editor's remark, translated as it stands. \"Der Welt wesen\": Wesen = essence or being."]],"reflm:3706":["S. I:\nIf, among the various predicates that may belong to a thing, existence could (g also) be counted as one of them, then certainly no proof could be demanded that would be more cogent and at the same time more comprehensible, in order to establish the existence of God, than the Cartesian one. For among all possible things there is one in which [all possible (g the highest) reality that is to be met with in one] all realities are to be met with that can be together in one being.[Among these] To these realities, i.e. truly positive predicates, existence also belongs; consequently existence belongs [some] to the most real of all beings on account of its inner possibility. One also objects in vain against this that [all reality only in thought with one another] such a possible thing includes existence within itself only in the understanding, i.e. is posited only as the thing itself is in thought, but not [in the real understanding] outside of thought, for in such a way we would have to say of all predicates that belong to a [thing] possible thing: they did not belong to it in fact, but were only posited in it in thought. This latter does indeed take place when one arbitrarily combines something with a concept that is not necessarily posited through it; e.g., thus wings are posited in thought on a horse in order to make a Pegasus, hence [the winged horse] wings belong to some horse only in thought. By contrast, where the connection |\nof a predicate |\nS. II: |\nwith a thing is not arbitrary, but is combined through the essence of the things themselves, there it does not belong to it because we think it in it, but it is necessary to think it in it, because it belongs to it in itself. For which reason I cannot say that the equality of the angles with two right ones belongs to a triangle only in thought, but rather in itself. Nor does it hinder this that I merely think such a possible thing; for [I] it is in itself always something, although no one thinks it, and the predicate belongs to it in itself, although no one combines it with it. So it is also with existence, if it could be regarded (g as) a predicate of things. For it would necessarily belong, among everything possible, to that [i.e. that thing will necessarily exist] in which all reality is, i.e. [to a] a most real being will necessarily exist, and its possibility would include actuality. Also, if without my thoughts or those of every thing existence [no predicate of the] did not belong to the most perfect being of all, the thought of this being would be false in general. For if it is correct, then it can represent no other predicates than those which also take place in the thing without this thought.\nS. III:\nthe question here is not whether more is posited (g in general through an actual thing) than through a non-actual one, but whether more predicates are posited in it; now [in a benevolent good one as such insofar as he is considered as existing] in the goodness of God, insofar as it is considered as existing, no more is posited than in the goodness that lies in his possibility; but the difference consists only in this, that in the first case the |\nthing itself with what is contained in it, but in the second the *respectus* of this manifold toward one another are posited.\nThe most essential difficulty is presumably this, that our linguistic usage, insofar as it seems to express our most precise idea [and], always designates existence like a predicate, which has the look of arising from the immediate constitution of our concept. I say by an existing thing: existence belongs to a thing [I] etc: I consider the existence of things separately and distinguish it from possibility. This constitution of our judgments deserves very much to be weighed. That is not called philosophizing, when one merely seeks to establish that something is a delusion, a deception of the understanding, but one must also learn to have insight into how such a deception would be possible. This semblance is itself a [tru] actual appearance in the nature of our mind, and I must either be able to explain it, or I have cause to fear that my judgment, which passes off [this] one or another opinions as a delusion, might itself be such a semblance.\nS. IV:\nI now ask: if existence is not a predicate of things, how then I can make use of the expression 'existence' at all; for this distinguishes something in the thing from the thing itself, which can be ascribed to it. e.g. [This] To a certain thing actuality belongs. Because one and the very same thing can be posited in different ways, this positing itself seems to be a mark of the difference, which one could ascribe to a thing as a predicate or separate from it. But it is certain that this difference depends merely on how this thing is posited with everything belonging to it, and not: what is posited in it. According to the difference of the way in which the thing is now posited, something different is also posited thereby: namely through its predicates in relation to the subject merely relations and |\nthe possibility, [through the] through the thing, insofar as it is posited *absolute*, the thing itself. [But because we that through which we] We therefore do not distinguish the possible from the actual through a predicate that belongs to the one more than to the other.\nThe concept of an existing thing can never be changed into a judgment of which the thing would be the subject and existence the predicate.\nRather, [the] something existing must be the subject and everything that belongs to it its predicates.",["\"existence\" renders both Daseyn and the Latinate Existentz; Wirklichkeit = actuality; allerrealestes / allervollkommenstes Wesen = most real / most perfect being of all.","\"überhaupt falsch\": either \"false in general\" or \"false at all\". \"eines jeden Dinges Gedancken\": literally \"the thoughts of every thing\".","Blendwerk = semblance; Täuschung = deception; Wahn = delusion; \"das ansehen hat\" = has the look (Ansehen, not Erscheinung)."]],"reflm:3707":["S. I:\nPreparation\nOn the certainty and uncertainty of cognition in general.\nUncertainty is either an uncertainty of the things or of insight. Although the objects of cognition are in themselves certainly that which they are, so [so] one can nevertheless ascribe uncertainty to these things, insofar as from that [Din] which one cognizes in them and which is thus given, nothing is settled about the remainder, which one seeks, one may [dasje] cognize these *data* as perfectly as one ever will. If in a mixture of three different metals (g whose specific gravity is given, in addition) the weight of the whole lump and its hydrostatic relation to water is given, then [is] one may cognize these *data* as one will, [so] it always remains uncertain here in what relation they are [among] mixed with one another, and indeed for this reason, because the cognized pieces |\nare in themselves of such a kind that from them this relation remains undetermined. This kind of uncertainty, which one could call the objective, must be encountered in the cognition of every cognition which is limited. He who does not cognize everything, from his pieces of cognition something must (g as to its constitution) remain unsettled, he may also make comparisons with this cognition as he ever will. [Es kommt nur d]\nNow if one cognizes [diese Er] that from certain *data* which one knows certain other [Best] pieces are undetermined, then [is] insofar no error can take place in our cognition, and the same is therefore not (g objectively) uncertain. If the apparent diameter of a star is known, but the distance unknown, then [is] it remains uncertain which is the true magnitude of the star, although [woferne man nur nicht weiter ge] from this uncertainty alone no error can arise. Likewise if in the observation |\nS. II: |\nof the angles it is once known that an error of two seconds could not be noticed, then all that which depends more or less on such a magnitude is undetermined from the observation, and, if one cognizes this as such, then to that extent no error can take place.\nSince uncertainty consists in the possibility of erring, i.e., of passing a judgment which is false, so all grounds of this possibility will be either negative or positive are, namely: they consist either in this, that grounds for a (g certain) true [cognition] judgment are lacking, or, that there are positive grounds to judge, which judgments are nevertheless not in conformity with the constitution of the things. The former ground in itself is not sufficient to understand the possibility of error thereby. For from this, because I do not know certain things, it does not yet follow that I could pass a false judgment. For presupposing that I cognize that I would have to know these pieces in order to judge, then I would, [since] when I find I do not know them, not want to judge and be safeguarded against all error. (g With) To the astronomer, as long as he knows of a comet the apparent diameter, to be sure, but not the [distance] parallax, it is insofar objectively uncertain how far and how large it is, but it is impossible that he can err in this, as long as |\nhe is conscious that he could not judge. Also this [Ungewishei] ignorance of certain given pieces is not even then a ground of the possibility of erring in itself, when the human being is not even conscious of this indeterminacy; for as long as there is no ground which occasions him to judge [so] in a thing about which he does not know enough, so long is he safeguarded against all judgment. Hence the common man is safeguarded against many errors; for, since he is unconcerned with regard to most things after which the learned strive so eagerly, even if he hears the questions that are raised, and most questions never come into his thoughts, there is nothing that should occasion him to judge. |\nS. III: |\nAccordingly one sees that uncertainty in the subjective sense (g also) presupposes a certain ground [is] for passing judgments. If to this is added uncertainty in the objective sense, of which, however, one is not conscious.\nHence with all the drive to pass a judgment, if consciousness of objective uncertainty is added to it, error is impossible, as one can see in geometry. The greater the desire to judge or the habit of judging is. We have adduced objective uncertainty only insofar as they are taken together with the [subjective] inner grounds for judging, in order to make the subjective possibility of erring comprehensible. But even if this desire is the same, then [is] the grounds of distinction of the true from the false are nevertheless either hidden and, relative to the agreements which a false cognition has with a true one, small, or not. (Since we infer from analogy, many agreements can be a ground of errors, e.g., reason of animals). In the case where one has many but not all grounds of distinction, error is possible only through an ignorance of the indeterminacy of the cognition from these grounds and the inclination to judge.\nCertainty must be just as possible in philosophical cognition as in mathematical and, as concerns the degree for conviction, be adequate to it. One cannot say that one certainty is greater |\nthan the other; nothing is ever more certain than that which is certain. But as concerns the kind, it concerns not certainty but clarity.\n(s All uncertainty is a possibility of erring. To err means to pass a false judgment. The possibility of erring is either under a potential or an actual condition. The first is understood thus, that one could err if one wanted to judge; the second: that there is also, from actual grounds, a conditioned possibility of judging, where it is nevertheless possible to err. The potential possibility of erring rests on the constitution of the things: namely where from that which one cognizes of them it is not determined what belongs to them or not; the actual condition of uncertainty is the ground for judging in which, after all, a potential uncertainty is lodged. This ground for judging [lies either in the] is lodged in the [Mang] apparent similarity of the procedure in a false cognition with that in a true one. As, first: Where I do not notice that something has been forgotten in the marks of the thing, there nothing has been forgotten. 2. Things that are similar in certain pieces are similar in others. 3. What belongs to many belongs to all.\nIn the manner of designating: where signs of [the same] similar things are regarded as the same.)\n(s The uncertainty of that of which various causes are possible. Existence of bodies.)\nOn S. I and II Kant had left free beneath the text a margin of about 4 cm across the whole width of the page. On S. I he subsequently filled this free space with additions as well, and indeed, as the content proves, still within phase γ. The additions show small differences in hand and ink. They are printed in the probable order of their origin.\n(s Means of mathematics: to take the *medium* from many.)\n(s Provisional judgments.)\n(s On philosophical opinions and hypotheses,\non noting the grounds why an opinion is uncertain.)\n(s Uncertainty is of a twofold kind: either where one does not find sufficient grounds of assent in the thing itself, or where, notwithstanding all of one's conviction, one is apprehensive, from the expectation of similar cases, of erring in calculating.)\n(s Uncertainty is either a possibility of erring under the condition that one wanted to judge of that for which there are no *data*, or a conditioned possibility of judging in that for which one has no *data*.)\n(s Immediately certain propositions: before one makes a concept distinct, whereby, however, one begins this.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition: many thinking ones cannot bring forth one thought.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition. can only be when without the predicate the subject cannot be thought, not when it seems one could not deny it.)",["\"thing\" renders both Sache and Ding; \"pieces\" renders Stücke; \"in the subjective/objective sense\" renders \"im subjektivischen/Objektiven Verstande\", Verstand here in its idiomatic sense.","\"of every cognition which is limited\" (eines jeden erkenntnisses) as it stands; a word for the cognizer may be expected. \"all grounds ... will be either negative or positive are\" keeps the broken construction (werden ... sind).","Deleted fragments that break off ([Din], [dasje], [Es kommt nur d], [diese Er], [Best], [woferne man nur nicht weiter ge], [Ungewishei], [Mang]) are left in the German."]],"reflm:3708":["Only rational concepts can be defined, empirical ones never. The concept of the latter serves only to designate the *subject* of synthetic judgments, but never for *analysis*.",["\"defined\" renders erklären (cf. Erklärung = definition)."]],"reflm:3709":["*Prolegomena Metaphysicorum.*\nIn our collective cognition some cognitions must, to others, |\nlie at the ground. At the ground of many concepts lie other concepts, and of many judgments other judgments. At the ground of the commonest concept of a clock lies the concept of time, of a motion, and of measurement. Whoever names the word \"friendship\" supports himself on the concepts of love, of honesty etc. etc. It is just so with judgments. Whoever says that envy is a vice grounds himself secretly on many hidden judgments: that love of one's neighbor is a duty, that that which is opposed to a duty is a vice, that envy is a disfavor and opposed to love etc. etc. One can call the fundamental concepts *notiones fundamentales*, the fundamental judgments *judicia fundamentalia*. Those fundamental concepts which[themselves] do not in turn presuppose others are called *notiones primitivae* (first fundamental concepts) and the judgments of such a kind *judicia primitiva* (first fundamental judgments). But something can be regarded as a first either absolutely or relatively to something else. Something is a *cognitio absolute primitiva* insofar as no other at all lies at its ground; but it is *respective primitiva* insofar as it [einer gewißen vernünftigen Absicht nicht ge] either is not within the powers of a subject to cognize its ground, or is not in conformity with a certain rational aim. At the ground of the concept of the good and evil, in agreement, lies much of various kinds, which the philosophers also clearly cognize, but the common man surely cannot penetrate so far into his concepts, and for him they are *notiones primitivae*. It is just so in common cognition with the concepts of space and of time. Some of these fundamental concepts can be *notiones primitivae* with regard to the collective human understanding, although *in sensu absoluto* they may be only *derivativae*, e.g. at the same time, being after one another etc. These one can call *notiones* [*absolute*] *primitivas in sensu subjectivo*. whether they are so *in sensu objectivo* as well, the human being cannot settle. Just so are *judicia in sensu subjectivo prima* with regard to some human beings. e.g. proverbs; or with regard to all, e.g. *principium contradictionis*.\nIf we now call all cognitions that lie at the ground of others *principia*, then there are *respectus* with regard to breaks off.",["\"lie at the ground\" renders zum Grunde liegen; \"fundamental concept/judgment\" renders Grundbegriff/Grundurteil; \"philosophers\" renders Weltweise.","\"in agreement\" renders \"überein\", which is doubtful as it stands.","\"breaks off\" renders the closing \"bricht ab\"; the deleted fragment that breaks off is left in the German."]],"reflm:3710":["All (g first) principles are either formal or material. The first contain the ground of how the concepts in the judgment are to be considered in relation. The second contain the *medium terminum* by means of which they are to be considered with one another in this relation.\n1. The way in which the judgments are to be compared is either through affirmation or negation. An affirmative judgment represents that a predicate is identical with the subject (is its mark). A negative one, that the predicate contradicts the subject (is an opposite of its mark). The universal formal principle of the former can thus only be this one, which indicates the essence of affirmation: *cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum*, and of the second the one which contains the essence of a negation: [*cuilibet subjecto*] *nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*. The first is the proposition of identity, the second of contradiction. From these principles alone nothing at all can be proved. For to prove means to judge mediately or to infer. Every syllogism, however, is a judgment by means of an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia, medius terminus,*). But the named propositions contain no *medium terminum* to others. All propositions that stand immediately under these two are indemonstrable; all that stand mediately under them are demonstrable.\n2. Universal judgments that contain the *medium terminum* to others are their material grounds; and, if they stand immediately under the two highest *principiis formalibus*, they are material first principles. e.g. A body is composite, stands immediately under the proposition of identity and contains the *medius terminus* to the proposition: a body is alterable; e.g. Everything composite is alterable, a body is composite, therefore it is etc. etc. By contrast the proposition: no space is without places, stands immediately under the proposition of contradiction etc:",["\"principle\" renders Grundsatz, \"proposition\" Satz, \"syllogism\" Vernunftschluß, \"relation\" Verhältnis; \"indemonstrable/demonstrable\" render unerweislich/erweislich.","cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum: to every subject belongs a predicate identical to it; nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum: to no subject belongs a predicate opposed to it."]],"reflm:3711":["*Nihil negativum* is entirely nothing. *non A* is also nothing and in itself not thinkable, but everything else can be thought. Now if the *Nihil negativum* were merely the nothing of a certain concept, then it would be A–A\n=0, thus the same as a *privation*; thus the *nihil negativum* is P–P = 0×P.",[]],"reflm:3712":["The mark of the absolutely necessary being cannot be the contingent in the existence of the other things, for then this necessity is only *hypothetica antecedentis*; thus not in this, that it is regarded as an ultimate ground of everything that exists, but that it is a ground of everything in general, both what exists and what is possible; for since possibility in general is certainly necessary, then that which contains the ground is also so.",["\"exists\" renders \"da ist\"; \"existence\" renders Daseyn; \"being\" renders Wesen."]],"reflm:3713":["On and \nThe determination of a being (s of something), insofar as what has been posited can be regarded, is quality.\nThe determination of a being (s of something), insofar as how many times something has been posited can be regarded, is quantity.\nA one in which there is quantity is a quantum.\nA positing of the same thing made several times is multitude.\n(s absolute unity is not a quantum.)\nIn any quantum whatever there is multitude, and that in which there is multitude is a quantum.\nTo cognize a multitude distinctly by successively adding one to one is to count.\nA multitude distinctly cognized by counting is number.\nA quantum in which how many times one has been posited is in itself undetermined (s which happens in that which is nothing but a mere possibility of coordination) is continuous.\nA quantum in which how many times one has been posited is in itself determined is discrete.",["\"Zuund\": the § numbers after \"Zu\" (on) and \"und\" (and) are missing from the source line.","\"quid sit positum\" (what has been posited) and \"quoties sit positum\" (how many times it has been posited) are the contrast behind quality and quantity.","\"numerare\" = to count; \"numerus\" = number; \"multitudo\" = multitude."]],"reflm:3714":["Every *caussatum* is *in se contingens*; the question is whether every *absolute contingens* is the *caussatum alterius*. The answer is this: *contingentia* can be discerned only through this, that the *existentia* of a thing, without being a *caussatum*, is undetermined.",["*caussatum* = something caused; *in se contingens* = contingent in itself; *caussatum alterius* = caused by another.","\"eingesehen\" (einsehen), rendered \"discerned\", i.e. have insight into."]],"reflm:3715":["*arbitrium* is either *liberum* or *brutum*. in the former one is conscious of the *contingentiae internae* and *externae* of the action.",["*arbitrium liberum* / *brutum* = free / brute choice; *contingentia interna* / *externa* = internal / external contingency."]],"reflm:3716":["S. I:\nA cognition of reason that has no other *principia* than empirical concepts can only be a critique; one can understand the universal (e.g. the moving) only from the particular, and the universal rules can only be abstracted from the particular ones of practice. it is the same with the doctrine of the probable.\nTruth has objective marks; but the certainty that in each case these objective ones are to be met with can have only subjective ones, i.e. the agreement of cognition with itself.\nA universal concept is empirical if it cannot otherwise [than] be understood than if one cognizes its *concretum*. e.g. One cannot get a concept of water through all definitions, [still] than if one has seen it. above all, all concepts of beauty, of simplicity, of the ridiculous are comprehensible only in their *concreto*. Hence the rules here too cannot occasion practice, but rather this makes the rules.\n––––––––––\nWith regard to the judging of the moral the question is not: whether such is agreeable, but: good. We do indeed cognize therein a con|nection\naccording to possibility with happiness, but it does not rest on it. The principles of moral judging are not to be grounds of the inclination to the good, [i.e.] for these belong to feeling. The most vicious human being, however small the moral inclination to the good may be proportionally, nevertheless holds it to be the best. The principles of this judgment are to be established.\n––––––––––\nAll sciences and arts relate either to the culture of healthy reason or not. in the latter case there also prevails a complete lack of taste. The age of healthy reason and of the arts and sciences is only the age of honor; this is found only in republics and monarchies, thus not in the feudal constitution, where an all too great inequality prevails.\n(g The *principium* of *identitaet* and of *contradiction* are for reason what space and time are for the senses. In general: the *principium* of agreement (be it of sameness or of difference). The *principium* of the highest reality is parallel to the sum total of all sensations.)\nPerfection is: what constitutes the worth of the person, happiness: what constitutes the worth of the state.\n(The proposition of sufficient ground is grounded on this, that one can cancel everything in and for itself, thus, in order to posit something, something else must be posited, with which this cancelling conflicts. This connection, however, is either according to laws of reason or of the senses.)\nThis proposition, however, means only that nothing can be cognized according to reason without a ground.\n––––––––––\n(g If we want to posit something through reason, we need a ground. I.e. positing *a priori* is through a ground, unless it were intuition.)\nIn the proposition: everything that is, has a ground, there lie the two: 1. something else, 2. through which the sequel is determined, the former *dependens*, the latter *contingens*.\nS. II:\nAll ideas of metaphysics are analytic except of space, time and force.\nAnalysis rests on the propositions of *identitaet* and of contradiction. All these and other propositions, however, are grounded on this one, that the understanding posits nothing *absolute*, but only insofar as it is necessitated to it by a *condition* either analytically or synthetically.\nThe idea of the possible. The idea of all or of a whole.\nMetaphysics is not a philosophy about the *obiecten*, for these can only be given through the senses, but about the *subiect*, namely its laws of reason.\nWe have laws for the use of our reason *a posteriori*; these cannot be *applicirt* to concepts, but only to objects of experience.\nMetaphysics thus treats only the *subiect* dogmatically, but the *obiect*, with regard to synthetic judgments, problematically.\nAll judgments are either logical, which *enunciiren* *respective* being, or real, which *enunciiren* that of the *absolute*. All being in [itself] itself (*position*) is an existence either *respective* (g *hypothetice*) or *categorice*.\nUniversality is understood either synthetically and then holds only of the cases of experience of the synthesis, or analytically and holds according to reason of concepts.\nTo determining the boundaries of reason there belongs something positive: first to show the domain of the cognitions of reason, and something negative: namely the limitations, finally also the quality of the boundaries, as it were the figure.\nCognitions are of a twofold kind: either those which go to objects that are given, or: to the concepts of the form, how reason considers every object. The latter are merely subjective and also alone can have a universality of reason.\nIn synthetic judgments I represent to myself an *identitaet* for the sake of the predicate, but not the predicate for the sake of the *identitaet*.\nThe fundamental concepts of analysis are: possibility, impossibility, necessity, contingency, unity etc etc.; of synthesis: space, time and force.\nNot all *conceptus superiores* are *abstracti*, but only those which arise analytically *a posteriori*; those *a priori* are not *abstracti*. These *abstracti* are empirical or rational.",["\"gesunde Vernunft\" = healthy reason; \"Beurtheilung\" = judging; \"Lehnsverfassung\" = feudal constitution.","\"an sich [sich] selbst\": the bracketed \"sich\" stands between \"an sich\" and \"selbst\"; rendered \"in [itself] itself\".","\"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" are both \"object\"; the Latinate forms are kept as *obiect(en)*."]],"reflm:3717":["27:\nS. I:\nThe *principium* of the form of all experiences are space and time.\nThe *principium* of the form of all judgments of pure reason: *identitaet* and contradiction.\nThe *principium* of the form of all judgments of reason *a posteriori*: ground and force.\nComparison, derivation, taking-together.\nPossible is *contradistinguirt*: 1. to the [confl] whose concept contains a conflict. 2. To the determined or true. 3. To the actual.\nIn all empirical judgments the basic formula is: [everything] In every thing that which contains the ground of one sensation is also the same *subiect* that contains the ground of the other. The synthesis is one of taking-together or of derivation.\nAll necessity and contingency that we can represent to ourselves is conditioned. The unconditioned is thought problematically. Absolutely contingent (e.g. free action) and absolutely necessary can both not be thought.\nAll relation is either of combination or of comparison or of taking-together.\nAll combination (in the real sense): space, time and force.\nAll definitions are grounded on the concept of essence; hence we can define nothing but pure concepts of reason and arbitrary concepts.\nAll abstract cognitions have first grounds *a posteriori*.\nThe universal judgment that has not arisen through abstraction *a posteriori* and thus empirically is not abstract, but a pure judgment of reason.\nIn metaphysics the grounds of cognition must be taken *a priori*, although the *obiecte* *a posteriori*.\nIf the grounds [from that] about that which must be are taken from that |\nwhich happens, then they are *a posteriori*. The synthesis is twofold: of the coordinated and of the subordinated.\nThe synthesis of reason or experience.\nS. II:\nBesides the proposition of sufficient ground this one also holds: all analysis [is posits a synt] includes conversely the possibility of a synthesis. Accordingly in every series of subordinated things there must be a first, because otherwise no complete synthesis and thus also no analysis would take place.\nThe synthetic principle of the understanding is bricht ab.\nMathematical analysis is always a philosophical synthesis, only as I think the whole before the parts; but if I think the parts before the whole, then it is a mathematical synthesis[in the]. all synthesis rests on coordination and happens through understanding. The *analysis philosophica*, however, on subordination and happens through reason. The ground is no part of the sequel, nor conversely.\nIn all images of things I coordinate nothing but immediate marks and partial representations.\nab represents [a co] the action of coordination, ac however of subordination.\nThe soul has three dimensions. sense and its clarity, the understanding and its form as well as matter (i.e. extensiveness) of coordination, and reason, that\nis height of subordination.\nWhether it is not also thus with the relations to feeling?\nBecause all negations serve only to prevent errors and to demonstrate the not-knowing: metaphysics is a very useful science, not insofar as it extends knowledge, but prevents errors. One learns that which Socrates knew.\nTaste goes to appearances and not to sensation, consequently to the constitution of the object insofar as it is an *obiect* of the senses, and is thus, as a cognition, subject to universally valid rules.\nThe sensible is a perfection, and confusion is not essential to it, but rather distinctness of coordination can take place.\n–––––––––––\nMathematics shows the greatest dignity of human reason, metaphysics however the limitations and its peculiar vocation.\nIt belongs throughout to everything beautiful that I need for it only understanding and as little reason as possible; for it is to please in appearance. Hence the coordination.\n–––––––––––\nRemark: that the rules of the beautiful are given through experience, for the reason that one cannot think a beautiful shape of a rational being without a human figure.",["\"bricht ab\" (breaks off) is the AA's editorial note, kept in German; \"[Wiederst]\" is a struck fragment of \"Wiederstreit\" (conflict), rendered \"[confl]\".","\"nur da ich\": \"da\" may be temporal (when) or causal (since); rendered \"as\" to keep both.","\"daß\" before \"ist Hohe\" reads as \"das\" (that); doubtful reading, rendered \"that\"."]],"reflm:3718":["After »dunckele« (l. 32) Kant inserts:\nclear ones, to be sure, but still in part confused.",["\"dunckele\" = obscure; \"klare\" = clear; \"verworrene\" = confused."]],"reflm:3719":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nBeside »ebenso nothwendig — doch ich muß« (ll. 15-18):\nIf the real sequel were contained in the real ground and thereby posited according to the rule of *identitaet*, then it would at all times be simultaneous with it. All alterations are thus possible only through the real relation of the grounds to their sequels, and the logical grounds are thus not through the limitations of my cognition, but in themselves, distinguished from the real ground.",["\"realfolge\" / \"realgrund\" = real sequel / real ground (Folge = sequel); \"Veränderungen\" = alterations."]],"reflm:3720":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nAfter »widersprechen« (l. 38) Kant inserts:\nbut indeed conflict: namely that the one cancels the sequel of the other. Contradiction is the taking-together of two *contradictorie*, i.e. *logice* opposed predicates in one *subiect*, and its result is *nihil negativum*: a and non a at once. Real conflict is the combination of two real grounds in one subject, of which one cancels the sequel of the other, and its result is\n=0, which is no *nihil negativum*.",["\"widersprechen\" = contradict; \"widerstreiten\" / \"Widerstreit\" = conflict.","\"aufheben\" = cancel; \"zusammennehmung\" = taking-together; \"Subiect\" and \"subiect\" are both \"subject\"."]],"reflm:3721":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nBeside ll. 25-29 (»widerstreiten — princip.«) Kant has made a brace ({) and added to the left of »zwar — entweder« (ll. 26-30):\nThat both positings can be removed does not yet constitute any [contradiction] conflict, and a contradiction is not here at all, but rather the conflict is possible only through this, that besides the sequel of a=x and the sequel of b=y, when both are taken together, a third, namely 0, is still possible.",["\"positionen removirt\" = positings (*positiones*) removed; \"[Wiederspruch aus]\" (contradiction) is struck in favor of \"Wiederstreit\" (conflict)."]],"reflm:3722":["Not possible =0P. Impossible –P. With the former I need only not think something, as *blitri*; with the latter I must cancel the possibility. For that an actual thought is required. but were it only the cancelling of a possibility, then it would again be 0.",["\"darf ich nur nicht etwas denken als blitri\": \"als\" may mean \"as\" (thinking something as *blitri*) or \"other than\"; kept ambiguous.","\"wirklicher Gedanke\" = actual thought; \"aufheben\" = cancel."]],"reflm:3723":["One must distinguish between the possible, the not possible and the impossible. If A is thought, it is possible; if nothing besides non A (which happens in the signification of an empty term), it is not possible; if the possibility is cancelled, it is impossible. Impossibility is negative possibility.",["\"terminus inanis\" = empty term; \"tollitur\" = is cancelled."]],"reflm:3724":["On and \nPossibility is respective positing conformable to the principle of contradiction. Truth (affirmative) is respective positing according to the principle of identity. Existence is absolute positing. From respective positing |\nto absolute the consequence is not valid. But from respective positing taken generally to absolute the consequence is valid, since, unless something were absolutely posited, no respect at all would be possible.",["\"Zuund\": the § numbers after \"Zu\" (on) and \"und\" (and) are missing from the source line.","\"positio respectiva\" / \"absoluta\" = respective / absolute positing; \"respectus\" = respect (relation); \"consequentia\" = consequence (inference)."]],"reflm:3725":["Absolute necessity is either logical: on account of the principle of contradiction, or real: not on account of the principle of contradiction.\nThe former is the necessity of judgments. or the necessity of the relation of predicate and subject.\nThe latter is the necessity of beings. 1. God is omnipotent. 2. God is. the latter through the contradiction of the opposite (g in itself) cannot be cognized. The opposite of existing is not-being. But not-being alone does not contradict itself. Existence is not a predicate, therefore its opposite is not a predicate opposed to anything.",["\"1. Gott ist allmächtig. 2. Gott ist.\" are in German in the Latin note.","\"Verum\" may be the conjunction \"but\" or the adjective \"true\" (\"true not-being\"); read as \"but\"."]],"reflm:3726":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum«:\nThere is no simple accident, since there is no simple action, but it is always a quantum.",["\"Simplex et compositum\" = the simple and the composite."]],"reflm:3727":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum« and \nQuantity is either of a composite or intensively such, i.e. degree. A greater degree is not the whole of the lesser ones, but the ground, i.e. a double degree, which can be the cause of two equal degrees; but in order that in the same |\nsubject there reside grounds of an effect which equals the effects of two substances taken together, there is required 1. a ground for any effect whatever, 2. a principle of uniting these grounds. E.g. an understanding understanding twice as much.\nWhatever is composite, since before composition whatever is given is simple, necessarily consists of simples, but not on that account is every *quantum* composite and a whole.",["The opening line is the AA's editorial indication of where the note stands.","ratio = ground; intellectus = understanding; principium = principle.","qvantitas … compositi vel intensive talis: 'of a composite' (genitive) as printed."]],"reflm:3728":["We can just as little think (g total) absolute contingency as absolute necessity through reason, but experience shows it in the case of that which happens.",["geschieht = happens; 'es' (it) is left without a stated referent, as in the German."]],"reflm:3729":["There is among the substances of the universe a commerce through established harmony; this is either an internally established harmony (physical influence) or an externally established one; the latter is either an externally pre-established harmony or an externally occasionally established one.",["commercium = commerce; stabilita = established; praestabilita = pre-established.","physical influence = influxus physicus."]],"reflm:3730":["If each of the existing substances were necessary, there would be no connection among them, [if] i.e. if there were no God at all, the variety of all substances would not constitute a world. If [plur] of some substances a common God, of others another, were the founder, there would be several [would be] possible worlds, not a single one. If there is one author of all, all that he has caused necessarily constitutes a single whole.\nThe system through which, for the reason that several substances exist, their relation and commerce seems necessarily to follow, is physical influence.\nThe system through which, for the reason that all depend on one, commerce and universal harmony is necessary, is pre-established harmony. [In this every influence]\nThe system through which, for the reason that all depend on one, [not yet necessar] commerce is only possible and is in itself contingent, so that each mutual dependence is established arbitrarily, is occasionalism.\nHere, however, it is to be noted that the pre-established harmony of Leibniz is a system that imagines for itself the contingency of the commerce of substances, even with one author posited, and a correspondence of one with another instituted arbitrarily. In true harmony, however, all harmonic acts happen according to a rule; in pre-established harmony and occasionalism [su] however [are] the divine act inducing the harmony is nothing but singular, and the difference of these consists only in time.",["colligatio = connection; commercium = commerce; influxus physicus = physical influence.","[plur], [nondum necessari] and [su] are broken-off deletions, rendered as fragments.","fingens = imagines (also 'feigns'); arbitrario = arbitrarily."]],"reflm:3731":["The question: why something is, presupposes the thought that it is possible that something also is not. This thought, however, [is grounded] |\ngoes [either] either to the existence of the substances or of their relations. As concerns the latter, that kind of composition which [itself] could not be understood from the necessary existence of the substances is a proof of the [existence] contingent existence [of them] of this composition. But as concerns the existence of the substance itself, the question why it is is grounded on the inner possibility of thinking its not-being.\nThere are only three kinds of concepts of things: first an absolute one, from the inner determinations, second a respective one, from the relation to possibility, third a relative one, from that to the actuality of other things. From the first concept no absolute necessity can be proved, for each thing with all its inner predicates can be cancelled without contradiction. From the third not either, because this is only a *necessitas hypothetica conseqventiae*. thus there remains the second, that possibility itself stands in such *relation* to actuality that it includes it.\nThe absolute necessity of a thing must be able to be cognized without any determinate condition. [This condition is]\nHow do we cognize absolute possibility? *Data* must always be given to us for it.\nIf I say: a certain *praedicat* is impossible for a thing, then I say: it [contra] does not belong to it, i.e. it contradicts it, although not what I think in it, but what belongs to it. (The question is whether I can say: it contradicts the rest of a thing, apart from its opposite.) What is thus impossible contradicts something or other; what is absolutely impossible contradicts everything, namely the contradiction rests on no particularly determined *condition*.",["Verhältnis = relation; relation in the Latin-type word is kept as *relation*; Wirklichkeit = actuality; aufheben = cancel.","[contra] renders the broken-off deletion [wiede], the beginning of wiederspricht.","Daseyn/Dasein = existence; Art = kind."]],"reflm:3732":["A concept concerning whose possibility nothing is established, remaining when the condition is removed under which alone it is permitted to judge its possibility, is pro|blematic.\nThus the absolute necessity of a being, the simple element of a body, an action determined by no ground.",["problematicus = problematic; ratio = ground; ens = being."]],"reflm:3733":["(g The thing,) What contains the ground of the inner possibility of things is absolutely necessary. Conversely (g the thing), what is absolutely necessary contains the ground of the (g inner) possibility of things; (g for) if even without it things were inwardly necessary, then there would be no impossibility at all in its not being. (g Or because [a thing] the opposite of a thing cannot contradict itself, it must conflict with some other.)\nWhat contains the ground of one inner possibility contains the ground of all. For let us posit that something possible still remained over, then this would not agree with something possible or other, that is: it would contradict something possible; but what contradicts something possible is impossible. (g The necessary being thus contains the ground of all possibility.)\nIn all possibility the formal and the material are distinguished. The ground of the first is the *principium contradictionis*; of the latter some existence or other which contains the ground of reality.\nAccordingly the most real being contains the ground of all possibility (s not a particular ground of the inner possibility of things and another one not.) and is absolutely necessary.\nThe necessary being is all-sufficient (s the most perfect *transscendentaliter*), i.e. the most real as a ground.\nThe necessary being is a single one. For if we posit several, then each of them will be able to be cancelled, yet in such a way that all the others remain; but because, if something necessary |\nis cancelled, the impossible arises, it would be possible that something possible were impossible. Moreover each of them would be a sequel of the others: *regressus in infinitum curvilineus*.\nThe necessary being is a simple one. For if a whole is necessary, then the parts must be so too; but then there would be many necessary beings.\nThe world is thus not a necessary being.\nThe necessary being is the highest good, for among all possibles *bonitaet* is a reality without which all existence is, in respect of worth, equal to nothing. Thus it contains the ground of all good. But the good consists in the relation of things (g to beings) which have cognitions and feeling, [the] and a being which for that reason contains the ground of things.\nThe necessary being has the most perfect understanding and will.\nThe necessary being is thus a person which contains the ground of all existence through understanding and will. I.e. it is a God.",["Wesen = being; allerrealeste = most real; allgnugsam = all-sufficient; aufheben = cancel.","Folge = sequel; bonitaet (Latinate, 'goodness') kept in italics.","wiederstreiten = conflict with, beside wiedersprechen = contradict."]],"reflm:3734":["Uniqueness follows either from the highest reality or from absolute necessity.\nEither from the notion of the most real necessary existence is concluded or from the absolute necessity of existing the greatest reality.\n[Greatest] Each principle is either drawn from experience or from mere notions. the former obtains if 1. the great perfection and then the absolute necessity of the author of the world is concluded, 2. if there is concluded breaks off?\n. . . . . .\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . infers\nEither from contingent existence to the necessary or from perfect existence to the most perfect.",["'breaks off?' renders the AA's bricht ab?; the dotted lines are the AA's, marking illegible or lost text.","Vnicitas = uniqueness; notio = notion; concluditur = is concluded; schließt = infers."]],"reflm:3735":["logical necessity,\nreal necessity.\n(s Impossibility.\nin the logical sense,\nin the real sense.)",["im logischen Verstande, im realverstande: Verstand here in the sense of 'sense, meaning', not understanding."]],"reflm:3736":["On the running title of M 333: »Exsistentia Dei«:\nFirst of all the question offers itself to investigation: what might be the foundation of absolutely necessary existence, or in what way the opposite of the existence of something can be absolutely necessary.\n1. the existence of contingents as things caused does not constitute the principle of absolute Necessity; rather, this is the ultimate ground of the possibility of the existence of several contingents.\n2. No other plain existence contains the ground of absolute necessity, for of contingents it has already been said, of the condition of necessary existence there is already the question.\n3. Therefore the proper mark of necessary existence will have to be found in its respect to possibility, which likewise is necessary.\n4. Impossibility consists in contradiction; [will be] it is namely properly so if you [den] posit what was to be denied, or vice versa. e.g. Impossible is a triangle [parallelo] quadrilateral. Which also sounds thus: a triangle is not a quadrilateral, which expresses a contradiction. For let |\nA be not B; then A, which is B, would be B not B, which is impossible and contradictory.\n5. The opposite of no existence contradicts itself. Contradiction is between subject and predicate, existence is not a predicate, therefore non-existence contradicts no subject in itself.",["The opening line is the AA's editorial indication of where the note stands.","'the opposite … can be absolutely necessary' and 'plain' (plana) are translated as printed; both readings are doubtful.","nota = mark; respectus = respect; ratio = ground; [nege] rendered [den], [parallelo] left as the broken-off word."]],"reflm:3737":["It is the question whether the power of God is greater than his decree and, since the former extends to all [*in nexu* possible] things, whether the latter does not also extend to them; in this case everything possible is actual. But if it seems that much that is possible is not actual, then we represent that to ourselves as possible whose *conditionen* are nevertheless impossible.",["Ratschlus = decree (also 'counsel'); gehet auf = extends to; wirklich = actual."]],"reflm:3738":["All analytic judgments teach what is thought in the concepts, but confusedly; the synthetic, what is to be thought as combined with the concept. In all judgments the concept of the subject is something (g a) that I think in the object x, and the predicate is regarded as a mark of a in the [synthetic] analytic judgments or of x in the synthetic.\n|–a—|—x——|\nall analytic judgments are rational and conversely. all synthetic judgments are empirical and conversely. first rational principles [either] of matter [or prior of form] are [judg] elementary principles; synthetic principles, if they were at the same time rational, would be called axioms; but, since such are not given, analogues of the rational ones in mathematics are so called. In philosophy there are no synthetic principles except *a posteriori*, i.e. empirically, and analytic principles *a priori*, i.e. elementary propositions, both material.\nI can compare notions by thinking their relation either according to the rule of the understanding: empirically (g and synthetically), or of reason: rationally and analytically, or according to the rules of the analogue of reason, i.e. |\nof imagination and wit. To this crusius owes many of his principles.\nLocke seems to have scented the distinction of synthetic and analytic judgments in his inquiry into man.",["object = Objekt; Merkmal = mark; verbunden = combined.","In the Latin: intellectus = understanding, ratio = reason, ingenium = wit, analogon rationis = analogue of reason.","accepta fert = owes, credits to."]],"reflm:3739":["If I deny something of a subject, then this happens in accordance with the principle of contradiction. This negation together with the affirmation is impossible.",["satz des Wiederspruchs = principle (Satz) of contradiction; Bejahung = affirmation."]],"reflm:3740":["Something opposite of nothing\n{\nnegative privative or negative.",["The brace is the AA's layout; how negativo, privativo and negativo attach to nihili is left as open as the Latin."]],"reflm:3741":["The material principles of judgments are notions\nthe formal\nthe principle of identity\nor of contradiction\nthe material principles of ratiocinations are notions\nthe formal\nthe *dictum de*\n*omni et nullo*\n(s The logical principle of judgments. The real principle of concepts.)",["ratiocinia = ratiocinations (syllogisms); notiones = notions; Begriffe = concepts."]],"reflm:3742":["(g There are material, there are also formal basic concepts.)\nAnalytic, synthetic principles.",["Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; the second line is Latin: Principia analytica, synthetica."]],"reflm:3743":["All mathematical concepts are synthetic.",[]],"reflm:3744":["There are synthetic propositions from experience, thus *principia prima synthetica*; of this kind are also (g the definitions of mathematics) the *axiomata* of mathematics concerning space.\n*Principia rationalia* cannot be synthetic at all.\nAll empirical propositions are synthetic and conversely.\nAll rational propositions are analytic.",["Sätze = propositions.","können gar nicht synthetisch sind: sind for seyn as written; rendered by the sense of the infinitive."]],"reflm:3745":["From the principle of identity and of contradiction there follow not only propositions, but necessary ones.",["Satz stands for both 'principle' (dem Satze der identitaet) and 'propositions' (Sätze)."]],"reflm:3746":["The *principia formalia* are only the first grounds of analytic or rational judgments.",[]],"reflm:3747":["All *principia* of human cognition are *vel formalia vel materialia*. The former contain merely the relation of concepts in judgments: either the logical or metaphysical. The latter of things, and are synthetic. (s Either of the analytic, and are called logical and hold for every understanding, are objective (only not conversely); or of the synthetic, and are called real, and, because they are stated universally without senses, they are either principles of the form of the understanding or of sensibility [and hold in both ca]; in the first case they are merely subjective laws. In the second they are objective only under the hypothesis of sensibility; but if the hypothesis already lies in the subject, they are objective.)",["Grundsätze = principles; Verhältnis = relation; ausgesagt = stated.","[und gelten in beyden fä] breaks off in fä(llen), rendered [and hold in both ca].","'Jene … Diese' rendered 'The former … The latter'; what 'of the analytic' and 'of the synthetic' qualify is left as open as the German."]],"reflm:3748":["Metaphysical basic concepts. Elementary concepts. Systematic basic concepts.",["Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; Elementarbegriffe = elementary concepts."]],"reflm:3749":["One can connect concepts with one another in order thus to make out of them a greater concept (synthetic); or one can think concepts as connected with one another in order to cognize that which is in them.\nThe concepts of causes are synthetic and thus empirical.",["verknüpfen = connect; erkennen = cognize."]],"reflm:3750":["All *principia primitiva* are either elementary propositions and ana|lytic\nor *axiomata* and are synthetic. Difference of an analytic and synthetic proposition in general. The rational are analytic, the empirical synthetic, likewise mathematical ones.",["Satz = proposition; elementarsätze = elementary propositions."]],"reflm:3751":["Not positing and cancelling is different.\nTo posit both of contradictorily opposed predicates at once is impossible through the principle of contradiction.\nTo cancel both of contradictorily opposed predicates is impossible through the principle of the excluded middle.\nTo think neither of contradictorily opposed predicates of something is possible: rule of abstract notions, which are indeterminate; that the latter is possible is evident from this, that, if they are not material, there is no contradiction.",["The first line can be read as 'not-positing and cancelling are different' or as 'not positing is different from cancelling'; the ambiguity is kept.","setzen/ponere = posit; aufheben/tollere = cancel; utrumque = both (each)."]],"reflm:3752":["[*respectus*]\nWhoever thinks a ground also thinks the sequel, and conversely: it is a concept of *respectus*. Since no simpler *respectus* can be thought, the concept of it is also inexplicable. One can nevertheless prevent through a judgment that ground and sequels are not regarded as entirely one and the same: so that it is said: *posita ratione ponitur aliud*; *sive: ratio et rationatum sunt diversa*.",["Folge = sequel; unerklärlich = inexplicable (Erklärung = explanation; definition).","verhindern, daß … nicht: the pleonastic nicht is kept.","posita ratione ponitur aliud = when the ground is posited, something else is posited; sive: ratio et rationatum sunt diversa = or: ground and grounded are different."]],"reflm:3753":["A ground is either of positing or of canceling.\n(g The relation is either logical: of concepts (of identity),\nor real: of things (*realiter diversi*).)\nThe relation of a positing ground is connection, of a canceling one is opposition.\nThe relation of a logical ground, either of positing or of canceling, is analytic — rational;\nthe relation of a real ground, either of positing or of canceling, is synthetic — empirical.",["“ground” is *ratio*, “relation” *respectus*, “connection” *nexus*, “canceling” *tollendi*.","*realiter diversi*: “really diverse”; the parenthetical lines mix German (der Begriffe, der Sachen) into the Latin."]],"reflm:3754":["The relation is either of connection or of opposition,\neach either logical or real.\nThe former on account of identity [post] or contradiction, the latter not through them.\n(s The logical ground and consequence are always simultaneous, the real ones after one another.)\n(In logical opposition, when one is posited, the other is canceled.)\n(In real opposition, when one is posited, the consequence of the other is canceled.)\n(s In the former the connection is cognizable analytically, in the latter synthetically. The former rationally, the latter empirically.)\nin the former one or the other is a negation;\nin the latter both are positive.\nin the former (s both being posited) the consequence is a negative nothing (s or impossible);\nin the latter (s both being posited) the consequence is a privative nothing =0.",["“relation” is *respectus*, “connection” *nexus*, “ground” *ratio*, “consequence” *rationatum*.","The Latin sets *nexus* (nominative) beside *oppositionis* (genitive): “either connection or of opposition”; the construction is left as uneven as it stands.","*nihil negativum*, *nihil privativum*: “negative nothing”, “privative nothing”."]],"reflm:3755":["The relation of the logical ground to the consequence is a judgment.\nThe relation of the real ground is a concept.\nVarious [Grundb] primitive concepts of real grounds.\nThe possibility of a real connection in primitive grounds cannot be rationally seen into.",["“consequence” is Folge, “relation” Verhältnis, “real connection” realverknüpfung.","“seen into”: eingesehen (einsehen), to have insight into.","[Grundb] is a deleted beginning, presumably of “Grundbegriffe”."]],"reflm:3756":["The logical of possibility is contradistinguished from truth, the real of possibility from actuality.\nThe relation of identity and contradiction thought negatively, i.e., that one concept is not identical with the other, and also does not conflict with it, is the logical or formal of mere possibility, the real of possibility is at the same time the material of it, and our concept of it extends as far as the simple of our sensations, likewise as the primitive relations (*respectus reales*) which we come to know through experience.\nThe logical of possibility is a relation of comparison, the logical of truth a relation of connection.\nThe cognition of the logical relation is rational, the [zweyte] real one empirical.\nGround and consequence are at all times different, but either (g only) logically, in that the consequence is only a partial concept of the ground (consequently where it is entirely the same as it, it is also no consequence), or really, in that it is thought not as a partial concept belonging to the ground, but as entirely different from it.\nAll connection of the logical ground with the consequence is an affirmative judgment, in which the predicate is the consequence and the subject the ground. In real connection this is never so; although the consequence can be a predicate of the cause, yet there is none of the ground in it. The logical connection and conflict can be cognized analytically and thus rationally, the real not otherwise than empirically. Since everything empirical teaches only what is customarily wont to be with or after one another (except in the properties of space), the question arises whether we |\nin this counter-relation do not distinguish the ground from the consequence through time.",["“relation” stands for both Verhältnis and Beziehung (“logischen Beziehung”); “consequence” is Folge, “conflict” wiederstreiten / Wiederstreit.","“the logical of”, “the real of”: das Logische der, das reale der, substantival adjectives left without a noun.","“yet there is none of the ground in it” (so ist doch keine des Grundes in derselben): it is unclear what “keine” (none) refers to, the consequence or the predicate."]],"reflm:3757":["That whose position is necessarily (according to universal laws) [etwa] combined with the position of something else is called its ground.",["“position” is Kant's loanword position, i.e. positing; “combined” is verbunden."]],"reflm:3758":["The possible, insofar as it is a consequence, is connected *a priori*; insofar as it is a ground, it is connected *a posteriori*. What is neither ground nor consequence is entirely unconnected. The series of all possibles subordinated to one another has a terminus *a priori* and *a posteriori*, and cannot [esse] again be connected with others.",["“consequence” is *rationatum*, “ground” *ratio*.","The text reads “connexum osse”; “osse” is presumably a slip for “esse”, rendered “be”."]],"reflm:3759":["Several connected things make a series. A series of [connexorum] coordinated things can be contained under the idea of all, a series of subordinated ones not so. The little word *simul* comprises everything.\nThe first grounds in the series of the coordinated parts of a body are simple things.",["*Vocula simul* is taken as the word “simul” (at once, together) being mentioned; it could also be read “the little word at once comprises everything”.","“simple things”: *simplicia* (neuter plural)."]],"reflm:3760":["reality and negation are distinctions between what is absolutely or meta|physically\nposited or negative and that which is called such only logically, in which there is only a relation either of position or of negation.",["“relation” is *respectus*; “position” *positionis*."]],"reflm:3761":["A universal and merely possible concept is not *omnimode* determined. But a singular concept is so and can yet be merely possible.\nExistence can be no predicate, for otherwise a thing would be cognized as existing only through a judgment and by means of the understanding. But as it is, we cognize the existence of things through sensation.",["*omnimode determinirt*: “determined in every respect”, i.e. thoroughly determined.","“existence” stands for both existentz and Daseyn.","“singular” is einzelner."]],"reflm:3762":["The respective positing of the predicates of a being toward one another in conformity with the principle of contradiction contains less in itself than the absolute positing of the same at once; therefore from the former to the latter the inference does not hold.",["“the inference does not hold”: *non valet consequentia*.","*simul* (“at once”) could attach to “positing” or to “absolute”."]],"reflm:3763":["Fictions can be possible [in sensu absolu]; but if one occupies oneself merely with their possibility, then an ideal way of thinking comes of it.",["“Fictions” is Erdichtungen; “way of thinking” Denkungsart.","[in sensu absolu] is a deleted, unfinished Latin phrase (“in the absolute sense”), left as it stands."]],"reflm:3764":["(s That in which there is quantity is a *quantum*.)\nQuantity is the positing of the same, made some number of times. It is pure: in which what has been posited is not asked at all, but it is something in general, as in Arithmetic. Quantity *in concreto* is: in which what has been posited is determined, i.e., in which a certain quality is given as one.",["“made some number of times”: *aliqvoties facta*.","The text spells *Arithemetica*.","“as one”: *pro uno*, also “for a unit”."]],"reflm:3765":["(s Unity is conditioned or unconditioned.)\nBy the 4 sections one has perhaps only wanted to say that the concepts of the one, the true, the perfect can be applied to all things in general.\nEach thing is not many: signifies the transcendental unity.\nEach thing contains much that is yet one and the same with one*: the metaphysical (g can be greater or lesser unity).\nEach thing is true, i.e.: it cannot be denied under all possible concepts and agrees with other things: transcendental.\nEach thing contains truths: metaphysical, the *principium* of the elementary propositions. (g some things do contain possibility, but not enough metaphysical truth, i.e., conditioned possibility.)\nEach thing is perfect, what it is: transcendental.\nEach has reality: metaphysical, it is comparatively perfect.\n[Das] The absolute unity, truth and perfection consists in the possibility which comprises all-sufficiency in itself, the possibility in every *respectu* and all reality.\n*(s in the circle there is more unity than in the polygon. The unity of the conic sections.)",["“all-sufficiency” is Allgnugsamkeit; “elementary propositions” elementarsätze.","“polygon”: the text reads “Vielek”, for Vieleck.","“one and the same with one” (einerley mit einem): “einem” may be “one thing” or the numeral."]],"reflm:3766":["Unity is either absolute (g categorical) (s numerical), which is opposed to multitude, or hypothetical, insofar as it belongs to several things joined together\n|\nunity either by coordinating a whole or by subordinating: unity of the ground and also unity of the consequence (in machines, in the aim).",["“ground” is *ratio*, “consequence” *rationatum*, “aim” *scopo*.","*coordinando totum vel subordinando*: “totum” may be the object of both gerunds or of the first only."]],"reflm:3767":["All necessity is either logical or real. The former is cognized rationally, the latter empirically. The opposite of the latter is called impossible because it never happens; the opposite of the former never happens because it is impossible.",[]],"reflm:3768":["That whose opposite is possible cannot for that reason be called contingent, if by the opposite is understood something else that is non A. For [loco] besides the substance whose determination is A, there can be another which is determined by non A; e.g., thought is not for that reason contingent to a human being because another can also be without thought; [aut] at least this contingency is only logical, not real, because in this human being it is perhaps necessary. Nor is a substance contingent whose determinations can succeed one another.",["“thought” is *cogitatio*; “human being” *homo*."]],"reflm:3769":["Opposite the number of M § 109:\nPossibility without existence of the same thing is not one and the same with the possibility of its non-being. I can represent to myself comets that are not; but to represent to oneself that the sun is not could be hypothetically impossible.",["The first line is the AA's note on where the Reflexion stands in Kant's copy.","“existence” is Daseyn, “non-being” Nichtseyn."]],"reflm:3770":["The (g absolute) possibility can be cognized only from the *nexu* with everything possible. e.g. whether in a triangle the square of one side can be equal to that of the two others cannot be cognized from a triangle |\nin general, but from its concept connected with the right angle.",["“to that of the two others” (dem der beyden andern): singular as in the German.","“connected” is verknüpftem."]],"reflm:3771":["A thing exists necessarily the opposite of whose existence is impossible. Yet for all that the opposite of its determinations is possible. After all, one says of alterable things that the same things successively have all sorts of determinations. If the opposite of the determinations were at the same time the opposite of the thing, then no thing would be alterable. Indeed, even (g in) an altered thing these alterations themselves can be necessary, for the logical opposite of a determination consists in this, that instead of A non A obtains at the same time; but if [statt] non A follows upon A, then this is [potentialiter] *disparatum*, *non oppositum*.\nThe change of a thing or alteration is what [mit] agrees very well together and where both follow necessarily upon one another. Only the thing must not cease to be, because then there is also no succession, consequently the real opposite.",["“change” is Wechsel, “alteration” Veränderung; “thing” stands for both Ding and Sache.","“at the same time” renders both zugleich and in derselben Zeit.","*disparatum, non oppositum*: “disparate, not opposed”."]],"reflm:3772":["Reality is (g that whose concept is) το *esse*;\nNegation is\nτο *non esse*.",["το *esse*, το *non esse*: Greek article with Latin infinitive, “the being”, “the not-being”."]],"reflm:3773":["Negation is a predicate. A being is a subject. A merely negative being would be something in which there is nothing.",["“being” is *ens*."]],"reflm:3774":["Opposite and \nReality is either relatively positing or relatively canceling. The diversity is only from the relation.\nThe negation of one of the opposites is the positing of the other.",["The AA's note on the Reflexion's position (“Opposite and”) is incomplete in the source.","“relation” renders *relatione*; “canceling” *tollens*."]],"reflm:3775":["The (s transcendentally) maximal being is the most real, and generally, in which there is not synthetic or extensive quantity, but intensive; for in the former the synthesis is never terminated. (s every minimum or maximum is only relatively such. e.g. diameter, periphery with respect to the area.) (s Comparative quantity of the universe: *universitas*.)",["“maximal being” is *ens maximum*, “most real” *realissimum*.","*et generaliter, in qvo*: the subject of “in which” is left unexpressed, as in the Latin.","*universitas* (spelled vniversitas) is left in Latin: “universality” or “the whole”."]],"reflm:3776":["Opposite and \nThe concepts of the (g absolutely) greatest and least in mathematical (g and ideal) synthesis are deceiving concepts; but in the real synthesis of qualities they are possible and are equal to totality, but in repeating the same there is no absolute totality.",["The AA's note on the Reflexion's position (“Opposite and”) is incomplete in the source.","“totality” is *omnitudo*; “deceiving concepts” *conceptus deceptores*.","*absolute* (“absolutely”) may qualify “greatest and least” or “concepts”."]],"reflm:3777":["several things in a being are either to be counted or to be weighed.\nthe minimum of a ground is a moment.",["“ground” is *rationis*; “moment” *momentum*."]],"reflm:3778":["negative determinations are not accidents.",[]],"reflm:3779":["The empirical concept of substance is identity in all varieties.",["“in all” renders bey allen, also “among all”."]],"reflm:3780":["inherence is not an accident.",[]],"reflm:3781":["In any accident there is regarded first the relation of real dependence on the [subiecto] substance, second the logical relation as of a predicate toward the subject. If there is only the former, the consequence does not therefore inhere in the ground, as spinoza wrongly held about the world in relation to God. if there is only the latter, the subject will be only logical, not a substance, and the predicate a determination, not an accident.",["“relation” renders both *respectus* and *relatione*; “consequence” is *rationatum*, “ground” *ratio*."]],"reflm:3782":["All properties which the poets personify are *phaenomena substantiata*.",["“properties” is Eigenschaften; *phaenomena substantiata*: “substantialized phenomena”."]],"reflm:3783":["The *accidentia* are not particular things that inhere in the subject, but predicates of a subject, i.e., ways in which the subject exists. The concept of inherence is a logical auxiliary means, because there one can represent all things in the form of predicates.",["“things” stands for both Dinge and sachen; “ways” is arten.","“there” (daselbst) may refer to the concept of inherence or to logic."]],"reflm:3784":["Whether [s] an accident is something accessory to the substance (g as color) and properly inheres in it, or only a form of the substance itself, positive and its actual determination? e.g. Motion inhering in a body.",["“and” renders “ct”, presumably a misreading of *et*."]],"reflm:3785":["three things are to be thought: substance, accident, the relation of both. This, however, is either of the accident and is inherence; or of the substance and [est] this again either of a potential ground and is force, or of an actuating one and is action.",["“three things” renders the distributive *trina*; “relation” is *respectus*, “ground” *rationis*, “force” *vis*."]],"reflm:3786":["Just as in logical matters subject, predicate and relation are distinguished, and therefore [praedicatum] the relation is not a predicate, so also in real matters. force is not an accident, but the relation of the substance as of a ground [resp] toward the accidents.",["“relation” is *respectus*, “force” *vis*; “in logical matters”, “in real matters”: *in logicis*, *in realibus*."]],"reflm:3787":["The *respectus* of a *partis* and of its *complementi ad totum* must be [mutuis] *mutuus* and *homogeneus*; thus a consequence cannot be a part of the ground and belong with the ground, as a part, to the same whole. The thought is not a part of the human being, but its consequence.",["*respectus partis et complementi ad totum*: “the relation of a part and of its complement to the whole”, Latin inflections kept as Kant wrote them.","“consequence” is Folge; “thought” Gedanke."]],"reflm:3788":["The mode of composition is not the essence of the composite, but of the composition.\nComposition is the form. The parts, the matter.",[]],"reflm:3789":["The question is whether (g in) a substantial composite there is no substance, but only substances, and whether only the plural of it is possible. A *totum syntheticum* is that whose [The] composition is grounded, as regards its possibility, on the parts, which can also be thought without [diese] all composition. A *totum analyticum* is that whose parts, as regards their possibility, already presuppose the composition in the whole. *Spatium* and *tempus* are *tota analytica*, bodies *synthetica*. A composite of substances is a *totum syntheticum*. A *totum analyticum* is neither composed of substances nor of accidents, but a whole of possible relations.",["*totum syntheticum*, *totum analyticum*: “synthetic whole”, “analytic whole”; *Spatium* and *tempus*: “space” and “time”.","“composition” is Zusammensetzung, and in the Latin *compositum*.","[The] is a deleted beginning, perhaps of “Theile”."]],"reflm:3790":["Either space contains the ground of the possibility of the copresence of many substances and their relations, or these contain the ground of the possibility of space.",["“copresence” is compraesentz.","“these” may refer to the substances or to their relations."]],"reflm:3791":["division is either logical, insofar as various things are regarded as contained under some higher notion. Mathematical, insofar as several things are distinguished as parts of the same whole (ideal division). Physical, insofar as it proceeds into parts in such a way that the real nexus through which they constitute the same whole is canceled (real division).\n(s A monad is not mathematically divisible either, since the several things discernible in it are not parts, but consequences.\nlogical division is distinguished from analysis; the latter is division of the notion [pr] and metaphysical; the former division of the sphere of the notion, and the less the notion is metaphysically indivisible, the greater the sphere.)",["“notion” is *notio*, “consequences” *rationata*, “canceled” *tollatur*.","“it proceeds into parts”: impersonal *agitur in partes*."]],"reflm:3792":["A moment is an element of a ground. Therefore the moment of free descent on an inclined plane is different. Moments *in facto*.",["“ground” is *rationis*; *in facto*: “in the fact” or “in the deed”."]],"reflm:3793":["(s The quantity of one thing as a whole is regarded extensively.\nThe quantity of one thing as a ground is regarded intensively.)\n[Die W] There is in a kettle full of boiling water more heat than in a spoon of the same, but not greater. Two donkeys pull a wagon with more speed, but not with greater.\nIf many individual things cannot be so combined that they give a greater degree — — breaks off.\nMore virtue, greater virtue; more welfare, greater welfare.",["“ground” is *rationis*; “heat” Wärme, “combined” verbunden.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's notice that the sentence is unfinished.","[Die W] is a deleted beginning, presumably of “Die Wärme”."]],"reflm:3794":["Infinity relates to the absence of an end (*limitis*), either in adding: and then it is the greatest, or in |\ntaking away: and then it is not for that reason the greatest. The former is as it were like a line to which none can be added. The second like one from which a certain line can never be taken away in such a way that it reaches the end.",["“it reaches the end” (sie das Ende treffe): “sie” may be the line taken away or the line it is taken from."]],"reflm:3795":["it is not yet established whether the greatest reality is compossible in a simultaneous being or in a successive series.",["*in ente compossibilis sit simultaneo*: “simultaneo” is taken with *ente* (“in a simultaneous being”); it could also be read “compossible with what is simultaneous”."]],"reflm:3796":["The impenetrable inert extended is matter.",["“the extended” is *extensum*; “inert” *iners*."]],"reflm:3797":["Opposite and \nThe existence of a being which all duration succeeds is the *Initium* (beginning).\nThe existence of a being which succeeds all duration is the *Finis* (end).\nBeginning and end are the termini of duration, the one *a parte ante*, the other *a parte post*. Beginning and end are *Instantia* (instants).",["'Gegenüber und' is a locator remark, cut off in the source.","'cui succedit omnis duratio': literally 'to which all duration succeeds', i.e. what all duration follows upon."]],"reflm:3798":["Useful is what is mediately agreeable, i.e. is a |\nmeans to happiness; for the gratifications are the elements of happiness, perfection consists of others.",["Vergnügen = gratification(s).","'besteht aus andern': 'of other ones' (other elements) or 'of others' (other things); left open."]],"reflm:3799":["If the concept of the world signified the whole of all possible things, namely those which are possible in interconnection with the all-sufficient ground, then it would be more fruitful.\nOf cosmology. 1. *commercium* of substances.",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; allgnugsam = all-sufficient."]],"reflm:3800":["The question is twofold: whether for some given world another* could be surrogated, i.e. substituted, or whether to a given world another could be superadded. The former is permitted on account of contingency (unless it is supposed that it must have a totality that is such *simpliciter*); the latter is not permitted for this reason, that they would be joined in time, either in the same [or] part of it or in different ones.\n*(s *alius* (another) is either *per oppositionem* or *per additionem*.)",["'vel eadem [vel] ipsius parte vel diversis': the deleted 'vel' leaves the construction doubled; rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:3801":["121. E II 1736.\nM 121':\nThe mathematical law of continuity is: Space and time are continua.\nThe physical law of continuity is: just as between any points posited outside one another in a simultaneous series [is space] there lies a continuous series of points, so also between two states succeeding one another there lies a continuous series of states,[as] also in diversity a continuous series of degrees of diversity. e.g. Of a motion the direction is not changed except through the continuous interposition of degrees of intervening directions. So perhaps neither the state of the mind.\nThe cosmological law of continuity is. Between any diverse species lie all possible intermediate degrees, and there is a continuous series of degrees of diversity.\nThe logical law of continuity.\n(s No sequence of two states is immediate, otherwise an instant would be common to them: *simultaneorum oppositorum contradictio* (contradiction of simultaneous opposites).)",["'Motus non mutatur directio' is irregular as it stands (Motus nominative beside directio); rendered literally.","'est.' after 'cosmologica' stands with a period before its content; kept.","mens = mind; Folge = sequence; Zustand = state."]],"reflm:3802":["Cause and caused do not belong to the same whole, because then the cause would suffer externally from itself; therefore the cause of the world is an extramundane being.",["'pateretur' = would suffer (be acted upon)."]],"reflm:3803":["Every Spinozist is an egoist. It is asked whether every egoist is necessarily a Spinozist.",[]],"reflm:3804":["The absolute perfection of the world does not consist in the multitude, order, variety of substances, but in the greatest [sense] and purest sense of pleasure. Hence the rules of perfection thus so-called physico-mechanical do not collide with the pneumatic ones, since in the former there is no perfection.",["'ita sic dictae': 'ita' and 'sic' doubled in the source; both kept.","voluptas = pleasure; pnevmaticis = pneumatic (of spirits)."]],"reflm:3805":["Rules of the perfection of the world of spirits and of the natural never stand in collision, for the latter has no absolute perfection.",["Geisterwelt = world of spirits."]],"reflm:3806":[") (ι1?) M 147'. E II 768.\nThe difference between the *influxu ideali* and *reali* makes no distinct system, but is merely a correctly determined concept of *influxus* in general. But because all *influxus* is ideal, it can be concluded from this that it becomes possible in no other way to think a *harmonia* of substances in their self-activity than insofar as they depend on one.*\nSpace and time are the first relations which all things receive thereby, and the first grounds of the possibility of a world-whole.\n(g The theory of the *influxus idealis* is not a [ground of a] particular system, but a ground of the abolition of the *influxus physici*. Then [still the possibility] must be shown how a *harmonia sub conditione influxus realis* (harmony under the condition of real influence) is possible. Then the possibility [of an *influxus*] of a *harmoniae praestabilitae* or rather its necessity must be demonstrated.)",["The asterisk after 'einem' has no corresponding note in the passage.","'ein [Grund eines] besonderes System': the article and adjective do not agree after the deletion; rendered as it stands.","gedenken = think; Weltganzes = world-whole; besonder = particular."]],"reflm:3807":["Rational beings in other worlds (s theurgy). *spiritus astrales*. *angeli*. Influence into the spirit of the human being. Citation of departed souls or of those still living, haunting.\nPoltergeists. (s idol. Fetish. Talisman. Amulet. sympathy.)\nRiebezahl. (g kobold.) *incubi*, *succubi*. delrio.\nChangeling. Vampire. (s Subterraneans.)\nWater-nixies. Mountain-mannikins. Ghost-seeing.\nCitation. *Pactum*. *Magia*. *obsessio*. *Exorcismus*. Ventriloquists.",["Citation = summoning (of spirits); kept as 'citation'.","Geist = spirit; mind. 'spücken' = spuken (to haunt).","'Riebezahl' (Rübezahl) and 'delrio' (Del Rio) kept in Kant's spelling."]],"reflm:3808":["The original being (The [first] independent one), highest, supreme If. (Ground.) The being of all beings (of all). The supreme ground of everything.\nThat it is no sequel, hence a first,\nThat it is a (g first) ground,\nThat it is a ground of everything.",["'Wenn' (If) is doubtful; it may be a slip, but is kept as it stands.","'er' (masculine) is rendered 'it'; it may refer to Grund or to the being as God.","Urwesen = original being; Folge = sequel."]],"reflm:3809":["The possibility of a thing is twofold: either 1, that what is there is combined in a certain way according to laws which already obtain in them by virtue of their own properties. e.g. If wood is there, then a house of wood is possible, or also that from forces an effect flows. 2, The possibility where nothing is there; because then there is no matter there for anything at all, so also the concept of no thing can arise. Thus the concept of possibility is indeed *logice* before actuality according to given concepts, but *realiter* it does not go before everything actual. Thus [that] a being which contains the matter for all possibility and in which possibility is to be distinguished from actuality is just as absurd as, when one takes space for an *obiect*, one cannot say it is possible.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Materie = matter; Eigenschaft = property.","The last sentence is irregular in construction (after the deletion of 'dasienige'); rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:3810":["Possibility is logically discerned only through [pr] judgments in which there is no connection, also no conflict.",["einsehen = discern (have insight into); Verknüpfung = connection; Wiederstreit = conflict."]],"reflm:3811":["All *negationes* are limitations. Limitations in general are possible only through the unlimited. Accordingly the unlimited is that where|by\neverything else is possible. The possibility of things *ascendendo* happens through comparison, *descendendo* it is discerned through derivation.",["Schranken = limitations; 'wo|durch' split across the line in the source."]],"reflm:3812":["In the Wolffian proof one can indeed cognize the independence, but not the (g inner) necessity; for its existence is necessary only for the sake of the world.\nFrom the necessity he infers the *omnitudinem realitatum*. — — If that is correct, [that] then he had no need to substruct an experience.\nIt is strange that one wants to prove the existence of an absolutely necessary being from something other than from the necessary. For if one understands what absolutely necessary signifies, and cognizes that the concept is something, then the proof has been done. If one does not know this breaks off.",["'absolutely' renders both schlechterdings and schlechthin.","'sein Daseyn': 'its' or 'his' existence; left open.","substruiren = substruct (lay underneath)."]],"reflm:3813":["It is to be proved, 1. that an absolutely necessary being is possible. 2. That it is *realissimum*. The absolute necessity of the thing must be derived from concepts [and not from] and not from the relation with other existing things.\nIt does not consist in this, that the opposite contradicts itself.\nNo opposite of existence contradicts itself; only of propositions.\nIt must thus be necessary in connection with the possible. Not as a sequel, but as a ground.\nPossibility is a *respective* position. Much can indeed be posited according to possibility, although it is not posited *absolute*; but without that nothing at all exists, there is no possibility.\n(g All simple possibilities contain the material ground of possibility; their possibility does not rest on the principle of contradiction.)",["'ohne das gar nichts existirt': either 'without that (anything existing), nothing at all exists' or 'without (the fact) that nothing at all exists'; the ambiguity is kept.","Satz des Wiederspruchs = principle of contradiction; setzen = posit."]],"reflm:3814":["Opposite the numeral of M § 815:\nAll judgments are logical or real. The latter are of existence and cannot, if they concern absolute necessity, be cognized through the principle of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3815":["God contains the material ground of all possibility, and herein lies at the same time, according to the *principio contradictionis*, the formal ground of it.\nThat God contains the ground of the essence of things is no *perfectio operativa*, but *qviescens*.\nThe divine all-sufficiency is either *omnisufficientia interna vel externa*.",["'die Materialen Grund': article and noun do not agree (plural 'grounds' is possible); rendered singular.","Wesen der Dinge = essence of things; Allgnugsamkeit = all-sufficiency."]],"reflm:3816":["God is [firstly] represented as the being of the highest perfection, as a ground, namely as a ground of all perfections in the ontological, secondly in the cosmotheological sense.",["Verstand here = sense (meaning), not 'understanding'."]],"reflm:3817":["According to the analogy of empirical concepts breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3818":["analogy of the one born blind with regard to colors and tones. The cognition of the properties of God with regard to the moral is dogmatic and positive, with regard to the theoretical is [analo] critical and negative. That one not err is the foremost thing. Thus the theoretical cognition that we need is very simple.",["einfältig = simple (plain)."]],"reflm:3819":["Moral properties. The goodness of the creator, The holiness of the lawgiver, The justice of the judge. } }\n}All-sufficiency as to duration, as to power, as to [omniscience] cognition. Eternity, omnipotence, omniscience.",["German 'der Dauer, der Macht, der [Allwissenheit nach] Erkentnis nach': 'nach' (as to) is placed after the nouns; the deleted words include it.","The braces are carried over as they stand."]],"reflm:3820":["That there is an original being.\nwhether there are more original beings?",[]],"reflm:3821":["That there can be only one most perfect being is clear.",[]],"reflm:3822":["The supreme cause is the author of the world through freedom.\nthe world is a being from another, but not from several, but from one; not through nature, but through freedom; therefore the cause of the world is author and God; but that God is at the same time all-sufficient and unique cannot be concluded from this.",["Urheber / auctor = author; omnisufficiens = all-sufficient."]],"reflm:3823":["Whether there are comparative gods, whose right is absolute with respect to human beings, and the subjection of human beings natural. It is denied. for there is no |\nnatural subjection, except under the will of him who cannot be obligated, therefore not under a creature.",[]],"reflm:3824":["Independence and omnisufficiency are the two *principia cognoscitiva* for determining the properties of God.",["'Independentz' and 'omnisufficientz' are Germanized Latin in the source."]],"reflm:3825":["*Cognitio prototypa* (prototypal cognition): cause of things.\n*Cognitio ectypa* (ectypal cognition): the caused.",[]],"reflm:3826":["God cognizes everything in that he cognizes himself.\nThe human being cognizes himself in that he cognizes other things.",[]],"reflm:3827":["One could ask whether all will also presupposes a representation of the good in the *obiect* and whether he who wills the highest good could not will still more than is good; the last will. If the ground of willing does not need a *causam moventem* in the *obiect*, but rests in the subject's nature, then breaks off.",["'der letzte wille': 'the last will' or 'the latter will'; left open."]],"reflm:3828":["The motive of God is only analogically related to ours; no ground of his action is in the *obiect*; but, because he |\nconsiders himself as the cause and as the most perfect cause, he is the author of the best.",["Bewegungsgrund = motive; betrachten = consider."]],"reflm:3829":["The concepts of substances, insofar as they are abstracted, are only respective: hence bodies are substances insofar as they are the subjects of the inherence of their accidents and inhere in no other known subject; but they would not be called substances insofar as they in turn [be] have another subject, of which they are merely the effects.",["'respectiv' = respective (relative)."]],"reflm:3830":["That will with which a smaller (g cognized) good agrees more than a greater is a worse will. The proof is conducted here *a posteriori* that no better world is possible. In the case [that] of the infinite series of better worlds no best will would have to be possible. Indeed, every possible will would be infinitely evil. The proof of *optimismus* under the hypothesis: there is a God, is more evident than even the proof of the divine existence. indeed, it is still more necessary, or rather one needs the idea of God only for this. Only the idea of the *maximum* can teach me what I have to expect from God.\nIt is asked whether not even only a single world is possible.\n(s One can represent to oneself no existence at all (in agreement with reason) which would be equally possible with the not-being. This is also to be regarded as a difficulty for the rational concept of freedom.)",["'welches mit dem nicht seyn gleich moglich sey': 'equally possible with (its) not-being', or, reading 'dem' as a pronoun, 'which would not be equally possible with that'; kept ambiguous.","Source has 'maximi' (genitive of maximum)."]],"reflm:3831":["Whether it is possible to create a spontaneous substance or one of a given degree of spontaneity.",[]],"reflm:3832":["At the very top of the page:\nOne will sooner say: infinite space is in God, than: God is in infinite space.",[]],"reflm:3833":["Naturalism is either of method or dogmatic naturalism; the latter is either of conservation or of origin, but this one is atheism. Naturalism of method is either of a propaedeutic or of a universal one; the former is Epicurus's and quite rational, the latter is atheism negatively such.",["'propaedeuticae vel vniversalis' are genitive, apparently with 'methodi' understood."]],"reflm:3834":["Synthesis is either philosophical or mathematical, and likewise also analysis; philosophical synthesis is combination through a ground.",["Verbindung = combination."]],"reflm:3835":["Nothing according to the concept or according to existence.",[]],"reflm:3836":["On the inner margin \nMaterials and form belong to impossibility, not to the not-possible.",["Unmöglichkeit (impossibility) is distinguished from Nichtmögliches (the not-possible)."]],"reflm:3837":["It is precisely the opposite, when a definition includes a circle, of that when it involves a *contradictio*.\nIn the case of what is necessary we do not ask why it is. Why a triangle has 3 sides. but rather: why three sides are.",["Erklärung = definition."]],"reflm:3838":["Contingent is that whose not-being is possible. Being is taken here without regard to time, whether it be the present, past or future. Not-being is the complete cancellation and thus from all time. The alterations themselves can be necessary; and if they are contingent, then the persistence of the thing is possible.\nThe opposite of a predicate at another time [signifies] proves that the subject in and for itself is undetermined with regard to it. It does not prove that it is possible that the determination had not been in the previous time. The alterations themselves, if they are necessary, since none of the successive determinations takes place without the opposite of the previous one being at the same time, prove not so much the contingency as the restrictions, i.e. the nothing which follows the something with equal steps.",["'in Ansehung derselben': 'with regard to it' (the predicate) or 'to that (time)'; left open.","Aufhebung = cancellation; Einschränkungen = restrictions; Beharrlichkeit = persistence."]],"reflm:3839":["That magnitude which can be the ground of another not otherwise than through diminution or separation is *extensiva*. That which is a ground without diminution is *intensiva*.\nThe magnitude of matter and form.\nThe degrees of possibility.",[]],"reflm:3840":["The elapsed infinite which has a beginning is impos|sible.\nIf everything future is a merely mathematical repetition, then future infinity is to be regarded as possible. But if the future is determined from the previous or otherwise, then it makes up a whole which is thoroughly determined through some ground or other, and its infinity is no more graspable than that of past and present time.",["durchgängig bestimmt = thoroughly determined; faßlich = graspable."]],"reflm:3841":["There is no necessary completeness of possible coordination, hence also no (g necessary) bounds of a whole in which the quality of the unities is undetermined. (g all bounds are conditions of a whole of contingent magnitude.) Hence every whole is only contingently determined with respect to the bounds, and the possibility of still more parts is the void. Hence because the elapsed time constitutes a whole, it has bounds, before which a time precedes. The future, which constitutes no whole, has no bounds. The (g multitude of) things in the present time constitutes a whole which has bounds, and the world is thus in absolute empty space. On the other hand every series of subordinated things necessarily has *a priori* a bound, and the *universitas* itself extends to all possible, not merely actual members, and the first of things can be seen into through reason.",["'bounds': Grentzen (Grenzen).","'unities': Einheiten, which can also be read as 'units'.","'be seen into': eingesehen werden (einsehen, to have insight into)."]],"reflm:3842":["In the concept of all together there must be a particular ground of the taking-together of all in the thing itself. If now each member is thought synthetically to the others, then the present past time is that which takes them all together, because it is itself a whole. [In the analytic] If the parts are given analytically from the whole, then the *completudo* of the *analysis* is with the breaks off.",["'thing': Sache.","'present past time': Gegenwärtig Vergangene Zeit; Gegenwärtig is uninflected and may be adverbial ('the presently past time').","'breaks off' renders the AA's notice 'bricht ab'; the sentence is cut off after 'with the'."]],"reflm:3843":["The relation of the cause to the effect is no relation of *identity*; consequently there is also neither similarity nor equality between cause and effect, but rather *conformity*. From the effect the cause is to be cognized and also to be named. *talis est notio causae qva talis, qvalis oritur e notione causati*.\nWe know every thing of the world only as cause, but in the cause only the *causality* of the effect, thus only the effects, and thus not the thing itself and its determinations, through which [they] it produces the effect.",["The Latin: 'the concept of the cause as such is such as arises from the concept of what is caused'.","'identity', 'conformity', 'causality': Kant's Latinate identitaet, conformitaet, caussalitaet.","'know': kennen."]],"reflm:3844":["*Effectus plenus* is measured not *in serie subordinatorum, sed coordinatorum*, that is not through the series of the effects of the effects, e.g. of a ball's force through the series of the balls which it can mediately move. From this one sees that it is always only the same effect which continues in time, but in different conditions; thus the effect which arose immediately always continues and neither increases nor diminishes.",["The Latin: 'the full effect' is measured not 'in the series of things subordinated, but of things coordinated'.","'continues': fortdauern, twice.","'ball': Kugel, also 'sphere'."]],"reflm:3845":["102'. E II 719.\nTo the closing word of M § 333 Kant adds: *aliqva*, and then continues on p. 102':\nbut the question is: what do we cognize first. Do we cognize first that something is an effect, and thus has a cause, or that something is a cause and thus has an effect. The former. For that |\nsomething is an effect, what happens, is posited by reason only *per aliud*; we cognize this *nexum* only *a posteriori*. were we to cognize causes beforehand, then the *nexus* would be cognized *a priori*.",["The second line is the AA editor's note, not Kant's text.","'what happens' (was geschieht) may stand in apposition to 'something' or be the subject of 'is an effect'; the German leaves it open.","'first': eher (sooner); 'posited': gesetzt; per aliud: 'through another'."]],"reflm:3846":["The immediate goodness is for the understanding the greatest, chiefly because it is necessary and universal; the mediate usefulness is smaller, because it pleases only contingently and under circumstances.",["'goodness': Kant's Latinate bonitaet (bonitas)."]],"reflm:3847":["If many causes agree together to one effect, then they are connected among one another *a posteriori*; from this one infers that they are so also *a priori*, i.e. that there is a common cause of them. Are breaks off.",["'connected': verbunden.","The last sentence breaks off after its first word, Sind ('Are', probably opening a conditional 'If they are'); 'breaks off' renders the AA's 'bricht ab'."]],"reflm:3848":["For reason not the *data*, but form are what is essential.",["The plural verb sind agrees with data, not with form, as in the German; 'what is essential': das Wesentliche."]],"reflm:3849":["The concept of matter also extends to something passive, hence the substantial forms are active forces: entelechies. Spirits in their republic cannot be called the matter of the whole, because they are not passive, but are combined self-actively and constitute their whole.",["'passive': leidend (literally 'suffering'); 'self-actively': selbstthätig.","'Spirits': Geister; 'combined': verbunden."]],"reflm:3850":["*forma dat esse rei*. For what is essential in the thing can be cognized only through reason; but now all matter of cognition must be given through senses; thus the essence of things is [merely], insofar as they are cognized through reason, the form.",["The Latin: 'form gives being to the thing'.","'what is essential in the thing': das Wesentliche der Sache; 'essence': Wesen."]],"reflm:3851":["Matter is at all times regarded as passive, and form depends on activity, likewise all activity extends to nothing further than form. Meanwhile corporeal matter as substance is according to Leibniz an *entelechia*, i.e. something active. Our cognition is merely passive as to the *data*, and the form rests on self-activity.",["'passive': leidend; 'self-activity': Selbstthätigkeit."]],"reflm:3852":["Form gives being to the thing. for through the understanding we cognize only form. Matter contains the data, from which various things can arise by determining in diverse ways; and therefore the determinate being of any thing depends on the form. Form is the specific difference.",["'understanding': intellectus; 'cognize': cognoscere.","'the determinate being': determinatum esse, which can also be read as 'being determined'."]],"reflm:3853":["On the 1st proposition of M § 381 (10718f.):\nFor otherwise all members of the series would be *caussata* of another member of the series. [Thus wou] Thus there would be a member of the series which would be something other than all members of the same.",["The first line is the AA editor's note; (10718f.) is page 107, lines 18f.","caussata: 'things caused'."]],"reflm:3854":["If the present (our) world is the [most per] only existing one, then it is also the most perfect whole from a single original being. But if there are several worlds, then one must surely be the most perfect among all possible ones, but perhaps not ours.\nThe idea of the most perfect world is as needful to us as of the most perfect cause; indeed the latter is properly needful to assume for the sake of the former.",["[most per] renders the deleted fragment allervo(llkommenste).","'needful': nöthig, kept apart from notwendig ('necessary').","'original being': Urwesen."]],"reflm:3855":["Life is the faculty of beginning a state (its own or that of another) from an inner *principium*. The former is not a complete life, inasmuch as that whose state is alterable itself requires something external as cause. Bodies do have an inner *principium* of acting in one another (e.g. interconnection), also [something] of preserving an externally imparted state, but not of beginning of themselves. Thus all alteration, all origin proves a first beginning and consequently freedom. [Thus] But because the beginning can be *comparative* the first, namely according to mechanical laws. e.g. When a carcass smells to the dog, then motion begins in it, which, because it was not produced according to mechanical laws by the smell, but was caused through the stirring up of desire. In animals, however, this is just as much an external necessitation as in machines; hence they are called *automata spiritualia*. but in the human being the chain of determining causes is in every case cut off, and hence one also distinguishes |\nthe immaterial as a *principium* of life from the material. In the human being the spirit is free and wills the good; the animal is *automaton*; were this spirit always to be effective only in the animal and not to alternate with its forces, then we would find more proofs of freedom.\n(s *libertas est independentia a necessitatione externa*.\n*libertas est vel originaria vel derivativa [haec vel]. Arbitrium autem est vel sensitivum vel intellectuale; illud est vel brutum vel liberum.*)",["'interconnection': Zusammenhang, here possibly physical cohesion; 'necessitation': Nothigung.","'smells to': anricht, a doubtful form, probably for anriecht; the sentences beginning 'But because' and 'which, because' are incomplete in the German.","The Latin: 'freedom is independence from external necessitation. freedom is either original or derivative [this either]. Choice, however, is either sensitive or intellectual; the former is either brute or free.'"]],"reflm:3856":["To be determined in freedom is indeed admissible, but not passively, neither through the way objects affect, nor through a supreme producing cause. I can say: in this moment I am free (g *liber aut devinctus*) and unbound to do what pleases me; but it is nevertheless unavoidably necessary that I act thus. It is a law of self-activity which makes the opposite impossible. Can one also with respect to the (g moral) evil be determined just so from free resolve. No! one can be determined to it only passively or not at all, because the pure will nevertheless always remains and thus cannot be bound at all, but does not always *exsert* its activity.",["liber aut devinctus: 'free or bound'.","'is indeed admissible': geht schon an; 'resolve': Vorsatz.","'exsert': exseriren (exserere, to put forth)."]],"reflm:3857":["The substance acts freely which, not externally determined, produces something which previously was not, and this freedom is opposed to inner or outer natural necessity. It acts from free choice insofar as the *causality* of the action lies in the good pleasure, which is not passive. The difficulties concern only the first idea of |\nfreedom, and it is incomprehensible in the necessary being as well as in the contingent. but for different reasons, because the former can begin nothing, but the latter can begin nothing first. The first degree of (g independence is) self-activity [is]: that of a substance in general; the second degree: independence in acting from all external determining causes; the third degree: independence from its own nature.\nThus the negative is properly incomprehensible, the positive of the motives is comprehensible.",["'choice': Willkühr; 'good pleasure': Belieben.","'incomprehensible' / 'comprehensible': unbegreiflich / begreiflich; 'motives': motiven, not Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:3858":["In all actions the ground, the *causality*, lies (g as to matter) in nature; but as to form either merely in nature, e.g. attraction, or in another faculty, which inwardly directs the forces of nature. The latter is freedom.",[]],"reflm:3859":["Freedom is (g a) practically necessary fundamental concept.\nThe first necessary is not to be comprehended, because there is no ground of it; the first contingent is also not [of] to be comprehended, because a ground making necessary must be there. The former, because it is necessary, although it has no ground, the latter, because it is contingent, although it has one.",["'fundamental concept': Grundbegrif; 'comprehended': begreifen.","'a ground making necessary': ein nothwendig machender Grund."]],"reflm:3860":["For freedom 1. *spontaneitas simpliciter talis* (*automaton*) (g *independentia a causis subiective necessitantibus (stimulis)*) is needful, so that [the arb what from the subject] the determinations can be attributed to the subject as its action. 2. The faculty of the *arbitrii*\n|\n*intellectualis*, so that it can be imputed to it as a *factum*. Because everything that happens presupposes, according to laws of the faculty of desire, a satisfaction, the *complacentia* which is independent of the subjective *necessitation* must be intellectual, and thus the former presupposes the latter.\nThe greatest difficulty lies in this: how a subjectively unconditioned choice can be thought (g *est obiective hypotheticum*) in (g according to) the *nexu causarum efficientium sive determinantium* or, if one departs from that, how the *imputability* of actions is possible.",["The Latin: 'spontaneity simply as such'; 'independence from causes subjectively necessitating (stimuli)'; 'of intellectual choice'; 'is objectively hypothetical'; 'the nexus of efficient or determining causes'.","'satisfaction': wohlgefallen; 'choice': Willkühr; 'faculty of desire': Begehrungsvermögen.","'needful': nothig."]],"reflm:3861":["On aversation.\nThe natural and practical possibility [has] is the same breaks off.\nThe first question is: how one is to comprehend the *causality* [of a] of freedom, whether it breaks off.\nIn a contingent (g acting) being the causality can be either necessitated or dependent.\nWhether the generation (g *genesis*) of the action is determined *aliunde* or *spontanea*.",["'breaks off' renders the AA's 'bricht ab', twice.","'is the same': ist die einerley.","aliunde: 'from elsewhere'; spontanea: 'spontaneous'."]],"reflm:3862":["One often says: The human being has talents, he also has no evil heart. He could become useful. It lies only in willing. One would think this circumstance can easily be changed; but nevertheless it does not happen (g Negligent). If he now in addition has an evil heart: will it then still be able to happen?\nIs, in evil human beings, the will evil, or is it good, but weak. The means of making the will good are moral; of making it strong, pragmatic. If one seeks to practice resoluteness, steadfastness in resolve, resignation. In some it seems not possible to produce a good will (although correct power of judgment, i.e. provisional will), in others not a strong will.",["'Negligent': Nachlaßig.","'resolve': Vorsatz; 'power of judgment': Urtheilskraft."]],"reflm:3863":["(g sensuality)\nFreedom from brutality (*spontaneitas practice talis*)\nFreedom from fatality (*transscendentalis*)\n*actus arbitrii vel originarii vel derivativi*.",["The Latin: 'spontaneity practically as such'; 'transcendental'; 'act of choice either original or derivative'.","'sensuality', 'brutality', 'fatality': Kant's Latinate sensualitaet, brutalitaet, fatalitaet."]],"reflm:3864":["Good pleasure is a desiring without any necessity of an efficient cause; consequently a pure good pleasure can be necessary for the sake of the goodness of the thing and is nevertheless free from inclination and breaks off.",["'Good pleasure': Belieben; 'goodness': bonitaet; 'thing': Sache.","'breaks off' renders the AA's 'bricht ab'."]],"reflm:3865":["Freedom is properly a faculty of subordinating all voluntary actions to the motives of reason.",["'voluntary': willkührliche (from Willkühr, choice); 'motives': Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:3866":["The *stimuli* [move] do not necessitate our choice *objective*, i.e. that which one ought to do is independent of all sensible stimuli.",["'sensible stimuli': sinnlichen Reizen; the first 'stimuli' is Kant's Latin stimuli.","objective: 'objectively'; 'choice': Willkühr."]],"reflm:3867":["No one counts as part of freedom the faculty of being able to desire that which is worthy of abhorrence (evil).\nWe thus have sensible cognitions. sensible pleasure and displeasure and sensible desires. The faculty according to motives of reason is freedom. The possibility of willing with consciousness that which is disapproved by reason is the weak will; the [Inc] aptitude for willing evil is the evil will.",["[Inc] renders the deleted fragment Nei(gung), 'inclination'.","'aptitude': Fertigkeit; 'motives': Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:3868":["The faculty of actively willing the cognized good, which is in our power, is freedom; but [not] there does not belong to it just as necessarily the faculty of willing the cognized evil, whose prevention is in our power. This is also not properly a faculty, but a possibility of suffering. Evil actions do stand under freedom, but do not happen through it.",["'suffering': leiden, also 'being passive'."]],"reflm:3869":["The true ends with respect to things are subordinated to the idea of the good; but with respect to the person the idea of that which is personally good is subordinated to the end, not which the person has, but to which the nature of the person is arranged and which one knows through observation.\nOf all things there is a twofold ground of their nature: the cause and the end. The end seems to be just as much a ground of existence, namely of the worth of existence, as the efficient cause.",["'existence' stands for both Daseyn and the Latinate existentz.","'end': Zwek (Zweck); 'efficient cause': Wirkende Ursache."]],"reflm:3870":["M 283':\n(s pathological is that which, as to the determination of the force, is in conformity with impressions.)\n(s Practical is everything that belongs to action according to rules, or everything that is connected with determining rules of actions. *Necessitatio est vel pathologica vel practica*; |\nM 283: |\nthe latter can be objective, the former not.)\nM 283':\nSomething is considered practically in general insofar as it is weighed according to the laws of free choice. If it happens according to the rules of good choice, then it is moral, hence practically and morally |\npossible or necessary. To practical philosophy there belongs only understanding and a physical feeling, to the moral understanding and a moral feeling. A human race would be possible even without the latter.",["The Latin: 'Necessitation is either pathological or practical'.","'choice': Willkühr; 'connected': verknüpft; 'impressions': Eindrüke."]],"reflm:3871":["Opposite M § 723 (13638-1371), probably \nAs concerns the inner necessity [of the distinction] in the faculty of desire, we see ourselves necessitated not only through feeling (*stimuli*) to desire something; but we see ourselves also necessitated [our these our desires] to put ourselves in the place of a being which is subject to no such desires or feeling, and to regard our actions themselves from merely universal concepts of satisfaction, and we feel ourselves necessitated to regard ourselves with abhorrence or satisfaction. Now because this abhorrence itself goes to the desire, it furnishes a more excellent law than the desire.\nWhy are we necessitated, the abhorrence vo bricht ab.",["The opening line is the AA's locus note; 'wohl' (probably) stands without its complement in the source.","Wohlgefallen = satisfaction; Abscheu = abhorrence; 'ein vorzüglicher Gesetz' is ungrammatical in the German (for 'vorzüglicheres').","'vo' is a broken-off word, left as it stands; 'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the editor's."]],"reflm:3872":["The free will is as it were isolated. Nothing external determines it; it is active without suffering. The *motiva* are only objects that harmonize with the inner law of its activity.* The good moves only a good will, i.e., it is only the effect [according to] that is possible according to laws of the same. Whether it be determined by moving causes or by *causis efficientibus* (the Godhead), the *subiect* is hung onto a foreign cause by means of a chain, and its actions are only derived, the good as well as the evil.\nFreedom consists in the faculty of being able to act independently of external determining grounds according to intellectual choice. [Of it] Under this stands all sensibility. Hence |\nwe think even precipitancies or a series of obscure representations, which are the causes of errors, as subject to our choice. The actions that happen according to mere rules of sensibility. We must distinguish in the human being the animal, i.e., him according to the laws of sensibility, and the spirit: according to laws of reason. His choice as animal is actually always determined by *stimulos*; but his will is nevertheless free, insofar as his reason is capable of altering these determinations of choice. Why it is that reason sometimes does not do this and the human being does not act according to the intellectual will is unknown. But precisely from this it happens that the human being, considered as spirit, censures himself as animal, is in contradiction and strife with himself, wishes himself other inclinations, but often loses himself in sensibility. The one spiritual use is to order the animal sensations and to direct means according to animal ends. The other is to consider the ends themselves *intellectualiter*.\nHow is it possible that one can censure oneself, if the self is not, so to speak, twofold? For otherwise one can indeed pass no other judgment on oneself than what is taken from oneself and also agrees with oneself.\n*(s Not the objective but the subjective grounds necessitate *actualiter*.)",["Willkühr = choice; Wille = will; Bewegursachen = moving causes; Geist = spirit (here opposed to Thier, animal).","'The actions that happen according to mere rules of sensibility' is a sentence without a main verb in the German.","'necessitiren' is a Germanized Latin verb, rendered 'necessitate'."]],"reflm:3873":["The (g contradictory) opposite of the [things] thing is to be distinguished from the opposite of its relation; the latter is the opposite of the judgment.\ne.g. a divisible body; its opposite is: *Corpus* ×0 + *divisibile* ×0 =0 *nihil privativum*. This opposite never contradicts itself.",["The deleted word is 'Sachen', the word kept 'Dinges' (thing)."]],"reflm:3874":["Space and time are mere possibilities, whose opposite, i.e., no space and no time, is impossible. (There is no space, there is no time). Now space and time are grounded on something actual, therefore something actual is absolutely necessary.",["wirklich = actual."]],"reflm:3875":["The absolute necessity of something being cognized, its existence is cognized.\nAbsolute necessity is not cognizable externally through things caused, but internally from the idea of it itself.\nAn existent with whose cancellation all possibility is cancelled is absolutely necessary.\nsome possibility (g internal) being cancelled, all possibility is cancelled; for that being cancelled, something absolutely impossible is thought; but if there were still some things thinkable, it would be possible that something is impossible. Otherwise: If some existent were absolutely necessary, another existent likewise, and so |\non: one of the existents could be cancelled with the existence of the others preserved, and therefore possibility would extend only as far as some existence reaches.\non the notion of possibility in which abstraction can be made from existence, in relation to the possibility in which abstraction cannot be made from it, e.g. space, time.\nImpossibility is either of repugnance or of negation; the former always obtains if the relation of certain given notions is impossible; the latter insofar as there is not anything that can be given for thinking, i.e., possible.",["tollere / sublatum = cancel / cancelled; causata = things caused.","'ipsius' may refer to the necessity or to the thing whose necessity it is.","The sentence after 'Aliter' is elliptical in the Latin and is left so."]],"reflm:3876":["That the possibility of something does not contain the ground of its existence itself. Nor existence that of its possibility. But that in the necessary, possibility and existence are the same. That nothing contains the ground of itself.",["Accusative-with-infinitive throughout, rendered 'That ...'; ratio = ground."]],"reflm:3877":["The cause of a substance through necessity of nature is emanative (*systema emanationis*).\nThe cause of a substance through freedom is creator.\nabsolute necessity of the substances of the universe with contingency and dependence of state bricht ab?",["'bricht ab?' (breaks off?) is the editor's, with his query."]],"reflm:3878":["Benignity is the divine will in relation to the state (g the possible happiness) of the rational creature. Holiness is the divine will as a ground of the rules of the human. Justice is the (g wise) goodness of the divine will in relation to the prescription of holiness, *virtualis*.",["Gütigkeit = benignity, Güte = goodness.","'des Menschlichen' may be read as 'of the human [will]' or 'of what is human'."]],"reflm:3879":["The world cannot be compared as regards time with any being outside the world. It has a beginning, but not in regard to God an origin, but rather a dependence. I can compare the duration of the world with its parts as an hour with minutes, but not with any thing that is distinct from the world. Each thing in the world could indeed well have been earlier, but not the world itself.",["'Eher konte wohl ein iedes Ding in der Welt gewesen seyn' can also be read 'each thing could well have been in the world earlier'."]],"reflm:3880":["The world, according to that which in it is a work of God, is the best among all possible ones. For this is the [practical] representation of the greatest practical perfection of God. The representation of the greatest perfection of the world on this side includes in itself all hopes that I can make for myself from the existence of God, as an identical proposition. And hence this proposition is also a principal proposition. But from the side of freely acting beings one cannot assert it in just the same way. If one wanted to say: God has the power to make the free beings do what pleases him, then this would be contrary to their freedom; or: he foresaw what they would do in all circumstances, and chose the best conditions, then this foreseeing would prove the necessary connection of the actions with these conditions, which are posited by God.",["'conditions' renders both the Latinate 'conditionen' and 'Bedingungen'.","Hauptsatz = principal proposition; Verknüpfung = connection."]],"reflm:3881":["Insofar as the occurrences of the world are supposed to arise from its nature, they all happen according to [communal] universal laws, because nature [with] (unless a miracle happens) always remains the same and thus the alterations are in accordance with a common *principio*. Now all things of the world, each of which has its particular |\nnature, are on one another [dependent] and one state dependent on the other; thus no occurrence happens merely in relation to one state, but to all states of the thing, namely to its nature, and at the same time according to the laws of the nature of other connected things. e.g. A human being perishes at sea according to laws of the nature of the water, the winds and the shore, and not merely in relation to his moral constitution. Now it is impossible that the occurrences as individually intended [by] with all things of the world should by chance accord, e.g. the human being's vicious deed and winds, each of which happens according to the order of the nature of the *subiect*; hence they could then not follow from nature and according to one and the same laws.\nOne is accustomed to answer the opinion of some [of the], that God directs his direction not[before] at *individua* but at the universal, by saying that the universal is thought only through separation from the *individuis*. Here it is to be noted that the universal is either the logically universal ([or] the common mark of many) or the *realiter* universal. [That one] e.g. [posits] suppose: a machine is designed for many individual effects [e.g] such as the duck of Vaucanson, then I can say: [its] the provision of the artist is directed at the universal of the animal actions, namely at the logically universal or at everything; but if the arrangement of the machine is so made that it is designed for no individual ones at all, but indeterminately for one ground of all of them, then the preparation is a universal real ground. e.g. thus a barometer is a universal air-gauge, and the feet of a leaper are arranged not for the individual dances, but for walking and dancing in general.",["'gemeinschaftlich' = common (deleted '[communal]' renders the same word); Merkmal = mark.","'ohn' and 'vor' are broken-off deleted words; the source writes 'ei' for 'ein' before 'Barometer'.","Springer = leaper (one who jumps or dances); Zurichtung = preparation."]],"reflm:3882":["It is something different: to concede a particular providence, and to have insight into its marks in a particular case or also to understand the particular rules of the application of the goodness and wisdom of God. He who pretends to recognize a particular divine aim in an occurrence asserts that he has insight into the grounds of the divine decrees through which an occurrence necessarily had to turn out so and not otherwise. But then just this same insight would also have to suffice to predict this occurrence from the known rules of the divine decrees: Just as he has no insight into the causes of eclipses [who them not] insofar as they are determined to a certain point in time, who cannot announce them in advance.",["einsehen / Einsicht = have insight into / insight; Rathschlüsse = decrees.","Finsternisse = eclipses."]],"reflm:3883":["A ground is that through the concept of which (i.e., a universal one) (g not according to the laws of phenomena) something else is posited, and hence by which something else is universally determined; from the grounded (g predicate) to the ground (g subject) the consequence is not valid, except indeterminately or particularly.",["ratio = ground, rationatum = the grounded; conseqventia = consequence."]],"reflm:3884":["The fundamental concepts are not determinations, hence also not the essential pieces.\nThe logical essence is hypothetical or absolute; the former, what expresses the whole (g primitive) concept that we have of the thing, the second, what expresses the possible concept.\nThe real essence signifies the *subiect*, which is unaltered and determinable. Everything that is there has a real essence, which always remains, and the rest are determinations.",["'die wesentlichen Stüke' = the essential pieces (Baumgarten's essentialia).","Wesen = essence; realwesen = real essence."]],"reflm:3885":["from being to being possible the consequence is valid, which is empirical. Being possible, concluded [being possible] through the principle of contradiction, is rationally cognized. The possibility of a real nexus we cognize empirically.\nfrom not being to not being possible the consequence is not valid, but it is nevertheless an imperfect empirical conclusion.",["esse / posse = being / being possible; nexus realis = real nexus.","'qvae est empirica' may refer to the consequence or to the being."]],"reflm:3886":["Whether magnitude is not something that is in itself relative in relation to that which is the greatest and contains the data for everything possible, e.g. the triangle in infinite space. [Since] This is the absolute magnitude, which we cognize by the fact that something can be still greater, and not by the fact that it can be diminished, i.e., contains parts. The latter is the relative magnitude.",["The deleted 'Da' may be 'since' or 'there'.","'Ob nicht ...' is an indirect question without a main clause."]],"reflm:3887":["Perfections simply are [necessary agreements], what harmonizes together toward the inner worth of things. Hence only rational beings can have perfections; e.g. understanding is distinguished from beauty and happiness.\n*in sensu respectivo* [either the perfection of the], where to one nothing is lacking either *in respectu* to something positive in general or *in respectu* to a determinate concept: form of perfection.\n(g the [inner] worth itself is the *bonitaet*, comes down to the matter of perfection.)",["schlechthin = simply; Werth = worth; zusammenstimmen = harmonize together.","'einem' may be 'to one' (a thing) or 'to someone'."]],"reflm:3888":["The [schl] internally necessary is simply necessary (*omnimode*); but we cannot have insight into the inner necessity because of the omission of the determinate conditions. But our concept of necessity also permits that of a ground, and indeed of a universal ground, and this is also understood in the idea of thoroughgoing necessity; but this is only a relational concept in the subordination of ideas and logically conditional, and also serves only to distinguish the *omnimode* necessary from that which is *restrictive tale*.",["'[schl]' is a deleted beginning of 'schlechthin', left as it stands.","'conditions' renders the Latinate 'conditionen'; durchgängig = thoroughgoing."]],"reflm:3889":["*Realitas in sensu substantivo* is a perfection in the metaphysical sense.\nThe highest reality consists not in everything being in it, but through it as a ground; for the *maximum* of reality is not possible synthetically or through *coordination*, but lesser degrees are possible only through limitation of the greatest. Now the highest reality is that which cannot be limited; thus that which is the measure of all things and wherein the reality of all things lies is only the sequel of the *ente summo*.",["Einschränkung / eingeschränkt = limitation / limited; Folge = sequel."]],"reflm:3890":["Every universal concept contains that which is common to many things |\n(g in which many are one and the same), and is undetermined with regard to the differences.\n[Through it one can think several things, and it contains many under itself]\nA particular concept is more determined, namely with regard to the differences. (g Of the thoroughly determined.)\nIt contains [many] the particular ones under itself and is *latior*; the particular ones are *conceptus angustiores*. It is called *superior* with regard to the particular ones, which are *inferiores*.\nIt [holds less in itself] is contained in the lower ones and contains less in itself.\nlogical *subordination*. *Conceptus summus* (g *minime determinatus*), *infimus*. *maxime determinatus* (g *singularis*). *Sphaera conceptus*. *Logica divisio*.\n*Progressus per synthesin, regressus per analysin*.\nIn the series of logical *subordination* there is a member which has higher ones over itself and lower ones in itself: *conceptus communis* [*subord:*] (s *subalternus*); if it is considered only in its *superiori*, then it is represented *in abstracto*; in its *inferiori*: *in concreto*; *maxime in abstracto* or *in individuo*.\n*conceptus communis* [*inferior*] (s *subalternus*) is with regard to its *inferioris* *genus*, with regard to its *superioris* *species*. A *genus* which with regard to its *superioris* is *species* is *subalternum*; one which is not: *summum*. A *species* which contains only *individua* under itself, *species infima*. *Continuitas formarum logicarum*. Between two *conceptibus* logically subordinated to one another there is always a middle one.\n*Subordinatio realis*. *Omnitudo realis*. In place of the determinations of the universal concept, limitation of the *sphaera* of the highest ground, *negationes*. In logical divisions one limits the *sphaeram*, in real determinations one limits the reality. The *conceptus* (g *logice*) *inferior* contains fewer determinations, the *realiter inferior* fewer *positiones* in itself, until *conceptus infimus*, |\n*realitas evanescens* is or *negatio pura*. Nevertheless the *subordinatio realis* is also only according to possibility and thus *Metaphysica*. from which the *caussalis* in existence is called *physica*.\n*Omnitudo realis* [signif] is opposed to the *particulari negationi*.",["einerley: one and the same; Bestimmungen: determinations; Einschränkung, einschränken: limitation, limit.","realitaet (Latinate spelling) rendered reality; [bedeut] is a deleted beginning of bedeutet, signifies.","von der die caussalis im Daseyn physica heißt: von der may mean from which, or as distinguished from which."]],"reflm:3891":["The origin is an instant upon which the whole existence follows; the passing away (end) the instant which follows upon the whole existence. Thereby it is shown first that the origin is an existence, second a *terminus* of duration.\nA whole arises out of something if the existence of the parts precedes the form, and out of nothing if nothing of the parts is prior to the whole.",["Ursprung: origin; Vergehen: passing away; Augenblik: instant; entspringen: arise."]],"reflm:3892":["Whether there is a *spatium absolutum* or *tempus absolutum* would mean as much as whether between two things in space one could annihilate everything [other] lying between and yet the determinate empty gap would remain, and whether, if [between the beginning] for a whole year motions and alterations in general ceased, the following could not begin, so that an empty intervening year would have elapsed. We do not resolve these difficulties, but answer our opponents by *retorsion*, because their conception has precisely these difficulties.\nThe ground or principle of a state is time, and indeed of the conjunction of all things which belong to the same state.",["Veränderungen: alterations; Auffassung: conception (also apprehension).","retorsion: Kant's Latinate term (retorsio), turning an argument back on the opponent.","Latin last sentence: ratio = ground, principium = principle, coniunctio = conjunction."]],"reflm:3893":["It is absolutely impossible that a thing be in many places at the same time, but not that it be in them one after another.\nIt is only conditionally [necessary] impossible that many substances be in one and the same place, namely those which are impenetrable.",["schlechthin: absolutely; bedingt: conditionally; einerley Orte: one and the same place."]],"reflm:3894":["Cosmology is either empirical (physics and psychology) or rational.\nThis considers either the material, i.e. [subject] the constitutive things, (matter and the immaterial), or the form. The latter is treated here.\n[Empirical] Rational cosmology belongs to metaphysics.",["Latin: materiale (singular) and constitutiva (neuter plural) stand in apposition; [subiect] is a deleted beginning of a word, perhaps subiectiva.","Haec, this, may refer to cosmology or to rational cosmology."]],"reflm:3895":["Whether the *ens realissimum* is at the same time *absolute perfectum* (g *summum bonum*), is the question; for *respectiv sive externe perfectissimum* it is certainly. The *interna perfectio* (*bonitas*) presupposes the feeling of pleasure.",["den read as denn, for.","respectiv: German-Latin form of respective, relatively."]],"reflm:3896":["Nothing is either in the logical sense opposed to something; in the metaphysical sense it is opposed to the thinkable. The former is nothing relatively such (none of those things which are contained under some notion). The latter is the opposite of those things which are contained under any notion at all.",["Latin: the first vel has no second vel corresponding to it; the second member follows without it.","notio rendered notion; cogitabile: the thinkable."]],"reflm:3897":["*ratio* is either among coordinates (s *ratio completa*) or subordinate sufficient.\nWith the former one no longer asks the »why« of the sequel, with the latter of the ground.\n( s The latter stands under no higher ground, the former is no part of a greater ground. The *terminus* of the subordinate grounds is *ratio primitiva*. The *terminus* of the coordinate grounds is *completudo*. )\n(s The whole of the series or of the ground.)",["ratio: ground; Folge: sequel (the consequence of a ground).","subordinata sufficiens: whether sufficiens qualifies ratio or subordinata is left open, as in the Latin."]],"reflm:3898":["In complex concepts the consequence *a posse ad esse* is not valid, but in simple ones.\nFrom the form of possibility one cannot infer to actuality, but indeed from the matter.",["conseqventia: consequence, the inference from being possible to being; schließen: infer; Wirklichkeit: actuality."]],"reflm:3899":["All unity is either of comparison or of connection. The first is, insofar as something is one and the same with much else. The second, insofar as many are combined in one ground.\nThe unity of taking together (*addition*): is mathematical, or of derivation: this is philosophical.",["Verknüpfung: connection; verbunden: combined; Zusammennehmung: taking together; Ableitung: derivation."]],"reflm:3900":["Everything is necessary. either conditionally (g i.e. through a ground) or unconditionally.\n[Everything] Among all grounds there must be a first ground, this is absolutely necessary.",["The period after nothwendig and the lower-case entweder are kept; the two may be read as one sentence.","bedingt / unbedingt: conditionally / unconditionally; schlechthin: absolutely."]],"reflm:3901":["The *synthesis* of reality consists merely in the separation from the |\nsingle greatest through negations, because negations presuppose reality; consequently therein lies the difference of the [gan] composite and simple.",["einigen read as einzigen, single; Absonderung: separation.","[gan] is a deleted beginning of gantz, whole, left untranslated."]],"reflm:3902":["Besides substance and *accidente* one can think yet something positive, namely the *respectum*. All *respectus* (g of these) is threefold: that of substance (empirical): space, that of the *accidentium*: time, that of substance to the *accidentibus*: force.",["respectus: relation; Kant keeps the Latin case forms (accidente, respectum, accidentium, accidentibus).","Kraft: force."]],"reflm:3903":["Substance alone is a thing or [something] object.",["Gegenstand: object; Ding: thing."]],"reflm:3904":["A state is the coexistence of the substantial with the accidents.\nThe substantial is immutable; for since it contains the first ground of the accidents and hence of the alterations, if it were altered, this alteration would be an accident, not the substantial. But the first ground is immutable for this reason, because, since there is no other ground of the alteration, it would alter itself, i.e. a and not a would be determined at the same time in the same being.",["Latin: status = state, ratio = ground, mutatio = alteration, ens = being."]],"reflm:3905":["On M 369:\nThe whole of the world must not be confused |\nwith the whole state of the same. For the accidents are not parts of the world. Consequently I can say: the world arises little by little, in that one part after the other is created.",["ZuM 369: on § 369 of the Metaphysica (M is the AA's siglum for it).","Das ganze der Welt: the whole of the world; Zustand: state; entspringt: arises."]],"reflm:3906":["( s Necessity and contingency in the world. Nature (s with ground) and freedom and the perfection of both: happiness and morality. Blind necessity, blind contingency. Hazard. Supreme *principium* of the rationally necessary, natural necessity [of the rationally contingent] and freedom; both are contained in the supreme being. )( s Nature and freedom fate and chance. { Connection. *saltus*. Order. *)\n*( (g this concerns occurrences, consequently what happens in the world; for the origin of the same is no occurrence.) )\nAll occurrences of the world happen either through blind necessity:\n*bruta necessitas*;\n*Fatum* or through fate — destiny — decree of the supreme cause;\n*per libertatem* or through freedom of the acting beings in the world;\n*casus* or not through fate or freedom;\n*fortuitum* or from no cause at all.\nBlind necessity (s — Rational)\nPredetermined. Destiny.\nThrough freedom (s Contingency.)\nwithout will — — — chance\nblind hazard. (s Natural chance.)\nNothing happens through a leap, chance and fate.\n( s In the series of everything contingent the first is the free action. In the series of everything necessary the first the absolute necessity. Boundaries. )\n(s quality of the world; the world does not consist of a void.)\n(s Every transition is through all possible intermediate members.)\n(s The in itself contingent, which nevertheless necessarily [is] happens without ground, is blind necessity.\nThe in itself dependent, which there [is] happens without any[ground] necessity, is blind chance.)\n(s The necessity in the world without any ground is the blind one.\nThe contingency in the world without any ground is the blind one.)",["Zufall: chance; Ohngefehr: hazard; Zufälligkeit: contingency; Schicksal: fate; Verhängniß: destiny; Rathschlus: decree.","Begebenheiten: occurrences; Grentzen: boundaries. The *) and *( are Kant's reference marks, kept.","Latin lines translated: Nihil per saltum, casum et fatum accidit; qvalitas mundi; mundus non constat vacuo."]],"reflm:3907":["( s (g negative: One does not speak of the divine reason.) Understanding. No sensibility. (*non affectus*.) intuitive, not discursive: goes to the possible and the actual equally strongly (g sees everything in itself). No difficulty on account of the future time. (g *Perfectiones qviescentes et operativae*.) [Will] Pleasure and displeasure From itself, blessedness. Will. Freedom. Independence from impulses (g *analogon* of motive); yet no *absolutum decretum*, but conformable to the goodness of the object. Moral motive: justice. Benevolent aim. )\nAll great properties which I state of God from the arbitrary idea of him are only expositions of the hypothesis which I assume. But those which I draw from the work (these, however, are only practically perfect) apply to a real being, and one given through actual documents.\n1. Existence [of God] of the original being: against the atheists (s dogmatic and skeptical). Existence [of God] of the supreme intelligence: against the deists.\n(s real condition of all possibility. Necessity. immutability. impassibility. Immateriality. Infinity.)\n[2. Original against the Epicureans] (Existence of the supreme person: against the fatalists.)\n3. Immutable and impassible: against the anthropomorphists (g that his concept is purely intellectual).\n4. Substantiality: against Spinoza.\n(s Not the sole substance (g Simple): against Spinoza (so that the world would inhere in him as an accident); not the sole substance (g composite) as the complex of all things, i.e. το παν [as if God were an accident inhering in the world]. God is an extramundane being.)\n5. that he is an extramundane being: against the Stoics, because they maintained a soul of the world.\n6. Uniqueness: against the polytheists.\n7. Supreme perfection (s Highest good): against the Manichaeans.\n8. Simplicity. (s Infinity: against the anthropomorphists.)\n9. Eternity. (s 10. Causality with respect to the world.)\n10. Omnipresence. (s Extramundane being (not *anima mundi*): against the Stoics.)\n11. Omnipotence. (s Intelligence: against the deists.)\na. Personality: against the deists through intellectual concepts of the consciousness of oneself, not insofar as we are affected, but act. (s The transcendental, natural and moral theology. God as a holy one.)",["Bewegungsgrund: motive; Antriebe: impulses; Absicht: aim; Willkührlich: arbitrary; Seeligkeit: blessedness.","conform der bonitaet des obiects: Kant's Latinate words (conform, bonitaet, obiect) rendered conformable, goodness, object.","The Latin heads are translated; treffen rendered apply to (literally hit upon)."]],"reflm:3908":["(s a)\na: Against the Manichaeans.\n(s a 2,)\n3. All-sufficiency. As cause of the universe, even of the possibles.\n4. Immutability.\n5. Impassibility (s Spirituality: against the deists, theism, but not anthropomorphism.)\n(s c)\n6. Unity. (s Holiness: against the heathens.)\n(s d)\n7. Simplicity.\n8. Immateriality.\n9. Intelligence. Living God.\n(s a 3. Infinity [ads] internal — relative, all-sufficiency [with respect]. the conditions are 9 and 10.)",["[ads] is a deleted fragment, left untranslated; [respectu] rendered [with respect].","omnisufficientia: all-sufficiency; ethnici: heathens."]],"reflm:3909":["transcendental concept of God.\nGod as the highest being. (s Defense against atheism.)\nmetaphysical Psychological:*\nGod as the highest intelligence. (s Defense against deism. Which properly attacks anthropomorphism.)\nmoral:\nGod as the highest good. (s Defense against Epicureanism.)\n(s He can be no [comp] rightful lawgiver of morals if he is not at the same time that of nature, and indeed in its whole completeness. For if he does not have fate wholly in his power, then he cannot command absolutely. But for that he must be creator; this is necessary to speculation, but not to religion.)\nα. Morality against the Epicureans. (s Moral theology, theological morals.)\n*(s we can cognize God only psychologically, according to the analogy with the forces of nature, which contain the complete causality of perfection. For we have no *data* of God, but only *data* of his relation to the world, thus we cognize in him only s breaks off.)",["Sitten: morals; Moral: morals; erkennen: cognize; Kräfte: forces; Verhältnis: relation.","[comp] is a deleted fragment, left untranslated; theologischeMoral is run together in the source.","s bricht ab: the AA's note that the s addition breaks off."]],"reflm:3910":["I know of God only the condition of his sufficient relation to the world, and indeed this relation is similar to that of the things of the world, because he is the *principium* of the series; but these properties are not similar to the properties of the things of the world. We can quite well ignore that which has no such relation. For it does not belong to us.",["kennen: know; hinlänglich: sufficient (as zureichend); Verhältnis: relation; Eigenschaften: properties."]],"reflm:3911":["God is perfect through his nature, but he is good through his will. The *ideal* of a good will is realized in God or through God.\nThat which is good, its existence has a worth. Perfection contains the *completudinem* of the *datorum* for something, from whose worth we abstract.",["Werth: worth; completudo datorum: completeness of the data, in Kant's Latin case forms."]],"reflm:3912":["The world has a beginning, i.e. a state which is no sequel from another state: *terminum a priori*; *non est aeternus a parte ante*. For if every state of the world were a sequel from another state of the world, then all states of the world would have another state of the world before them; thus a state of the world would be distinct from all states. which *contradictorium*.\nFrom the beginning of the world up to the present time no eternity has elapsed: *mundus non creatus est ab aeterno*.\nThe world could not have been created earlier; but we could well have existed later.\nWe cannot distinctly represent how the causality of a necessary cause begins. For we always think that it was not before. But we must indeed think at the beginning of all things a *relation* of it *a parte post*, but none *a parte ante*, thus no relation of the world to a previously elapsed time.",["Folge: sequel; Zustand: state; unterschieden: distinct.","relation (Kant's Latinate word) and Verhaltnis are both rendered relation.","es in daß es vorher nicht gewesen sey may refer to the causality or be impersonal."]],"reflm:3913":["[*consensus vel opposit*]\nAll combination is either one of combination or of comparison. The former is either of agreement or of conflict.\nThat of combination is either analytic (of the part with the whole and thus also with itself) and is logical; or synthetic: of the parts among one another, of the [parts] combined unities among one another.",["Verbindung: combination, repeated as in the source; Einstimmung: agreement; Wiederstreit: conflict.","The deleted Latin heading [consensus vel opposit] breaks off and is left in Latin."]],"reflm:3914":["There is nothing in the [human soul] metaphysics empirical except what concerns the universal laws of thinking in the human soul.\nHence also the synthetic principles hold not as rational but as subjectively universal; and it is not possible that the understanding should begin from the concepts and merely compare the concepts analytically, if some rules of *synthesis* do not lie at the ground.",["'principles': Grundsätze.","'hold ... as subjectively universal': subiectiv allgemein gelten; 'subjectively' may qualify 'universal' or the verb 'hold'."]],"reflm:3915":["[The relation of magnitudes is the ground of an ideal combination, in that I from a]\nThere are comparisons which are not connections, e.g. of identity, diversity. These are ideal connections or conflicts.\nComparisons of magnitude.",["'combination': Verbindung; 'connections': Verknüpfungen; 'conflicts': wiederstreite (Widerstreit).","'identity, diversity': identitaet, diversitaet, Latinate forms in the source.","The deleted opening sentence breaks off after 'aus ein'."]],"reflm:3916":["The comparison of concepts according to the law of the imagination is not wholly to be rejected. Things that are always encountered combined with one another give presumption that they are combined according to the law of the understanding. Hence the inference from induction. According to the law of wit the inference from analogy.",["'presumption': vermuthung (also 'conjecture').","'wit': Witz; 'inference': Schluß."]],"reflm:3917":["(s All cognition has two *termini*, *a priori et a posteriori*; hence *principia prima a priori* or *a posteriori*. [If ab] The *principia absolute prima a priori* are all indemonstrable; thus metaphysics contains only *principia comparative prima*.)\n(s All pure ideas of reason are ideas of *reflexion* (*discursivae* and not *intuitus*, as Plato asserted). Hence through them objects are also not represented, but only laws for comparing the concepts which have been given to us through senses.)\nAll cognition has grounds and among these the first. The first are either those that naturally proceed in the arising, or those [from which] in which it finally lets itself be resolved and out of which again be composed: *a priori* or *posteriori*. Grammatical: empirical, rational.\n(s empirical or intellectual principles)\nThe first grounds from behind of cognition are experiences.\nThe science of arriving at the supreme grounds *a priori* is metaphysics. This science is resolving. Fundamental concepts, fundamental judgments.\nMethod of nature.\nConditioned indispensability of this science in higher judgments of reason.\nHistory. Names.\nOrder of the disciplines.\n( g Grounds of one's own insights\nothers' [Ins] testimonies Proverbs )",["'it ... lets itself be resolved': sie ... sich auflösen ... lassen; 'sie' may refer to the cognition or to the grounds.","'Grammatical': Grammatic, perhaps 'grammar'; the sense of the abbreviation is uncertain.","'resolving': auflösend; 'judgments of reason': Vernunfturtheilen."]],"reflm:3918":["The *territorium* of metaphysics. Parts.\nThe function of it determines its worth. Is not the satisfac|tion\nof the desires for knowledge, but to determine the boundaries, partly positively, partly negatively.\nIt is a science of the ends of our cognition. Comparison with mathematics.\n[history. Aristotle.]",["'desires for knowledge': Wisbegierden (plural).","'boundaries': Grenzen; 'ends': Zweke."]],"reflm:3919":["(s The understanding connects concepts because one includes the other; the *imagination*, because one accompanies the other; wit, because one is similar to the other. With negative judgments it is just the same. *e.g.* Because we combine contact with the immediate effect, we deny attraction.)\nThe law of the understanding is not brought into exercise otherwise than with a great intermixture with the law of the *imagination*.\nIt is not enough to have grounds for a cognition; one must also slowly accustom oneself to it. (Friction). It is not enough to cancel grounds; one must also for a time resist habit, gradually to hold back the same former judgment even without grounds.",["'connects': verknüpft; 'combine': verbinden; 'cancel': aufheben.","The final infinitive clause may state the purpose of resisting habit or what the habit does; the German leaves it open."]],"reflm:3920":["With all judgments of the understanding the case is as follows. (If (g any) something x [which] through a representation a cognized [is, is compared with another concept b, either that it the representation b] can be, then a is a mark of something x, [but] the cognition, however, of x through a is a concept. Thus extension, motion, ignorance etc etc. is a mark of something x). If any something x, which is cognized through a representation a, is compared with another concept (g b), either that it include this or exclude it, |\nthen this relation is in the judgment. This judgment is (g thus) either the cognition of agreement or of conflict, so that in the [concept of] thing x, which I know through the concept a, either b is contained as a partial concept and thus x [also], which is cognized through a, can also be cognized through b, or that [through] x cancels the concept of b.\nIn all judgments the matter and the form are to be considered. That the first are the concepts of the subject (y+a)\n= x. and of the predicate b. secondly the form, which among the logicians is called the concept of combination (*copula*). (One can represent every concept in relation with all others; those that it includes stand toward it in the relation of affirmation, those that it excludes, in negation.) x is called the possible concept of a thing, a the representation through which I think it. All form of judgments is either affirmation or negation. The first represents the relation where the concept of the thing y+a includes the mark b and thus is identical with it as to its part. Negation consists in this, that the concept of the thing y+a is the opposite of the mark b, and in the representation of the cancellation consists negation.",["The German word order of the first conditional is kept so that the deletion stands where it does; the final text reads 'if something x can be cognized through a representation a'.","'That the first': Daß erstere, as printed, perhaps for 'Das erstere' ('the first').","'know': kenne; 'partial concept': Theilbegriff; 'concept of combination': Verbindungsbegriff; 'cancels', 'cancellation': aufhebt, Aufhebung."]],"reflm:3921":["The predicate is no part(g concept) of the subject, but a representation of the [whole] subject through a partial concept. The understanding cognizes something always through a clear or obscure judgment, in that it resolves something into its predicates. All [signs] our concepts are marks drawn from sensation. Sensation itself is no object of the understanding, but its marks; hence e.g. the concept of the human being is nothing other than the representation of something which has the predicates into which we can resolve the concept of a human being. Hence in every judgment the subject is in general something =x, which, cognized under the mark a, is compared with another mark. Hence it is also no wonder that we cognize no subject |\nprior to all predicates except merely the I, which nevertheless is no concept but an [sensation] intuition. Hence we cognize through the understanding in bodies not the real subjects, but the predicates of extension, solidity, rest, motion etc.. The cause is: through our senses only the relations of things can reveal themselves, and we can represent the absolute or subject only from ourselves. The idea of substance comes properly from the *repraesentatione sui ipsius*, insofar as we represent that something is distinct from us, and predicates cannot be thought without subject and without ultimate subject, [in addition c] the constant predicates are then together called the subject.\nThrough a predicate I do not represent to myself a part of the thing or have a concept of the part, but represent to myself the object itself and have of it a partial concept, hence also designation through mathematical signs is impossible. Let y+s be the thing itself which is represented under the concept s, and its predicate p. then y+s–p =0 would be, consequently y+s=p.",["'object': obiect (Latinate) in both places.","'thing': Ding, and in the last paragraph also sache ('a part of the thing').","'real subjects': eigentliche subiecten (also 'proper subjects'); 'constant': bestandigen."]],"reflm:3922":["M III:\nMaterial principles seem to be: what [arises] happens must have a ground. Every successive series has a beginning*. Such propositions implicate themselves; for because the beginning is an arising [is] or happening, there would have to be, to that extent, again a ground of it. The idea of freedom indicates an arising without a preceding determining ground. The nature of our understanding brings it with it that according to its rule nothing (g contingent) is thinkable without connection with grounds, and that a sequence (according to time) without ground and a happening of something without in connection with its ground cannot be thought, because then the understanding would be entirely unusable.\nM IV:\n*(g and every series of subordinated things has a first. †)\n†(s It is difficult to think in general, in the series of all things, a beginning which everything else succeeds, as creation. Just so it is impossible to think an elapsed infinite series. To use the idea All of an infinite series seems to be false, and yet on this is grounded the necessity of the *causa prima*, because otherwise everything would be *causatum*.)\nM III:\nAnother synthetic principle is: what thinks is only a simple subject. Every thing must (not *absolute*, but *respective* to another) be at some time, either simultaneously or one after another.\nIt does not follow that what must be judged according to the laws of our understanding is true, if it concerns such things about which our understanding is not determined, according to the arrangement of its use, to judge.\n[There would be no philosophy] We borrow from bodily appearances the law of sufficient ground; but if we want to make it universal [then] and apply it to things that are elevated above the idea of our understanding, then we mix the idea of the absolute unintelligibility for us with inner impossibility.",["'sequence': Folge; 'arising': entstehen; 'happening': geschehen.","'without in connection with its ground': ohne in Verknüpfung mit seinem Grunde, as the German has it.","'unintelligibility': Unverstandlichkeit (from verstehen)."]],"reflm:3923":["Some principles are analytic and concern the formal element of distinctness in our cognition. Some are synthetic and concern the material, such as the arithmetical, geometrical and chronological ones. likewise the empirical ones. But there are still principles which concern the use of reason in *synthesi* in general. The nature of our reason, however, has this law, that it cognizes things not immediately but mediately; hence it can expect everything that happens only according to a ground, and what is determined by no other ground is irrational to it.\nMatter is lifeless, is a rational and not an empirical judgment, [it] because one also [assumes, but] perceives much life in matter, but distinguishes this from the material properties. On the other hand: matter has attractive forces, is empirical.",["'the formal element', 'the material': das formale, das materiale, substantival adjectives in the German.","'perceives': warnimt; 'assumes' in the deletion: annimt."]],"reflm:3924":["From the imperfect idea of substance it follows that one can with difficulty represent that which is and acts merely through alien force, with difficulty, as a distinct substance. Spinozism.",["'with difficulty' is doubled in the German (schweerlich ... schwerlich).","'alien force': fremde Kraft (also 'another's force')."]],"reflm:3925":["The *synthesis* is either one after another,[or] i.e. of the subordinated, or next to one another: of the coordinated. from the latter arise the judgments of the whole and its parts.",[]],"reflm:3926":["How do empirical and synthetic judgments become universal.\nDo we not perhaps have, besides the *principiis formalibus* of rational propositions, also *formalia* of the synthetic and empirical. Likewise does one not have just so *principia formalia* of real connection as of the logical.",["'propositions': sätze; 'real connection': realverknüpfung."]],"reflm:3927":["Of the empirical fundamental concepts through abstraction there are innumerable ones, which cannot be further resolved into their elements, although one can indeed show the determination in their cause. The rational fundamental concepts, in which solely and alone the sensed properties of things can be explained, are, in the case of outer objects: space, time, motion. In the case of the [out] inner: A 1. immediate representation of the present, of the past, of the future. 2, comparison, distinction and sameness. 3. relation (logical) of connection and of conflict. 4. consciousness, judgments, inferences. B 1. feeling. pleasure, displeasure. 2. in relation to the judgment of the understanding or of the senses. C. desire etc etc. [Fundamental concepts which are common to both sensations] Through the nature of the understanding, not *abstrahendo* but *iudicando*, fundamental concepts of *synthesis* arise. Existence, possibility, unity, substance, *accidens*, relation, [the lo] *respectus realis*, *logicus*, necessary, contingent. Whole, a part. Simple, composite, ground, sequel, force, cause.",["'fundamental concepts': Grundbegriffe; 'existence': Daseyn; 'sameness': einerleyheit.","'sequel': Folge; 'relation': Verhältnis in the list A 3, relation in the list of concepts of synthesis."]],"reflm:3928":["The *principia* of the form of all analytic judgments (g of the existential judgments of things *absolute* or of the predicates *relative*) are the proposition of identity and of contradiction, and [the subjective] the proofs are conducted in that through the *analysin* of the given concept the identity or opposition with the predicate is shown. The *principia* of the form of synthetic judgments are: whatever is at all times combined with [the] a known part of the possible concept of a thing belongs along with it, as a part, to this concept etc etc. The proposition: every body is impenetrable, is analytic, because [itself the b] not only can the body not be thought without impenetrability, but because it can be thought solely through impenetrability; this mark belongs as *pars* to the notion of the body. But: every body is inert, is a synthetic proposition; for inertia is *compars* with the concept [of the b] of that which is thought under the expression body, to a whole concept, which is necessarily combined with those partial concepts that belong in the notion of the body. If one had the whole concept of which the notions of the subject and predicate are *compartes*, then the synthetic judgments would transform themselves into analytic ones. The question is how far there is something arbitrary here.\nSo much for the *nexu logico*. As concerns the *nexum realem*, its *principia materialia* are the experiences, the formal *principia* are: everything that happens has a determining ground, and secondly: everything has a first ground. These *principia* are both synthetic, the former for the use of our reason, the latter for the *termino* of this use. For according to the former we always look, in the series of mutually determining causes, to the higher grounds, and according to the latter we admit that this series is bounded. But it is just as impossible to represent to oneself a series of subordinated grounds that has no |\nbeginning, as to conceive how it begins. Meanwhile the proposition: everything that happens has a determining ground, this proposition, which makes an infinite series necessary, is the *principium* of the form of all our judgments of reason about real connection. But the proposition: every series of subordinated things and every successive series has a first, is a synthetic proposition, which is abstracted more from the bounds of our understanding than from the object of cognition.\nThe first in the series of causes is always free choice. That this has no determining ground is an empirical proposition, but to that extent uncertain; but its certainty depends on this, that otherwise there could be no first at all.\nOf the possibility of alteration, i.e. of coming to be and ceasing in general.",["Satz der identitaet und des wiederspruchs: singular Satz with two genitives, rendered 'the proposition of identity and of contradiction'.","Struck '[sich der K]' and '[des K]' are cut off; 'K' presumably Körper, rendered 'b(ody)'.","Latin case forms (analysin, nexu logico, nexum realem, termino) are kept as Kant wrote them; Willkühr is rendered 'choice', Veränderung 'alteration'."]],"reflm:3929":["Properly the representation of all things is the representation of our own state and the relation of one representation to the other according to our inner laws. The impossibility of separating concepts, or the combination of them without any laws of our understanding, are merely subjective; likewise also the possibility. We cognize the possibility of free actions only empirically; in a rational sense it conflicts with the laws of using our understanding. *Analysis sine termino* [is] makes the *synthesin* impossible. Hence according to the laws in the use of our understanding we posit a ground in everything that happens, but [according to] precisely for that reason the synthesis is impossible (this very thing applied to the *analysin* of the body *sine termino*, where no synthesis is impossible). Of the first relations according to laws of our understanding no further ground can be given. (g The *analysis completa* led [in all actions] from the many to unity, *in serie successivorum* to the *principium*, *in simultaneis* to the *monas*.)\nThe *axiomata* (*synthetica*) of philosophy pertain solely to the relation which can be cognized only subjectively according to laws of our understanding.",["'wo keine synthesis unmoglich ist' (where no synthesis is impossible) is rendered as it stands; the double negative may be a slip.","Verhältnis is rendered 'relation' throughout; 'relation' in the first sentence is Kant's own Latinate word.","'die Verbindung derselben ohne alle Gesetze': 'ohne alle' may attach to Verbindung or to the whole clause; left ambiguous."]],"reflm:3930":["Some concepts are abstracted from the sensations, others merely from the law of the understanding to compare, [and] to combine or to separate the abstracted concepts. The origin of the latter is in the understanding, of the former in the senses. All concepts of such a kind are called pure concepts of the understanding, *conceptus intellectus puri*. To be sure, we can set these activities of the understanding in motion only on the occasion of the sensible sensations, and become conscious of certain concepts of the universal relations of abstracted ideas according to laws of the understanding; and so here too Locke's rule holds, that without sensible sensation no idea becomes clear in us; but the *notiones rationales* arise, to be sure, by means of the sensations, and can also be thought only in application to the ideas abstracted from them, but they do not lie in them and are not abstracted from them. Just as in [mathem] geometry we do not borrow the idea of space from the sensation of extended beings, although we can make [it cl] this concept clear only on the occasion of the [bodily] sensation of bodily things. Hence the idea of space is *notio intellectus puri*, which can be applied to the abstracted idea of mountains and of barrels.\nThe [scienc] philosophy about [all that] the concepts of the *intellectus puri* is metaphysics. it is related to the rest of philosophy as the *mathesis pura* to the *mathesis applicata*. The concepts of existence (g reality), of possibility, of necessity, of ground, of unity and plurality, of the whole and the parts (g all, none), of the composite and simple, of space, of time, of alteration, (g of motion,) of substance and of accident, of force and of action, and everything that belongs to ontology proper, is in relation to the rest of metaphysics as the (g universal) arithmetic in the *mathesi pura*.",["Struck fragments '[Mathem]', '[ihn gl]', '[Wissensch]' are cut off; rendered '[mathem]', '[it cl]', '[scienc]'.","Daseyn is rendered 'existence', Vielheit 'plurality', Veränderung 'alteration'.","The final verb 'ist' is singular after a plural list of subjects, kept as 'is'."]],"reflm:3931":["The first *Principia* of empirical philosophy are propositions of experience which are universal, from which one descends to the lower ones. Those of the rational are pure grounds of reason. In empirical philosophy one proceeds analytically, in the rational synthetically.\nWe can give grounds of synthetic principles *in sensu subiectivo*, but not *in sensu obiectivo*. Only of abstracted concepts can we give grounds through the *analysin obiectivam*, but of rational ones only through *analysin subiectivam*. Or rather: of rational propositions we can give grounds only through *Analysin subiectivam*. *e.g.* Of the proposition that a being exists necessarily, namely because our thoughts of a possibility without any actuality are [contradictory] null, inasmuch as all possibility must be borrowed from something actual.\nMetaphysics proper or pure philosophy consists of ontology.",["Grundsatz is rendered 'principle', Satz 'proposition', Wirklichkeit 'actuality'.","'nichtig' rendered 'null' (void, empty)."]],"reflm:3932":["The ideas and rules of reason are also used in the relation of empirical concepts, and this is their natural and correct use; but they are then grounded on *iudicia empirica primitiva*, which are universal only through induction. but precisely these judgments of reason, insofar as they are pure, are supposed to be universal in themselves. The *philosophia pura* is not, like the *mathesis pura*, certain in itself.",[]],"reflm:3933":["In all identity of concepts [which not] two concepts agree in one, i.e. [one concept is common in both. In every judgment] one [mark] concept belongs to two; in every judgment two concepts belong to one thing. The thing that I think through the concept A, the very same |\nI think also through the concept B. is a judgment (g of connection). The concept that I think in A, that I think also in B: is a judgment of comparison.\nIn identity I compare two subjects which have one and the same predicate.\nIn unity I compare two predicates or concepts which have one subject.",["Struck '[die nich]' and '[Merkm]' are cut off; rendered '[which not]' and '[mark]'.","Verknüpfung is rendered 'connection', Vergleichung 'comparison'; einerley 'one and the same'."]],"reflm:3934":["To the unthinkability of divine properties belongs not only his presence in all places of space at once, but also in all points of time at once. i.e. that he can represent an eternity to himself wholly.",["Undenklichkeit rendered 'unthinkability'; it could also mean what is beyond (our) thinking, inconceivability.","'gantz' may attach to 'eine Ewigkeit' (a whole eternity) or to the act of representing (wholly)."]],"reflm:3935":["The proposition: all occurrences have sequels (each one), and all together in turn have sequels, are not one and the same.\nAll rational synthetic propositions are subjective, and conversely only the analytic ones are objective. *principia identitatis et contradictionis*.\nThe synthesis of reason (g rational) or of experience (g empirical).\nThe first is either of coordination: whole and parts, number and unity, or of subordination: ground and sequel.\n[Empirical] The second of coordination according to space and time.",["Singular 'Der Satz' with plural 'sind' is kept: 'The proposition ... are'.","Begebnisse rendered 'occurrences', Folge 'sequel'."]],"reflm:3936":["Everything is considered either *distributive* or *collective*. The former indicates a universal concept insofar as it is a common mark of the lower ones; the second indicates the taking together of |\nmuch. Now since the collection of everything cannot always happen, the *omnitudo collectiva* is sometimes imagined.\nThe *principia subiectiva*, when they are weighed objectively, contradict each other. E.g. Everything has a ground, contradicts this proposition: nothing is necessary through itself etc etc.",["Zusammennehmung rendered 'taking together'; 'vielem' may be 'much' or 'many'.","eingebildet: 'imagined' or 'imaginary'."]],"reflm:3937":["One is supposed to think something absolutely necessary, because everything that is, is necessary, but everything cannot be hypothetically necessary. But one can think nothing absolutely necessary. One must think the world as bounded, but one can also not think the bounds.\nThose synthetic propositions which by their nature do not [through] cancel the same conditions of comprehensibility which they posit hold objectively, as of the *commercio substantiarum*.",["schlechthin rendered 'absolutely'; aufheben 'cancel'; Begreiflichkeit 'comprehensibility'.","'als vom commercio substantiarum' may mean 'as that (proposition) of' or 'for example of'; left ambiguous."]],"reflm:3938":["Besides those determinations without which objects cannot be, there are in our reason also conditions without which we cannot think certain objects through reason, even though these conditions are not determinations of the objects themselves. These *conditiones* are therefore subjective, and their concepts signify nothing in the object. All synthetic judgments of pure reason are accordingly subjective, and the concepts themselves signify relations of the actions of reason to themselves.",["'objects' renders obiecte in the first clause and Gegenstände thereafter.","'zu sich selbst' may refer to the actions of reason or to reason itself."]],"reflm:3939":["Logic treats the objective laws of reason, that is, how it ought to proceed. Metaphysics the subjective ones of pure reason, how it proceeds. In both, reason is the object, for the (g rational) form is in every science of philosophy and mathematics. That reason, according to its laws, wants to treat the laws according to which it ought to think, brings it about that it makes use of the rules which it first wants to discover. Hence come the tautologies and rules that are without practice.",["'object' renders Obiect, not Gegenstand.","'Praxis' rendered 'practice'."]],"reflm:3940":["In all cognition of reason only relations are to be considered, and these are either given (sensibly or through the nature of reason) or invented. But we can invent no relations (g of) whose possibility we can be convinced, except as to magnitude, through repetition in the science of number. To invent with regard to the quality of relations, and thus to invent (g first) concepts of reason, is just as impossible as first sensations of the senses.",["dichten / gedichtet rendered 'invent' / 'invented' (also: fabricate, feign).","The added '(g von)' leaves the relative clause grammatically unsettled, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3941":["Metaphysical concepts pertain 1. merely to the relation of coordination: (g *absolutum et relativum*, whole, part, *continuum*, *discretum*) (g many) some, all (g the first, last, a single one); 2. or of subordination in the logical sense: universal or particular; 3. to subordination in the real sense: ground, sequel, cause, effect. From this arises the concept of the first cause, of the last sequel, of the cause of everything, of some. 4. to existence: necessary, contingent (g possible); 5. substance. (g subject, predicate) simple, composite, *actio*, *passio* (g *vis*, *receptivitas*), *spontanea*, [*coacta*] *iners*. (g whole of substances. world.)\nTime and space.\n| Duration, moment, eternity, beginning, end. Alteration, persistence. | Place, extension, point. | (g Space and time:) Motion, rest. Omnipresence.\nEffect in space: filling, force, mass.\nEffect in time: alteration, creation, annihilation.",["Erfüllung rendered 'filling' (of space); Beharrlichkeit 'persistence'.","Verstande in 'im logischen / realverstande' rendered 'sense'."]],"reflm:3942":["True is a cognition which agrees with the constitution of the object. Since through the idea of space the representation of outer objects is only possible, all axioms of space, and what is derived from them, agree with the object, likewise all relations of concepts according to the rule of identity. For the ideas then agree among one another. But since the metaphysical concepts of ground, substance etc. are not properly representations of objects, inasmuch as even the most perfect sense cannot sense these in any thing, and without these relations things altogether, although not through our reason, can be represented: so these concepts are not objective; thus in their axioms everything is subjective. Hence, if they are falsely regarded (g as) objective, neither truth nor falsehood holds of them. In general, if one wants to find the concept of cause, then apart from the relations of connection according to ideas of time one will find no definition that would not include a circle, and it seems nothing else.\nThe surest proof that they are not objective is that they stand in evident contradiction.",["'nur Möglich' may mean 'possible only through the idea of space' or 'merely possible'; left ambiguous.","erklärung rendered 'definition' (also: explanation).","'und sie scheint nichts anderes' is elliptical and possibly incomplete; rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:3943":["(g Metaphysics is the ground of the form of all philosophy.)\nThe utility of metaphysics [first in th] can best be seen in the actual (g natural) use of it.\nThis utility is positive, to increase insights;\nnegative, to prevent false metaphysics;\nin both respects the insight is objective, through which one seeks to acquire the various objects of cognition, or subjective, where one seeks to know the limitations of cognition.\nIt is noble. The subjective can be certain, although the objective is uncertain.",["Struck '[erstlich in de]' is cut off; rendered '[first in th]'.","Schranken rendered 'limitations'; kennen 'know'."]],"reflm:3944":["The logical form of our cognitions is to be distinguished from the metaphysical; the first is analysis, the second synthesis.",[]],"reflm:3945":["About all objects of outer and inner senses we judge rationally, in that we [the laws of the] through universal laws of appearances indicate the ground of them. The first possible laws of appearances cannot be established through experience, but rather the first grounds of them rest on pure reason; *physica pura et pnevmatologia*.",["The deletion \"[die Gesetze der]\" leaves the sentence as Kant left it; \"Grund\" (ground) stands without an article."]],"reflm:3946":["M XXXXIII:\nAll pure philosophy is either logical or metaphysical. The former [judges] contains only the subordination of concepts under the *sphaeram* of the others, either immediately: in judgments, or mediately: in inferences. [The latter] It leaves, however, the concepts themselves, which can be subordinated to one another, undetermined and does not settle which predicates belong to things according to laws of pure reason. Hence to cognize the first predicates of things through pure reason is a matter of metaphysics, consequently to find the first fundamental concepts with which we judge through pure reason, and the principles.\nM XXXXIV:\n(s Metaphysics goes to cognitions solely through reason, logic to all, even empirical ones. All concepts of reason are universal; logic shows only the relation of the universal to the particular in general. Metaphysics, however, the origin of universal concepts, to which all cognition must be traced back if the appearances are to be transformed into concepts.)\nM XXXXIII:\nMetaphysics is thus a science of the fundamental concepts and principles of human reason, and not of human cognition in general, in which there is much that is empirical and sensible; logic a science of the relations of universal concepts and propositions in general. Logic borrows concepts and propositions from metaphysics or from some empirical cognition or other and teaches how to make use of them.\nLogic contains the concept of the universal, metaphysics universal concepts of reason (s the former deals with the relation of universal concepts to one another, the second indicates the universal |\nconcepts under which [other things] the concepts of reason of things are contained; that is logical theory, for logical *praxis* is tautological if the cognition of the subject is not presupposed); logic propositions which express the relation of the universal to the particular without predicate and subject, metaphysics, however, universal propositions. It contains the elements of which all cognition of reason is composed.\nM XXXXIV:\nLogic leaves the particular nature of human reason undetermined and is valid for every reason; metaphysics shows the universal concepts that flow from the nature of human reason, and their particular laws.",["\"fundamental concepts\" = Grundbegriffe; \"concepts of reason\" = Vernunftbegriffe; \"cognition of reason\" = Vernunfterkentnis.","\"[The latter] It\": Kant struck \"Diese\" and wrote \"Sie\", which can refer to logic or to pure philosophy.","\"It contains the elements\": \"Sie\" may refer to metaphysics or to logic."]],"reflm:3947":["All rational cognition contains either the first grounds of that which is an object of merely pure cognition, or the first grounds of appearance. Dogmatic and objective are the cognitions which are valid for everyone and rest on mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:3948":["If the question were whether in empirical sciences new discoveries are still to be hoped for, then there is no doubting it, because more experiences give more and new cognitions. If the question is whether in the science of pure reason [more] new discoveries are to be expected, then it is clear that in mathematics, logic and pure morals many are possible, because according to the rule of identity from universal concepts according to all sorts of arbitrarily stipulated combinations sequences [will] can be inferred. But with metaphysics it is entirely otherwise. Here it is not asked what in arbitrarily assumed |\nrelations according to the rule of identity flows from the universal to the particular, but rather which relations really lie at the first ground of universal rules. Now since these relations are given neither through the senses in experience, nor through the understanding in an intuiting and singular concept, nothing further can happen than that one analyzes one's confused ideas. From this, however, only a science of the subject can arise. Since no object is given here, we would also be able to discover nothing about it through this cutting and analyzing of our ideas.",["\"folgen [werden] geschlossen werden können\": before the deletion \"folgen werden\" reads \"will follow\"; after it \"folgen\" reads as the noun Folgen (sequences).","\"discoveries\"/\"discover\" = Erfindungen/erfinden, also \"inventions\"/\"invent\"; \"analyze\" = zergliedern."]],"reflm:3949":["Under the logical rules stand mathematics and morals (*purae*), under the metaphysical the pure cognition of that which falls into outer and inner senses, consequently the first grounds of reason of outer and inner appearances. In both one must consider that which is the most universal of the outer and inner senses according to its first grounds of reason.\nThus there is no philosophy of pure reason except logic and *metaphysica*.\nThe fundamental concept of logic is that of the universal, insofar as it contains something under itself or not; according to this relation things are thought, but in it they are not represented. The concept of the species or genus is not given, but rather is only the relation according to which *conceptus dabiles* can be compared.\nLogic gives no fundamental concepts of reason at all, but rather of the laws according to which we [to ourselves] in general make cognitions distinct through one another. Thus it contains the rules without which we can obtain no distinct cognition of things; metaphysics, however, the rules without which objects cannot be cognized by us at all. The logical propositions are [arbitr] rules which we use arbitrarily as means in order to make cognitions distinct through comparison. Metaphysics shows the marks which are given through the nature of reason (logic only the use of marks in general).",["\"grounds of reason\" = Vernunftgründe; \"things\" stands for Dinge and, in \"cognition of things\", for Sachen.","\"[arbitr]\" renders the broken-off deletion \"[willkü]\"."]],"reflm:3950":["Synthetic [propositions] concepts are space, time and number. [and are objective.]\nIf the concepts also are synthetic, but the propositions are analytic, i.e., according to the rule of identity, then they are objective.\nBut if concepts are subjective, i.e., of no object at all, neither of a given condition of the representation of objects: space and time, nor [of] arbitrary concepts of quantity, then the judgments are nevertheless objective and universally valid, i.e., either universally true or false, [but are] if their form is analytic. But if [then] their form is synthetic, then they are subjective, and objectively they are valid only as rules of appearances, consequently as empirical judgments.",["\"If the concepts also are synthetic\" (Wenn die Begriffe auch synthetisch seyn) may also be read \"Even if the concepts are synthetic\".","\"condition\" here is the Latinate Condition, not Bedingung."]],"reflm:3951":["Dogmatic *philosophia pura* contains two disciplines.\n1. The rules of the universally valid use of the understanding;\n2. the rules of the universally valid use of the free will.\nIn both one judges according to the *nexu logico* from no *datis* of experience; but in the *physiologia transscendentali* the *data* from experience are given according to the relations of space and of time in such a way that the most universal concept of the object of all outer and inner sensations is taken and the ground of it is sought.",["Kant's Latin case forms (*nexu logico*, *datis*, *physiologia transscendentali*) are kept as written."]],"reflm:3952":["Metaphysics is a science of the laws of pure human reason and therefore subjective. The objective pure philosophy |\nhas either analytic *principia* without any axioms of experience or synthetic ones. The former [consists] rests on universal judgments according to the rule of identity and on the subordination of the particular under the universal. Thus the universally valid rules of the understanding and will, consequently *Logica et Moralis pura*. The second has as its object the most universal of outer and inner sense and thus contains the pure grounds of reason to which all natural sciences of outer and inner sense can be brought.",["\"axioms of experience\" = Erfahrungsaxiomen; \"grounds of reason\" = Vernunftgründe."]],"reflm:3953":["The principles of space are objective; but whether a thing is in space or not is subjective, because the concept of space is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3954":["A chief error arises from this, when one [compares] confuses the *genus* with all *individuis* and regards as a sequence according to concepts that which is only a lawful appearance for immediate intuiting. Hence the ground is nothing objective. What we call a (g real) ground is only a concomitant appearance for the intuitive concept. And that which we call reason, likewise all its pure concepts, since we infer from the universal to the particular, have their reality, chiefly in regard to their fundamental concepts, only through the fact that they lie in things and can be separated from them. Accordingly our reason is only a supplementing of the lack of *intuitus*.\nIf I therefore say: a principle is subjective, i.e., it contains the *conditiones* under which alone we can judge through our reason according to laws of experience, then this does not signify that our reason must assume this law in the objects; for it does not concern them at all; one can thus say neither that it is false nor true.",["\"sequence\" = Folge; \"separated\" = abgesondert, also \"abstracted\".","\"since\" (da) may also be read temporally or relatively (\"where we infer\")."]],"reflm:3955":["All cognitions are either empirical, insofar as they presuppose sensations, or pure cognitions, insofar as they have no sensation as their ground. The latter, namely the pure cognitions, are either [singular] *conceptus singulares* and are called *intuitus puri*, or universal [concepts] and are pure concepts of reason. The empirical cognitions are sensation, appearance and empirical concept; from the first the matter of all cognition is to be derived, the second adds the form of intuition to it, the third brings both under a universal concept.",[]],"reflm:3956":["Immediate judgments of experience according to relations of space and time must be distinguished from reflected ones according to relations of reason.",["\"relations of reason\" = Vernunftverhältnissen."]],"reflm:3957":["M XXXXVII:\nAll human cognitions can be divided into two chief genera: 1. those which arise from the senses [or] and are called empirical; 2. those which are acquired not at all through the senses [into the], but rather have their ground in the constant nature of the [cognitive power] thinking power of the soul, and can be called pure representations. Since all materials for thinking must necessarily be given through our senses, the matter of our entire cognition is empirical. For just that reason all pure concepts must concern merely the form of cognitions. Now we have a twofold form of cognitions: the intuitive and rational form. The first takes place only in the immediate cognition of singular things, the second in universal representations; the first I will call intuiting, the second concepts of reason. Now in all empirical cognition one can look firstly |\nmerely to the matter, and this consists in sensation; secondly to the form of appearance [and this consists]; thirdly to the form of reason in concepts. The form of appearances rests solely on space and time, and these concepts arise through no senses or sensation, but rather rest on the nature of the mind, according to which the various sensations can be represented under such relations. Hence, when all sensation of the senses is set aside, then that of space and time is a pure concept of intuition, and because in it lies everything that the understanding can cognize in experiences, it is a concept of the understanding; and, although the appearances are empirical, it is nevertheless intellectual. In the same way sensations and appearances made universal are not pure but empirical concepts of reason; but if one leaves out every effect of the senses, then the concepts are pure concepts of reason, such as: possible, substance etc. Hence all pure concepts are intellectual and intuitive, or rational and [discursive] reflecting concepts. |\nM XXXXVIII: |\nFurther, all cognitions are either given or fabricated. The matter of cognition cannot be fabricated, thus only the form, and in the form only repetition; thus all fabrication of reason concerns only mathematics; on the other hand the form which is given in geometry is space.\nM XXXXVII:\n(g Because one can describe only the form of experience, it belongs to the understanding.)\nM XXXXVIII:\nA science of pure reason is possible and also necessary. It is, however, either philosophy or mathematics; the former considers: what there is, and reason solely: which *respectus* there is; the latter: how many times one and the same is. Space is an object both for philosophy and for mathematics. The judgments about space which are intuitive are not yet mathematical, nor philosophical either. Both assess space not\n|\n*a priori* [through the rea] from principles of space which reason cognizes, but rather from principles *a posteriori*, i.e., intuitively and singularly, although not empirically. The philosophy of pure reason is either dogmatic, and then its object is given through that which all senses have most in common, and as it were through the *genus* of the senses: thus firstly the power of representation and the faculty of desire as the objects of inner sense, 2. space and time, consequently universal mechanics, as the most universal of outer senses; hence the dogmatic pure philosophy is theoretical logic, theoretical morals and universal natural science. Secondly it is critical, consequently subjective. It is zetetic, skeptical, problematic.",["\"fabricated\"/\"fabrication\" = gedichtet/Erdichtung; \"power of representation\" = Vorstellungskraft; \"faculty of desire\" = Begehrungsvermögen.","\"the first I will call intuiting\": \"anschauende\" stands without a noun in the German.","\"[through the rea]\" renders the broken-off deletion \"[durch die Ver]\"."]],"reflm:3958":["All cognitions [from experience are either] from experience (g empirical ones) belong either to sensation and contain the matter of empirical cognition, or to appearance and [comprehend] contain at the same time the form, or to the concept and contain the [what to many objects] universal of different sensations or appearances. Sensation represents individual objects insofar as they stir the senses, e.g. red, black, sweet, hard, warm etc., [the] consequently only the matter of empirical cognition. The form of objects is thought according to space and time. The form of empirical cognition is that of *coordination*, that of rational cognition is that of *subordination*.\nLet one leave out all matter of cognition, consequently everything that stirs the senses, then there still remains the empirical form of appearances; let one leave this out too, then the rational form remains; |\nand cognitions of the first kind are pure concepts of intuitions, of the second pure concepts of reason.",["'matter' renders materie; 'stir' renders rühren.","The opening sentence has no main verb for its first subject after the deletion; left as it stands."]],"reflm:3959":["Metaphysics contains nothing but ontology*, which is falsely considered as a science of things in general *qvoad praedicata vniversalia et disiunctiva*.\n(g for there is no matter, consequently a something in general, the object.)",["The asterisk after 'ontology' is Kant's reference mark for the addition in parentheses.","*qvoad praedicata vniversalia et disiunctiva*: with respect to universal and disjunctive predicates.","'object' renders obiect."]],"reflm:3960":["All cognitions are either sensible cognitions or cognitions of reason. The former are either of sensation bricht ab.",["'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the AA's note that the sentence is unfinished.","'cognitions of reason' renders Vernunfterkentnisse."]],"reflm:3961":["All (g cognitions from) experience are called empirical and are either [representations of sensation or of appearances senses or] sensations or appearances or concepts. In the former everything is given through sense [in the second] and merely the matter for cognition; the second contains in itself the sensations at the same time according to the form of space and time; the third [just] the sensations or appearances made universal through reason.\nIf one leaves out the two last actions, then sensation remains. If one leaves this out, then there remain pure concepts 1. of the understanding: of *coordination*, 2. of reason: *subordination*.",["'empirical' renders Kant's spelling Empyrisch.","'actions' renders Handlungen."]],"reflm:3962":["All concepts become universal through abstraction, but they do not all ori|ginate\nfrom it; *non subtrahendo a sensibus oriuntur, sed abstrahendo*.",["The | falls inside entspringen ('originate').","*non subtrahendo a sensibus oriuntur, sed abstrahendo*: they arise not by subtracting from the senses but by abstracting."]],"reflm:3963":["Cognitions of reason are either given in the senses as to their matter and have only the form of reason, e.g. universal concepts, or they express the form of reason itself; the former are empirical, the latter *notiones purae*.",["*notiones purae*: pure notions."]],"reflm:3964":["Metaphysics is a critique of pure reason and no doctrine. Logic is the doctrine 1. of pure, 2. of mixed reason. The application of pure reason to objects that are given through experience is only in the case of empirical concepts, where one can infer from the universal to the particular.\nThe science of a cognition considered *subiective* is critique, of it *obiective* is doctrine.\nThere are certain universal concepts which are given through the nature of reason, according to which others (g and their relation) are thought. [But there are others] E.g. subject and predicate. These are metaphysical. There are representations which are given through the senses and are made universal through reason.",["'critique' renders Critik; 'doctrine' renders doctrin, Kant's Latinate word, not Lehre.","'objects' renders obiecte; 'relation' renders Verhältnis.","'of it' (von der) presumably refers back to the cognition; the construction is elliptical."]],"reflm:3965":["There are pure concepts of intuitions, arbitrary ones of fiction, and universal ones of reason.\nA concept that cannot be regarded as an impression of the senses is pure.\nPrinciples:\n[1. What we do not through intuitions] 1. We cognize everything through judgments that we do not cognize through intuitions.\n2. All judgments of our reason are mediate.",["'arbitrary' renders willkührlich; 'fiction' renders Erdichtung.","'Principles' renders Grundsätze."]],"reflm:3966":["The logical essence is the subjective fundamental concept and does not hold for all, is also changeable; the real essence is objective; the former concerns the meaning of a word, which admittedly is gradually worn down and through use becomes unanimous.",["'essence' renders Wesen; 'fundamental concept' renders Grundbegrif.","'is worn down' renders sich abschleift; 'unanimous' renders einstimig (agreeing in voice)."]],"reflm:3967":["Reason feels the need of a real ground and yet cannot think it according to its own laws; from this it is to be seen that this concept is not objective.",["'real ground' renders Realgrund."]],"reflm:3968":["All connection is either of *subordination* or *coordination* [the former is either of]. The unity, when to many grounds one consequence is subordinated, is the unity *a priori*, e.g. in the case of the utility of things. The second: when something is the ground of much, or when many consequences arise from one ground.\nIt is peculiar that, when many grounds agree together toward one consequence, one also thinks a common ground for it, e.g. the many things for the utility of the human being, many limbs on one animal.",["'connection' renders Verknüpfung; 'consequence' renders Folge.","'peculiar' renders besonders."]],"reflm:3969":["Our reason contains nothing but *relationes*. Now if these are not given through the relations according to space and time in experience, nor through the repetition and the composition of the one out of many in pure mathematics, then they are not *relationes* which refer to objects, but only relations of our concepts according to laws of our reason.",["'relations' renders Verhältnisse, beside Kant's Latin *relationes*.","'refer to objects' renders auf obiecte gehen."]],"reflm:3970":["Intuiting concepts of the senses, concepts of the understanding of *abstraction*, concepts of reason of [relation] relation, which universal cognitions have.\nThe relations of the senses are space and time, those of the understanding: the universal and particular, consequently also all judgments and the rules of all syllogisms. Those of reason: the ground and the consequence, namely *obiective*: the ground of things (real ground).\nThe relation of sameness and difference is logical (g likewise the relation of the universal and particular), [all] that of repetition is mathematical, [that] the remaining are metaphysical — namely the real relations.\nMetaphysics is the critique of human reason, logic is the universal doctrine of the same; the first is subjective and problematic, the second objective and dogmatic.",["'Intuiting' renders Anschauende; 'things' in 'ground of things' renders Sachen, not Dinge.","The deleted and the retained word are both Verhältnis, in two spellings; 'sameness' renders Einerleyheit.","'which universal cognitions have' (welches allgemeine Erkentnisse haben): welches may refer to the relation or be read as 'which [relation] universal cognitions have'."]],"reflm:3971":["True is that which holds for every cognition, and thus not merely under the condition of reason, which judges mediately. Accordingly that is true whose opposite contradicts itself, because the opposite cannot even once fall into the senses. But that something should be without ground indicates only a subjective condition, and the object could also fall into the senses without this.",["'object' renders obiect.","'without this' (ohne diese) may refer to the subjective condition."]],"reflm:3972":["The concept of the ground (g of the consequence) contains not only that something which is there is accompanied by something else, but moreover that this relation is universal and necessary; for, where such a thing (g b) is, there is such a ground a, and where a [ground] a is, there is the consequence b. Now all real grounds and even the possibility of them are knowable only *a posteriori*; these, however, do show a constant |\naccompaniment, but no universality of the connection, consequently the concept ground is not objective.",["'relation' here renders Beziehung, elsewhere Verhältnis.","'thing' renders Sache; 'knowable' renders kennbar; 'connection' renders Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:3973":["No other merely arbitrary concepts of pure reason can arise in us than those through repetition, consequently of numbers and magnitude. All [other] concepts of reason that contain another relation require that this relation be given through experience or the nature of the understanding and reason; empirical relations, however, presuppose a fundamental relation-concept. The absolute [can] and simple one cannot feign.",["'fundamental relation-concept' renders fundamentalVerhältnisbegrif.","'feign' renders erdichten; the last sentence has the absolute and simple as object of 'feign'."]],"reflm:3974":["In the use of pure reason there arises from the mixing up of these 2 concepts a particular confusion. Everything (*singuli*) has a ground; but everything taken together cannot have a ground, thus something is without ground. The first is a usable rule of reason for the explanation of appearances; the second a consequence of the *synthesi completa*, which is impossible for our understanding. Just so: everything that is to be posited through reason is necessary; but everything is necessary under its *hypothesi*.\nAll concepts are either sensible concepts or concepts of reason. The first are either of sensation or of appearance; these have as ground of the form space and time. The second can be found through no *analysin* of experience, although all experience is coordinated with them, and are pure concepts of reason if through them no object of experience is thought; if however it is the latter, then they are empirical concepts. E.g. a *genus* is a pure concept, but a stone in general or the *genus* of stone an empirical one.\nThe science of reason of the rules for judging objectively, i.e. of all [concepts] judgments and inferences insofar as they arise *per analysin*, is logic. The science of reason of [analytic] synthetic cognitions and judgments is metaphysics.\nSpace is no concept of reason, but metaphysics seeks the concept of reason of it.",["'mixing up' renders Vermengung; 'confusion' renders Verwirrung; 'explanation' renders Erklärung.","'these have as ground of the form space and time' (diese haben zum Grunde der Form Raum und Zeit): 'of the form' may go with 'ground' or with 'space and time'.","'science of reason' renders Vernunftwissenschaft and Vernunft Wissenschaft; 'inferences' renders schlüsse."]],"reflm:3975":["Because through reason, i.e. through the cognition that is no sensation, only concepts arise through which it remains undetermined whether the thing is posited or not, there belongs something to it which necessitates us to posit something. Thus the concept of a ground has a subjective origin, and the word: something is posited through another thing or for the sake of another, signifies *obiective* only: things accompany each other or follow upon one another.\nThe idea of the ground (g cause) arises from experiences.\nTo cognize through reason that something is not or that something is not possible is one and the same; that something is or that something is necessary is one and the same. On the other hand everything appears as contingent which I indeed cognize through senses, but not through reason, that it is.\nThe only concepts of pure reason through whose relation according to the rule of identity truths can be discovered are the magnitudes; but these are also arbitrary fictions.",["'there belongs something to it' renders so gehöret etwas dazu.","'identity' renders identitaet; 'fictions' renders Erdichtungen."]],"reflm:3976":["It is necessary according to the subjective law of reason to assume a first action through which all the rest follows; but it is just as necessary to assume a ground in general of every action and thus no first.\nThere are pure fundamental concepts of intuition or of reflection; the first are the principles of appearance, the second of insight; the first show *coordination*, the second *subordination*. Because everything is represented in time, all our concepts of reason are nevertheless always at the same time thought under the condition of the *phaenomeni*; the conditions of the |\nsecond do not agree with the first. In time no first is possible, and in the ground there is supposed to be one.\nIf the comprehensibility of an [act] thing were to be complete, then we would have to have a first ground; but we also cannot posit a first ground through reason, and thus it follows from this that the comprehensibility of no absolute position is complete through human reason.\nThe simple concepts of reason, indeed all simple concepts, are subjective; the objective ones consist in the agreement of cognition with itself and are thus composite. Hence the concept of the first is subjective, for it contains the concept of everything.\nAll real relations (space and time excepted) are given through experience (g according to the relations of space and time) and thus can serve for no propositions of pure reason.",["'reflection' renders reflexion; 'principles' renders principien, not Grundsätze; 'position' renders position.","'[act]' renders the deleted, unfinished Handl, the start of Handlung.","'thing' in 'comprehensibility of a thing' renders Sache; 'comprehensibility' renders begreiflichkeit."]],"reflm:3977":["All truth consists in the necessary agreement of a cognition with itself. If the cognition that is to agree with itself constitutes the form of appearance itself, e.g. space and time, then every judgment is [subjec] objective and either true or false. But if the cognition concerns solely a law of human reason through which we compare concepts, then it is not objective at all, hence neither true nor false. Hence ground and consequence is no property at all of things, which is given through mere reason, but is given only through experience. It is, however, a law of reason to look to this relation; all universal rules of reason about cause and effect have no validity at all of objects.",["'[subjec]' renders the deleted, unfinished subiec.","'which is given' (die ... gegeben ... ist) may refer to the property or to the things; the singular verb favors the property.","'property' renders Eigenschaft; 'validity of objects' renders Gültigkeit von Obiecten."]],"reflm:3978":["Every objective concept is either an original image (*archetypon*) or |\ncopy (*Ectypon*). The former happens when the concept of the object is thought arbitrarily or yet without it no concept at all of the objects [mo] and no experience is possible. The latter happens when the object is given through the senses. The pure concepts of reason, which are given through the nature of reason, are [are] not copies, secondly also not original images, for they are not thought arbitrarily, but contain the ground of the possibility of all arbitrary ideas of reason. They are also universal concepts; consequently they do not contain the form of experiences, and without them [representations] immediate ideas of experience are always possible; consequently they are only concepts of condition, without which a cognition through our reason is impossible, hence not objective.\nThe logical form all cognitions of reason have, but the metaphysical form only those [in] which presuppose real fundamental concepts or principles of pure reason.",["'original image' renders Urbild, 'copy' Nachbild.","'[mo]' is the deleted, unfinished start of möglich ('possible'), left as it stands.","'without it' (ohne denselben) may refer to the concept or to the object; 'principles' renders Grundsätze."]],"reflm:3979":["M XXXXI:\nA dogmatic science of pure reason has arbitrary ideas as its ground, and their relations it thinks to itself in logical form. But no relations can be thought arbitrarily other than those of repetition, i.e. of numbers, and consequently the science is mathematics; but one can also seek the rule of choice itself, insofar as it is supposed to be universally valid, and this is morals.\nWhere the concepts are given neither through experience nor through the nature of reason, there choice is the ground of them, either as to form, if no matter at all (g object) is given (but the arbitrary form of concepts is only the mathematical one), |\nM XXXXII: |\nor (g pure) choice (g in general) is itself the matter (g the |\nobject) and form for the concept, and the judgments are passed in both according to logical form. In mathematics it says: I will think these relations to myself, which rules follow from them according to the logical form. In morals: I will think choice itself in relations, which rules hold for it in general. Thus mathematics is the bricht ab.",["'choice' renders Willkühr; 'arbitrary' and 'arbitrarily' render willkührlich, its adjective.","'object' renders obiekt and Obiekt; M XXXXI and M XXXXII are the pages of Kant's copy.","'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the AA's note that the sentence is unfinished."]],"reflm:3980":["If I want to understand something in nature, then I must not go outside nature with my explanation. But if I want to understand the whole of nature, then I must be outside its bounds.",["'explanation' renders Erklärung; 'whole' renders gesamte."]],"reflm:3981":["The pure concepts of reason have no *exemplaria*, but are themselves the archetypes; but the concepts of our pure reason have as their archetype this reason itself, are therefore subjective and not objective.",["“archetypes”: Urbilder; “concepts of reason”: Vernunftbegriffe.","“subjective”, “objective”: subiectiv, obiectiv."]],"reflm:3982":["The logical laws of reason contain the rules according to which concepts are subordinated one to the *sphaera* of the other, which is called logical subordination. The metaphysical laws are, according to which the concepts are subordinated to one another *realiter*.",["The second sentence is elliptical in the German (“Die metaphysischen Gesetze sind, nach welchen …”), with no antecedent for “according to which”; kept so."]],"reflm:3983":["All concepts are either archetypes, which [conc] are grounds of the determinations that belong to the objects and through which the object itself is determined among all possible ones, or they are ectypes, which |\nare consequences of the determinations of things. Since through space and time all concepts of experience are possible, so bricht ab.",["“archetypes”: Urbilder; “ectypes”: Nachbilder (copies).","“consequences”: Folgen; “[conc]”: [Begr], an abbreviation of Begriffe.","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3984":["Everything that happens, i.e. that one is necessitated [through the] prior to experience to cognize as having happened, has a ground; but what one wills, that it shall happen, has no further ground. For every object that is determined through choice has no further ground why it is thought thus and not otherwise than this choice. Since through the will something first arises, so it can just as little be asked in the case of arbitrary ideas, which are a ground of actions, as in the case of arbitrary speculative ideas bricht ab.",["“prior to experience”: vor der Erfahrung; vor may be the eighteenth-century spelling of für, “for experience”.","“choice”: Willkühr; “arbitrary”: willkührlich.","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3985":["We can posit nothing through reason without subordinating it to another. We can think no subordination without the ground being thought as *antecedens*, hence the whole series being thought *per synthesin a priori completam*.\nNo *synthesis* is *completa* except only that which is completed *successive progrediendo* in a time. If, therefore, a whole is given, then we posit that *regrediendo* a *terminus* is found from which the *synthesis completa* begins, consequently that a whole is neither divisible to infinity nor composed to infinity. The *synthesis infinita*, however (eternity), never gives a whole.",["*per synthesin a priori completam*: through a complete synthesis a priori; *successive progrediendo*: by progressing successively; *regrediendo*: by regressing.","“to infinity”: ins unendliche."]],"reflm:3986":["One can assume that the motion of a body is only a successive presence of a great efficacy of impenetrability in space |\nwhere not the substance changes its place, but this effect of impenetrability succeeds gradually in different places, as with sound the waves of air. One can also assume that in space there are no substances at all, but a greater or lesser efficacy of a single supreme cause in different places of space. From this it would follow that matter would be infinitely divisible.",["“efficacy”: Wirksamkeit; “effect”: Wirkung.","“place” renders both platz (first) and Ort, Örter (afterwards)."]],"reflm:3987":["The concept of God is the only idea that is thoroughly determined through reason and is also given as the supreme ground of all determination.",[]],"reflm:3988":["[In our judgments we attend either only to the idea of a thing alone and to that through which we think it to ourselves, or to its relation to others.]\nWe have two kinds of concepts: those that can arise in us through the presence of the thing; or those [which] through which the understanding represents to itself the relation of these concepts to the laws of its own thinking. To the latter belongs the concept of ground, |\nof possibility, of existence. Hence the principles about the former are objective, those about the latter subjective.\nMetaphysics is a science of having insight into the relation of human reason to the first properties of things.\nAll rational fundamental concepts are concepts of form, the empirical ones are *principia* of matter. the former are merely subjective. i.e. abstracted from the [action] laws of our thinking. the latter objective. from the representation itself, through which the object is represented. The understanding is applied to experiences only according to laws of the understanding, but the separated idea of the relation of the representation of sensation in general according to laws of the understanding makes up the pure rational concept. The understanding proceeds according to a natural law when it thinks one and many. This understanding, [abstr] applied to the sensation of a body, abstracts the idea of a whole not from it, but from itself.\nThe *philosophia pura* about the rational concepts is only subjective and never synthetic; the *principia* of the *philosophiae applicatae domestica* are the first laws of experience in combination with the *principiis rationalibus*, insofar as these refer to the *empirica*. The proposition: nothing comes from nothing, and the proposition: everything has a ground, have the same fault, that they are expressed rationally, although they are valid only empirically.\nThe idea of an undetermined freedom cannot be thought at all according to laws of our understanding; but it is not false on that account.\nBy what does one cognize the plurality and unity of substances. The representation of the I is actually a unity; but is the diversity of places a proof of plurality?\nIf one only observes bricht ab.",["“have insight into”: einzusehen; “properties”: Eigenschaften; “representation of sensation”: Empfindungsvorstellung; “rational concept”: rationalbegriff.","*philosophiae applicatae domestica*: the Latin is doubtful as it stands; domestica (native, proper) may go with *principia* (“the proper principles of applied philosophy”).","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3989":["A–A=0 signifies an empty representation, for negating is not |\nempty; but whether an empty representation signifies as much as something impossible, is a question.",[]],"reflm:3990":["If I first posit something and afterwards cancel it, then it is 0 (g *rei*), if I at the same time posit and cancel, then [0 *cogitationis*\n. . . 0] is impossible. The impossible does not signify merely *conceptum inanem*, in which nothing at all is thought; for it is possible to think nothing at all.",["“cancel”: aufheben. *rei*: of the thing; *cogitationis*: of thought; *conceptum inanem*: an empty concept.","The bracketed passage is defective in the AA; the construction of “then … is impossible” is accordingly uncertain."]],"reflm:3991":["Through the *principium contradictionis* is cognized not the possibility of things, but of the predicates of the same or of their relation.",[]],"reflm:3992":["Affirmative judgments serve for cognition of the identity of a thing with others, negative ones for distinction. Hence tautological propositions are ridiculous.\nReason contains nothing but *respectivae notiones*; the senses, since they cognize without reflection, must give something absolute.\nWe must here pay very careful attention that reason judges about itself according to its own laws, consequently also not always objectively.",["*respectivae notiones*: relative notions.","“reflection”: reflexion."]],"reflm:3993":["From the nature of our understanding flows the idea of boundaries, i.e. of the first and last.",["“boundaries”: Grentzen."]],"reflm:3994":["There are 2 concepts which are not properly predicates of things, but through which the things themselves are thought with all their predicates.",[]],"reflm:3995":["In metaphysics, just as in optics, one must pay attention that the combinations or separations of concepts whose ground is subjective are not taken for objective.",["“taken for”: vor … gehalten (= für … gehalten)."]],"reflm:3996":["There are two principles of the objective laws of reason, which are analytic; two principles which are subjective, and are synthetic. The latter are principles of every [use] real use of reason: the rules how we are to use reason in all real relations, even in the case of the senses. These are falsely regarded as principles of pure reason, which are objective.",["“principles”: Grundsätze; “use” (n.): Gebrauch; “to use”: brauchen."]],"reflm:3997":["On and \n*Conceptus ponens*: A; *tollens*: non A; [*deficiens*\n= A×0] *deficiens*\n=0; *conceptus inanis*: to which no object corresponds, e.g. a certain fictitious force. *Conceptus implicans* =A non A (non A + non A). *Ens ponens*: A; *ens tollens*: –A; *ens deficiens*: A–A=0. the *realiter* opposed not contradictory to itself, and the impossible is different from the not possible.",["“On and”: Über und; the heading looks incomplete.","*ponens*: positing; *tollens*: cancelling; *deficiens*: lacking; *inanis*: empty; *implicans* (sc. contradictionem): self-contradictory.","The last sentence has no verb in its first clause (realiter oppositum sibi non contradictorium); kept so."]],"reflm:3998":["The proposition can be immediately certain; but its immediacy can be uncertain (g be hidden), as: a whole is greater than a part.\nThere are concepts which are problematic for reason, others which are dogmatic.",["The German doubles the verb around the addition: “ungewiß (g verborgen seyn) seyn”.","“for reason”: vor die Vernunft (= für)."]],"reflm:3999":["Since we form rational concepts only through judgments, but in these the thing is represented through predicates, we represent to ourselves an object of thoughts and its being or non-being separately as a predicate; if we abstract from this [and the] there still always remains an object of thought, which, insofar as it does not cancel itself, is something possible.\nThe possibility of judgments is contradistinguished from truth, and the possibility of things from actuality.\nThe contradictions of pure reason arise from this, that it does not, like empirical philosophy, draw the universal propositions from particular ones, but judges universally from (g pure) concepts, but nevertheless needs a *terminum* [a priori] (g of subordination or coordination), which contradicts universality.\nWe can through reason cognize only the possibility of judgments, but not of things wholly, because to these belongs the presupposition of the possibility of matter, which must be given through the senses and thus a posteriori. As regards matter the understanding cannot even invent something impossible. The falsity of the form in the *synthesi* can be established through nothing but through the conflict of the consequences with other certain cognitions.\nPossibility is only a form, but the possible also contains matter.",["“object of thoughts”: Gegenstand der Gedanken; “object of thought”: Gegenstand des Gedankens. “separately”: besonders, which can also mean “especially”.","“wholly” (gantz) may go with “cognize” or with “of things”; left as ambiguous as the German.","“conflict”: wiederstreit; “consequences”: folgen."]],"reflm:4000":["Some things are valid *obiective* under an arbitrary condition, others *subiective* under a (g through the understanding) given condition. These given conditions are appearances (space and time lie every|where\nat the ground). Hence although, if the concepts ground and cause were objective, one of two propositions: There is a first cause or: there is none, would have to be true; but now, since neither of the two is objective, both are true at the same time as subjective laws.",["“arbitrary”: willkührlich.","The sentence beginning “Hence although” has no main clause answering “although”; kept so."]],"reflm:4001":["It is difficult to represent to oneself how the consequence is after the ground, if this relation is not merely a *phaenomenon*.",["“consequence”: Folge. “after”: nach, which can also mean “according to”."]],"reflm:4002":["Where there are nothing but real connections, there is no 0, also no alteration. All alterations in the world therefore stem from real oppositions; the last effect of them is therefore 0.",["“real connections”: Realverknüpfungen; “real oppositions”: realoppositionen."]],"reflm:4003":["Impossibility of propositions and impossibility of judgments. If I include the condition of the impossibility in the concept of the thing, then it is absolute impossibility according to concepts or according to experience.",["“propositions”: Sätze; “judgments”: Urtheile."]],"reflm:4004":["Every concept is possible in which there lies a predicate that is considered in the subject and does not contradict it; but not every *synthesis* is possible in which there is no contradiction. i.e. real relations are not seen into through the principle of contradiction.",["“principle of contradiction”: Satz des Wiederspruchs."]],"reflm:4005":["We can through reason cognize only the conditioned possibility. The absolute possibility that we cognize through reason |\nhappens according to the *principio contradictionis* and concerns propositions into which we resolve the concepts.\nAll absolute possibility must otherwise be given through experience; what is there is in itself possible.",["“what is there”: was da ist, i.e. what exists."]],"reflm:4006":["In the case of all elementary representations we can borrow possibility only from actuality, because the former are given for thinking only through the latter.\nIf something under certain conditions always happens in the same way and yet has in these conditions no natural ground, then it must have the ground in an intention or in freedom. e.g. if the game always falls on one side. Thus freedom is a first ground and is not understood along with that which happens.",["“the former … the latter”: jene … diese, with a plural verb, so that “the former” may be the elementary representations rather than possibility.","“intention”: Absicht; “game”: Spiel, perhaps a throw of dice.","“understood along with”: mit verstanden."]],"reflm:4007":["Of everything that is there we must cognize a ground if we want to cognize through reason that it is*; therefore we cannot cognize the absolutely necessary. In everything subordinated we must assume a first, which therefore is necessary in itself**; therefore there is a conflict of subjective laws.\n*(s therefore it is a subjective rule of reason. 2. everything that (g contingently) is an object of experience, (happens), has a ground: is an objective rule of [the senses] experience.)\n**(s principle, which we assume only for this reason, because otherwise we could not see into something a priori.)",["“conflict”: Streit; “principle”: princip."]],"reflm:4008":["Everything that (g comes to be) happens is through something or other, is similar to the proposition:\nEverything that comes to be is out of something or other. From nothing comes nothing, i.e. substance cannot come to be. Which only indicates that our understanding is valid only *in consequentia* and that in all explanations of nature we must assume that substance always is. arising and passing away, coming to be and ceasing are also bound to time and are valid only for phenomena.\nIt is also a law of our reason: The supreme cause (one or many) always is, can neither arise nor pass away. Includes also the relation of time in itself.",["“comes to be”: wird (werden); “arising and passing away”: entstehen und vergehen.","“explanations”: erklärungen."]],"reflm:4009":["In this demonstration 1. the word “nothing” in the logical sense (which signifies as much as the universal negation in a judgment and affects the *copulam* of a universal judgment) is confused with the nothing, namely the remotion of things. e.g. no thing has a ground, Nothing has a ground, and: the nothing is the ground of some things.\nThus the author also did it with the *definitio* of the *minimi*.",["“remotion”: remotion (Latin remotio, removal).","“in the logical sense”: im logischen Verstande."]],"reflm:4010":["Everything has a supreme logical ground\nand last logical consequence.\n(g The former the most universal concept, which under none, the latter the singular, under which none is contained.)",["“consequence”: Folge. “which under none”: the verb is missing in the German (der unter keinem)."]],"reflm:4011":["Is a necessary hypothesis of healthy reason, is therefore valid |\nonly in the world and is also the principle of the use of the same in it. Everything has a first ground, is also such a principle, because it begins a posteriori; but to begin a priori from the first ground and to posit it itself is an action of original reason.",["“healthy reason”: Gesunde Vernunft, i.e. common sense.","“the same” (derselben) may be reason or the world; “to posit it itself” (ihn selber zu setzen) may mean to posit the ground itself or to posit it oneself."]],"reflm:4012":["The *principium rationis*, likewise the *principium rationati*, is a rule of healthy reason and thus restricted to the objects of experience. The boundaries of the sensible world are therefore also the boundaries of its use. If one goes further and wants to make their validity universal, then one errs and brings it into science.",["*principium rationis*: principle of ground; *principium rationati*: principle of the grounded. “sensible world”: Sinnenwelt.","“it” (ihn, masculine) in the last clause most naturally refers to Gebrauch, use; “their” (deren) to the two principles."]],"reflm:4013":["Everything that is, has a ground, is a merely subjective principle of reason and has no relation to the possibility of appearances. What happens, has a ground, signifies that in which I am passive and observe it. But what I do freely is for me not something that happens. (s Is a conditional proposition of the reality of our |\nrepresentations, which is opposed to their nullity, namely to fantasies. If the representation of an object is not to be [given] made by us, but rather given, then it must be posited according to [all] a universal rule under certain conditions, whereby the relation of this object to the representation and the existence of it is valid for others too. It is entirely different when the representation is made by us (through freedom); for then we are immediately conscious of its reality, i.e., a state in us that we ourselves make can be cognized as a true state, even without ground.\nTo cognize something as what, to understand, to have insight, to comprehend. We cognize that which is given to us as what; i.e., we give its representations an object through the function of the understanding.\nIf, where, why. *accidentia* and composition are not things.)",["\"principle\" renders Kant's Germanized \"princip\"; \"object\" renders both Gegenstand and \"obiect\" (Latinate).","\"to cognize something as what\": etwas wovor (= wofür) erkennen; the same wovor recurs in the next sentence.","\"to have insight\": einsehen; \"the existence of it\": derselben, most naturally the representation."]],"reflm:4014":["Everything that arises has a ground;\nEverything that passes away, also a positive ground.",[]],"reflm:4015":["All signifies either the sufficiency (g of the derivation) *a priori* or the completeness of the taking-together *a posteriori*; e.g., We can say something in general of all motion, but not cognize all motion together.",["\"All\": Alles, the word itself is under discussion.","\"taking-together\": Zusammennehmung."]],"reflm:4016":["The cognition of the logical essence is acquired merely analytically, that |\nof the real essence synthetically. The former is the first subjective fundamental concept, the latter the first objective one. *In conceptibus arbitrariis qva talibus* subjective and objective are the same.",["\"essence\": Wesen; \"fundamental concept\": Grundbegriff.","*In conceptibus arbitrariis qva talibus*: \"in arbitrary concepts as such\"."]],"reflm:4017":["*praedicata logica* are concepts [which] through which I can cognize [can] or posit certain things. Accordingly all concepts are predicates; but they signify either things or their position: the former is a real predicate, the second only a logical one.\nConcepts are undetermined with regard to many predicates, but things are not. That is why thoroughgoing determination also adheres to actuality and is more in existence than in possibility. This also flows from the fact that through possibility I posit merely the concept, through actuality the thing. If God takes existence from a thing, then he takes from it no predicate, but rather the thing itself (s but not the possibility or [its] the concept of the thing). Whoever denies existence removes the thing with all its predicates.\nExistence can indeed be a logical, but never a real predicate of a thing.",["\"things\" renders both Dinge and Sachen; Sachen in \"signify either things\", \"but things are not\", \"the thing itself\", \"the concept of the thing\", \"removes the thing\".","\"existence\": Daseyn; \"actuality\": Wirklichkeit; \"removes\": removirt."]],"reflm:4018":["The existential propositions express: how we are to think things, not: what; namely *absolute*, not *respective*.",["*absolute* ... *respective*: Latin adverbs, \"absolutely\" ... \"relatively\"."]],"reflm:4019":["An object is given insofar as it is thoroughly determined. If through its concept it is thoroughly given, then it is necessary.",["\"thoroughly\": durchgängig."]],"reflm:4020":["Existence pertains only to things, actuality also to their actions.",["\"Existence\": Daseyn; \"actuality\": Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:4021":["We can represent nothing to ourselves as possible except what has been given in the senses both in its *data* and in its real relations; thus our possibilities are merely modifications of that whose universal ground is given through experience.",["\"real relations\": realverhaltnissen."]],"reflm:4022":["The proposition that a possible necessarily exists signifies as much as: *sublata omni actualitate tollitur possibilitas*; whether this latter proposition be true must be investigated.",["*sublata omni actualitate tollitur possibilitas*: \"when all actuality is cancelled, possibility is cancelled\"."]],"reflm:4023":["If each thing is only *in nexu omnimodo* with all determinations of the supreme cause and thus also with its whole effect, but of that being [only] the whole effect is only a single one: then everything that does not actually exist is also not possible *in totum*.",["The first clause has no complete predicate (\"is only in nexu omnimodo\"); kept as in the German.","\"its\": dessen, masculine or neuter, so not grammatically the cause (Ursache); left ambiguous.","*in nexu omnimodo*: \"in an all-round nexus\"; *in totum*: \"as a whole\"."]],"reflm:4024":["Magnitude is cognized either through the composition or through the limitation of a given all. The first always requires time.\nWhy is *mathesis* capable of such evident propositions and of such correctness? Origin of its concepts.\nOur faculty of counting is (g in itself) unlimited; if it is [no] not limited, it is undetermined; for it cannot be taken whole. but in the *realissimum* the *omnitudo* precedes the limitations.",["\"limitation\" renders both Einschränkung and Schranken (\"the limitations\"); \"unlimited\": uneingeschränkt.","\"all\": All (noun), the whole; *omnitudo*: allness."]],"reflm:4025":["transscendentaliter something is considered when it is weighed in relation to its (s own) essence* as the sequel; metaphysice, when the essence is considered with regard to its sequels as a ground.\n*(s if it is considered respective to the essence of things in general, then it is called metaphysical.)",["The asterisks are Kant's reference sign; the Latin words (transscendentaliter, metaphysice, respective) are left without italic asterisks so as not to be confused with it.","\"essence\": Wesen; \"sequel\": Folge; \"weighed\": erwogen."]],"reflm:4026":["transcendental unity.\nMetaphysical unity, truth and perfection. Agreement of one as a ground with the manifold, of the manifold with one as sequel, and of the manifold among one another.",["\"Agreement\": Zusammenstimmung; \"sequel\": Folge."]],"reflm:4027":["The transcendental unity is logical, *respective* to the essence of the thing.\nThe metaphysical unity is real, *respective* to possibility in general.\nThe former is the unity of the taking-together, where something does not contradict itself.\nThe latter: unity of connection, where the many is a ground of One and the unity is not arbitrary.",["\"taking-together\": Zusammennehmung; \"connection\": Verknüpfung.","\"where something does not contradict itself\": wo sich was nicht wiederspricht, also readable as \"where nothing contradicts\" one another."]],"reflm:4028":["All finite things are defective as things in general (not *transscendentaliter*). One indeed supplies the imperfection of the other; but because it is another thing, it replaces the imperfection only in the whole.\nThe perfection of a thing *respective* to a certain concept or [*respective* to] with regard to the concept of a thing in general is the *completudo rationis*, the former the respective, the latter the metaphysical.\nGoodness is the ground of perfection, *unum ad qvod consentitur*.\nThe goodness of human beings is the moral. The relative goodness is that which is only a means to [absol] something else that pleases.",["\"Goodness\": Kant's Germanized \"bonitaet\" (*bonitas*); \"supplies\": supplirt.","*completudo rationis*: completeness of the ground; *unum ad qvod consentitur*: the one with which there is agreement."]],"reflm:4029":["Perfection taken *substantive* or *adiective* [betr].\nIn the latter case it is no quality or quantity or relation, but only the *modus* of a determination; the *perfectio substantive sumta* is a determination in general, insofar as this *modus* belongs to it; with regard to entity, reality is that to which the *modus* of perfection belongs.",["[betr] is the start of a struck word (betrachtet?), left untranslated.","*perfectio substantive sumta*: perfection taken substantively; \"entity\": entitaet."]],"reflm:4030":["Necessity is combined with every categorical position of reason.\nSomething is posited through concepts.\n*Necessarium et contingens* is either absolute or hypothetical.\nLogical or real necessity. The latter the absolute or hypothetical.\nNominal concept through omission of the condition.\n*oppositum entis sibi non contradicit*. *Conceptus terminator*.",["*oppositum entis sibi non contradicit*: \"the opposite of the being does not contradict itself\"; *Conceptus terminator*: terminating concept."]],"reflm:4031":["Law of reason, not of the senses:\nEverything that is, is necessary, either simply or conditioned.\nAll necessity is conditioned, but under condition of the inner determinations of a thing (of that which is inwardly in the thing) or under outer conditions.",["\"simply\": schlechthin; \"conditioned\": Bedingt, here used adverbially."]],"reflm:4032":["Everything that happens is contingent in itself; but because it must nevertheless be necessary in virtue of the above, it is necessary through an alien ground.",["\"alien\": fremden, also \"another's\"."]],"reflm:4033":["All (g absolute) necessity is either of judgments or of things. The first, as the logical, is at all times a conditioned necessity of the predicate. The necessity of things that we can cognize is at all times conditioned; for in itself we can at all times cancel every thing, because, since we affirm nothing, we also contradict nothing through the negation. The concept of the necessary is nevertheless first of all a concept given through reason, because through it alone something is determined. Absolute necessity is a boundary concept, because without it no *completudo* in the series of the contingent would |\nbe. This boundary concept, however, is itself problematic and cannot be cognized *a priori* through reason, because it is a *conceptus terminator*, nor be had insight into *a posteriori*, for that is not called having insight, nor immediately; it is therefore problematic.\nThe supreme *principium* of everything contingent is the absolutely necessary, and the supreme *principium* (g of the possibility) of arising is freedom. It is thought that with regard to a freely acting being every state that is in its power constitutes a *terminum a priori* that is not connected with the preceding ones through a natural link. It is itself no link of a greater chain, at least not as regards activity.",["\"things\": Sachen; \"cancel\": aufheben; \"boundary concept\": Grentzbegrif; \"have insight\": einsehen.","\"in itself\": an sich selbst, which may go with \"we\" or with the thing; \"nothing\": keinem, also \"no one\".","\"link\": Ring, literally \"ring\"."]],"reflm:4034":["The actuality of the *omnimode contingentis* (free action) is just as little to be comprehended as that of the *omnimode necessarii*.[The latter] This, for the reason that, if the *necessitas* is *mediata*, the first necessity is not to be understood; that, because [if the *contingentia* is only *respectiva*] the existence of such a thing is not to be comprehended.",["\"This\" (Dieses) and \"that\" (jenes): the latter and the former respectively.","*omnimode contingens / necessarium*: the in every way contingent / necessary; *necessitas mediata*: mediate necessity."]],"reflm:4035":["All necessity is a necessity of judgments or of things; with the former, if I want to think the opposite, I retain the subject and cancel its identical predicate; with the latter, if I want to think the opposite, I cancel the thing with all its predicates; hence in the former case I can cognize the (g absolute) necessity from the principle of contradiction, but in the latter not.",["\"things\": Sachen; \"cancel\": aufheben; \"principle of contradiction\": Satz des Wiederspruchs."]],"reflm:4036":["Not necessity, but contingency is incomprehensible for rea|son.\nThis idea of contingency is either logical (with universal concepts that are undetermined with regard to particular determinations) or consists in this, that I can cancel the thing; this possibility, however, proves too much.",["\"undetermined\": undeterminirt; \"cancel\": aufheben."]],"reflm:4037":["Inwardly contingent is what, if one abstracts from all conditions, is contingent; absolutely contingent is what (g either has no conditions or) is contingent even with all conditions, because they are the conditions.\nAbsolutely necessary is what even under necessary conditions, and thus outwardly, is necessary. The inner or outer necessity makes no difference in this concept, but only in its conditions.\nNothing is absolutely necessary except what is necessarily connected with something inwardly necessary. Absolutely contingent is what is contingent under all conditions; such a thing one must deny.",["\"absolutely\": schlechterdings throughout, not absolut.","\"because they are the conditions\": weil es die Bedingungen sind; the reference of es is left as open as in the German."]],"reflm:4038":["Above and beside the running head »Necessarium et contingens«:\nAbsolute necessity is more than the hypothetical.\nAbsolute contingency is less than the hypothetical,\nbecause absolute possibility is the least.\n(g The *omnimoda contingentia*)",["The first line is the Academy Edition's statement of where the note stands.","\"the least\": das kleinste, literally \"the smallest\"."]],"reflm:4039":["The concept of an *absolute necessarii* is a *conceptus terminator* (because we [in de] must regard everything contingent as necessary through a ground, and finally the condition must fall away); and since the con|dition\nof intelligibility falls away, it cannot, according to the laws of reason, be had insight into, in that in the series of possible insights it is something in relation to the sequels and borders on nothing with regard to the grounds. The concept of freedom is also a boundary concept of an absolute beginning. (g Likewise the concept of the simple and in general of the first. All laws of the understanding serve for comprehensibility in the series i breaks off.)\nThe concept of substance is also *conceptus terminator*. Likewise the boundary of the world. But who is *conceptus comprehensor*.\nPossibility without actuality rests on the difference between the self-active representation through concept and the passive one through sensation, since the former remains even if the latter has been entirely canceled. In general the possibility of the non-actual is only something that is thought of a certain universal concept.\nAll *conceptus terminatores* are taken from possibility *per synthesin* and always require a first. Hence *a parte inferiori vel posteriori* there is no *conceptus terminator*. The *terminus* of the series is its first member, but the *conceptus terminator* the concept through which a first of the series is possible. Of this concept, which is supposed to be perfectly comprehensible through itself, [can nevertheless] such properties must be thought whereby it can be, in general and without a limitation to be taken from elsewhere, and thus as an unlimited being for itself, the *principium* of the series.\nMorality also has its own *termini a priori* and *a posteriori*: a lawgiver and a reward and punishment according to spiritual laws, thus another world.",["\"i breaks off\": Kant's \"i\" followed by the Academy Edition's \"bricht ab\"; the note is unfinished.","\"be had insight into\": einsehen; \"boundary\": Grentze; \"self-active\": selbstthatigen; \"passive\": leidenden.","*a parte inferiori vel posteriori*: on the lower or later side; \"for itself\": vor sich selbst, also \"by itself\"."]],"reflm:4040":["The two propositions: a state is possible in which a certain de|termination\nis not, and: it is possible that a certain state not be at all, are different. The opposite of a state is therefore possible either as a particular state or as the not-being of the given one. In general the cancellation of that which is, is not the same as non-being.\nTo be necessarily and to endure necessarily are different concepts. The necessary being cannot be compared with time at all; therefore it can in time neither persist with the same *determinationes*, nor change them [nor] or vary with regard to them.\nWhat I call unalterable is nevertheless considered under the hypothesis of time; accordingly the *intellectualia* are neither alterable nor unalterable. The beginning of sensibility contradicts itself because then the sensible is considered as bordering on the *intellectuali*.",["\"cancellation\": Aufhebung; \"not-being\" and \"non-being\": nicht seyn and Nichtseyn.","\"alterable\": Veranderlich; \"contradicts itself\": contradicirt sich."]],"reflm:4041":["Contingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place; [zuf] Alterable: [whose] that which is possible in connection with its opposite.\nIn every alteration [is] there are: 1. *oppositae determinationes, qvatenus eidem competunt*. 2. *successio earundem*. The possibility of mutation is not to be cognized from mere contingency. For because it is possible that instead of one predicate there be another, it is not yet to be cognized from this that the subject has the *opposita* one after another. The relation of the real ground to the sequel seems at the same time to contain the idea of succession; for [in de] the sequel is here too something other than the ground, and both can hardly be distinguished otherwise than through *prius et posterius*[erkann]. Whether a simple substance can alter itself in itself? and how through the *compraesentiam* and |\nthe *nexum* of several the alteration is possible? All alterations can be thought only in time.",["*oppositae determinationes, qvatenus eidem competunt*: opposite determinations, insofar as they belong to the same thing; *successio earundem*: succession of the same.","\"mutation\": mutation, \"contingency\": contingentz, beside Veränderung (\"alteration\"); \"sequel\": Folge.","[zuf] and [erkann] are struck word-beginnings, left untranslated."]],"reflm:4042":["(s Whether a concept has objective reality?)\n*realitas* taken *adiective* is opposed to ideality and signifies [relation] the objective; e.g. realists with regard to bodies or idealists.\nJust so nature taken *adiective* signifies something other than nature simply. (s Magnitudes and the magnitude of a thing.)",["\"[relation]\" renders the struck-out Beziehung.","\"simply\": schlechthin."]],"reflm:4043":["Negation is either the consequent of a cancelling or of a deficient ground*; the former is privation. The latter cannot properly be called a consequent, because nothing is posited in it.\n*(s The latter can still be thought as a vanishing ground and thus as specifically one and the same with the ratione ponente. e.g. point an infinitely small space, rest as vanishing motion.)",["Latin: rationatum rationis tollentis vel deficientis; \"consequent\" renders rationatum.","The asterisk is Kant's reference sign linking the addition to the first line; it is kept, and the Latin ratione ponente (positing ground) is therefore left without italics.","\"specifically one and the same\": specifisch einerley."]],"reflm:4044":["Opposite and \nIn so far as a being is not the most perfect, some defects are necessary in order that certain perfections be obtained. e.g. abstraction for distinctness. A certain degree of cowardice, laziness, ignorance is very necessary to human nature.",["\"Opposite and\" (Gegenüber und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"defects\": Mängel."]],"reflm:4045":["whether realities are all of one and the same kind and can be measured through more and less.",["\"of one and the same kind\": von einerley Art."]],"reflm:4046":["The *omnitudo collectiva* (g in One) or totality rests on the *positione simultanea*. From the [*omnitudine*] *multitudine distributiva* I can infer to the *unitatem collectivam*, but not from the *omnitudine*, because the progression is infinite and not complete. Time is infinite with regard to potential simultaneity. Hence we represent space to ourselves as *actualiter* infinite.",["\"in One\": in Einem, capitalized in the source; it may mean \"in one thing\" or \"in a unity\".","\"infer\": schließen."]],"reflm:4047":["The whole is either of subordination: series (serial order), or of coordination: aggregate (heap).\nThe whole, insofar as in it the parts are homogeneous, is a magnitude; but they can also be heterogeneous, and to that extent it is not a magnitude.\nThat whole of which no part is an absolute unity is infinitely divisible.",["\"series (serial order)\": Reihe (reihenfolge); \"heap\": Haufen."]],"reflm:4048":["The ground of the unity of coordination: whether it is founded on the unity of subordination [and whether not sel], insofar as they all flow immediately from one ground.\nThe matter of the whole is the *subiect* of the *coniunction* (relation), i.e. the parts. The form of the *coniunctio coordinationis*. The possibility of this form is cognized either *per intellectum*: through space and time, or *per rationem*: through *rationem aut causam communem*.",["\"sel\" in the struck-out phrase is a broken-off word and is left as it stands.","*per intellectum* / *per rationem*: through the understanding / through reason; *rationem aut causam communem*: a common ground or cause."]],"reflm:4049":["The consequences are also combined with the grounds, but not as parts [with] in a whole. The combination of the parts with the whole is [to] according to the rule of identity, but that of the parts among one another is not according to the same.\nOften one has first the idea of the whole or of the *dispertibilitaet*; e.g. universal concept with regard to the classes, space with regard to the parts.\nAn ideal or real whole, the former either logical or arbitrary.",["\"consequences\": Folgen; \"combined\", \"combination\": verbunden, Verbindung.","*dispertibilitaet*: divisibility into separate parts (dispertibilitas), kept in Kant's form.","Last line Latin: Totum ideale aut reale, prius vel logicum vel arbitrarium."]],"reflm:4050":["Extensive or intensive quantity. Degree.\nWhat with respect to some *quantum* cannot be thought except intensively, [is] namely as a ground. e.g. Heat. Wit. God.\n(s A greater degree of heat cannot be composed out of smaller ones. But the calorific force of the rays is infinite and is not limited except by the inertia of matter. Hence by combining out of weak (through rarefaction) rays of light the intensity of heat can increase.)",["The whole passage is Latin; \"ground\": ratio; \"Wit\": Ingenium.","\"by combining out of weak (through rarefaction) rays\" keeps the Latin order: combinando ex debilibus (per rarefactionem) radiis lucis."]],"reflm:4051":["»*Prima matheseos intensorum principia*« (first principles of the mathematics of intensive things).\nMaximum and minimum: deceptive concepts.",["The first line is a title in quotation marks and is kept in Latin; \"deceptive concepts\": conceptus deceptores."]],"reflm:4052":["Because logical *subiecte* can in turn be predicates, we are |\naccording to laws of reason necessitated to think to ourselves a last *subiect*; this is substance.",[]],"reflm:4053":["Are substances *realiter* distinct from *accidentibus*? is it not the substance itself, which I can think through all its predicates, and does even the least remain over, in order to distinguish it from its predicates. Only the succession of something, while something remains one and the same, seems to confirm the distinction.",["\"of something, while something\": von einigem, indem einiges; it may also be read \"of some things, while some things\"."]],"reflm:4054":["The concept of substance has, besides the idea of the *subiect*, also the concept of persistence in that which follows upon one another, and of sameness in this sequence, which one therefore calls alterations of the very same thing, with it. But because [as well] all *accidentia* are variable and the *substantiale* is not known at all, the persistence of the *substantialis* is assumed *precario*. Even consciousness can flow. Above all if everything endures only through the divine force.",["\"sameness\": Einerleyheit; \"alterations\": Veränderungen.","*substantialis* stands in the genitive as written; *precario*: as on sufferance, without right."]],"reflm:4055":["The word \"predicate\" indicates no new distinction of things at all; hence every thing or the concept of it can be predicated of something. e.g. rational beings are spirits. Thus [if] nothing can exist without being a predicate of other things. The human being is a predicate of a rational inhabitant of the earth.\nThis logical *respectus* can be transformed into a real one, if [it] something exists in another, not in the possible concept of another, and indeed as in the real ground. For a part is in the whole from logical grounds, because the whole is only in part something other than its *partes*; otherwise all *partes* would lie in one whole (not in the |\nplace so to speak for all these parts) and the whole would thus still be something other than all the parts together.",["\"predicated of something\": wovon praedicirt werden.","\"place\": Platze, not Ort."]],"reflm:4056":["Every substance has a force, is an identical proposition. For substance is properly the *subiect* which contains the ground of the accidents and of the effects; consequently the concept of substance arises from the necessity of the force of a *subiect*.",["\"force\": Kraft."]],"reflm:4057":["Under and \nThat whose representation properly [is that through which we something] is the predicate of the representation of another thing is its determination; and if it is something positive in itself, then it adheres to others (inherence).",["\"Under and\" (Unter und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"adheres to\": hängt ... an; \"inherence\": inhaerentz."]],"reflm:4058":["60. E II 1058.\nThe concept of substance depends on inner sense. Just so the *phaenomena* also teach us only the concomitance or consequence, hence not the *compositionem* or *nexum* and the ground, but *phaenomena* of them. Thus the *axiomata phaenomenorum* are not to be regarded as *intellectualia*. E.g. to be sure, everything that happens has a determining ground and follows upon something else according to a determinate law; but that is no rule for the actual actions which we perceive in ourselves.",["\"concomitance or consequence\": concomitantz oder conseqventz."]],"reflm:4059":["All successions are alterations of the same abiding sub|ject.\nThe substances do not flow, but their *status*; so reason demands it, so experience shows it; whence this agreement of experience with reason?",["\"abiding\": bleibenden."]],"reflm:4060":["If the very same *subiect* x is to be after one another a and non, then the *subiect* must not be altered; otherwise x would first be a and then y non a.\nThe *substantiale* is unalterable. For in the succession of the accidents it is always the same, and in *ortu* and *interitu* it is not altered.",["\"a and non\" as written (a und non).","*ortu* and *interitu*: arising and perishing."]],"reflm:4061":["In every composite all composition can be cancelled, but not in every *Qvanto*. In all parts of space parts can be indicated, but not in any one part of space can all parts be indicated.",["\"cancelled\": aufheben; \"indicated\": anzeigen."]],"reflm:4062":["In every whole [parts] several things are coordinated, not subordinated; therefore ground and consequent do not make a whole.",["Latin: ratio et rationatum, \"ground and consequent\"."]],"reflm:4063":["Every whole is composed either of substances or of accidents or of relations; since relations are either of substances or of accidents, the latter will be time, the former space.",[]],"reflm:4064":["That in composition through which it is distinguished from all other is *modus compositionis*.",[]],"reflm:4065":["That every *compositum substantiale* consists of simple parts does not signify that through the analysis of the concept of the *compositi* one arrives at simple parts, but: that according to the law of human cognition the material of the composite, i.e. the simple, precedes the form. It therefore proves nothing objectively.",["\"analysis\": Zergliederung; \"material\": material."]],"reflm:4066":["Every substantial composite consists of simple substances. For since the parts are substances, [possu] their existence is subsistence, and they can exist even if they are not composed with one another; therefore all composition can be abrogated, in such a way that nevertheless all the parts of such a composite remain. But when all composition has been abrogated, the remaining parts are simple.\nThis proof applies only to the *compositum substantiale*, [which] and its possibility can be thought a priori, i.e. *synthetice*, because then *partes non compositae* make up the *principia* of the *synthesis* through which the idea of the *compositi* arises. But a *compositum accidentale* is not always of that kind. Time and space have no parts which could [be] take place even without all composition.",["The first paragraph is Latin; [possu] is a struck-out, broken-off word.","\"abrogated\": abrogari."]],"reflm:4067":["A [unity] plurality we cannot without (g absolute) unity |\nthink, if the [*data* as well] whole plurality is to be thought through reason. But if it is given as a phenomenon, then the analysis can be *absque termino*.",["\"plurality\": Vielheit.","*absque termino*: without end-term."]],"reflm:4068":["Composition is an *accidens*. Because the *substantiale* is thought before all *accidentibus*, the parts are thought before all connection.",["\"connection\": Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:4069":["Opposite and \nThings which exist in the very same time are at the same time; those which exist at different, are after one another.",["\"Opposite and\" (Gegenüber und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"at different\": zu verschiedener, with \"time\" understood and not supplied.","\"at the same time\" / \"after one another\": zugleich / nach einander."]],"reflm:4070":["Time is everywhere, or the parts of space are at the same time. Newton.\nSpace is at all times, this signifies: that time extends over all concepts, outer as well as inner sensations, but not that the concept of space extends over time.\nThat is not the shortest way which goes through the smallest space, but that which can be completed in the shortest time; just as that book is the shortest through which I can comprehend the object in the smallest time.",["\"completed\": absolvirt; \"comprehend\": begreifen."]],"reflm:4071":["[All] The concept of time is a singular concept. For all [parts of the] different times are after one another, consequently contained in one whole of time. No two years can be at the same time. Time has only one dimension, but existence measured through time has two.\n after one another\nat the same time\nSpace is nothing actual, but a possibility which has its ground in something actual. The possibility of the alterations of things is grounded first on their connection (for one thing alone would not alter), secondly on their dependence on another; for the being which as to its existence does not depend on another would not be able to alter without ceasing.\nOne cannot think any space, e.g. cubic foot, without an outer space which surrounds it, and thus no space without contained in the whole. Likewise no two spaces without a determinate distance and position relative to one another.",["\"singular\": einzelner; \"dimension\": Abmessung.","\"after one another\" / \"at the same time\" stand on their own lines in the source.","\"without contained in the whole\": ohne in dem Ganzen enthalten, as elliptical as the German."]],"reflm:4072":["The concepts of understanding are reflected representations and also have their elements of reflection; space is an intuitive representation.",["\"concepts of understanding\": Verstandesbegriffe."]],"reflm:4073":["Concepts are either intuitive or [rational] reflected.\nThe former either sensitive intuitions or pure intuitions, insofar as either the matter or the sole form of sensitive representation is present. Reflected concepts are either also empirical or pure; the former are universal concepts containing matter given by the senses, the latter only form |\n[conc] containing.[the former The former are intellect] Space and time are concepts of the pure understanding. Metaphysical notions are concepts of pure reason.\nSpace is either determining (possible) or determined (actual).",["The passage is Latin; \"sensitive\": sensitivus; \"understanding\": intellectus.","[conc] and \"intellect\" in [priores Priores sunt intellect] are broken-off words kept as they stand."]],"reflm:4074":["Above, beside and \nThe persistence of the existence of all things in One is the *omnipraesentia*, space.\nThe persistence of all existence of each thing in One is conservation, time.\nIf the whole existence of a thing depends on one, then it is of contingent nature; if only the beginning depends on it, then [sei] the duration of its state is contingent.",["\"Above, beside and\" (Über, neben und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"persistence\": Beharrung; \"conservation\": Erhaltung."]],"reflm:4075":["If all existence depends on one as well *extensive* as *protensive* breaks off.",["\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) records that the note ends here."]],"reflm:4076":["The whole existence possible at all is called eternity. The [existence] eternal existence [with], which is properly only the [boundary] continuous limitation of eternity, is in time as it were a continuous beginning in the one *respectu* and continuous beginning in the other. Is related as motion through a line to the extension in this line.",["\"limitation\": Begrentzung; \"[boundary]\": Grentze.","\"beginning\" stands twice in the source (Anfang ... Anfang); the reading is doubtful."]],"reflm:4077":["Time and space precede the things: that is quite |\nnatural. Both namely are subjective conditions, [under] under which alone objects can be given to the senses. Taken objectively this would be absurd. Hence the difficulty of the place of the world and of the time before the world. Yet in absolute time no place is determined without actual things, thus absolute time cannot furnish a ground of the explanation of the *phaenomenorum*.",["\"place\": Orte; \"explanation\": Erklarung."]],"reflm:4078":["That space is a mere *phaenomenon* and something subjective, but not a representation of the things, is to be seen from this, because in it relations are represented which yet are no effects, but merely grounds of the possibility of the effects, and these grounds are themselves no things.",["\"things\" here renders Sachen, not Dinge."]],"reflm:4079":["Perhaps the eternity of the world is a consequence of the infinity of it as a *quanti simultanei*; for from a limited number of causes the effects must come to an end, but from an infinite one the combinations and consequences are infinite.\nThe difficulty of representing to oneself a *Quantum simultaneum* as infinite rests on the nature of the human understanding, which can think a *totum* as to its possibility only synthetically, i.e. *successive addendo unum uni*. But the *synthesis* which is to go to infinity is never complete. On the other hand one can very well think an *infinitum successivum*, precisely because the *synthesis* has no *terminos*. But precisely |\nfor that reason it is also impossible according to the nature of our understanding to think this successive series as whole and completed in the idea of a being.",["\"consequence\", \"consequences\": folge, folgen.","*successive addendo unum uni*: by successively adding one to one."]],"reflm:4080":["Opposite the running head »Finitum et infinitum«.\nInfinitely great is what in the *synthesi partium dabilium aeqvalium* has no last. What in the *analysi* has no last is infinitely divisible.",["*synthesi partium dabilium aeqvalium*: synthesis of equal parts that can be given.","The first line is the AA's locus note."]],"reflm:4081":["(g These propositions show how one mixes up the subjective rule with the objective. It follows only that we could not distinguish such things, [but not] or that the [things] concepts many would not belong to them.)\nOn M § 270, first sentence:\nFor they would be distinguished only by places (g and times). thus properly be only just the same at different times and places. For the substance itself must be presupposed before all relation; and if that is the same, then it is one and the same thing.\nThe difference of the [things] places does not constitute a difference of the things themselves, but presupposes it. i.e. [it is the differ] a thing stands in relation to itself if it is in relation with one that is one and the same with it.",["die [Dinge] Begriffe viel nicht zukommen würden: viel (perhaps for viele, many) is doubtful and the clause is left as it stands.","einerley Ding / mit ihm einerley: one and the same (einerley); Verhältnis and relation both rendered relation.","und wenn die dieselbe ist: die taken as demonstrative (that), referring back ambiguously."]],"reflm:4082":["For in that case they would be distinguished only by magnitude, i.e. one would be completely equal and similar to the part of the other, *qvod contra prius* (which is contrary to the preceding).* The following proposition lies at the basis here.\n*\n(g Likewise, since a smaller magnitude can be transformed into a greater, they would, if (g the lack) of sameness rests only on the circumstance, be able to become completely congruent.)",["einerleyheit: sameness; the inserted (g der Mangel) leaves the construction of the clause loose, and it is kept so.","liegt hier zum Grunde: lies at the basis (Grund)."]],"reflm:4083":["For equal things must be *homogenea*, hence *totaliter aeqvalia* be *totaliter homogenea*. (s *totaliter aeqvalia* here means equal in every relation.)",["*homogenea*: homogeneous; *totaliter aeqvalia*: totally equal; *totaliter homogenea*: totally homogeneous.","Beziehung: relation; heissen: literally are called."]],"reflm:4084":["Rest is the enduring presence of a body in the same place.\nIf in a given place a body did not act, change of place would not be a succession of action. A force of change is properly not thinkable [but rather a force], but it is necessary that mutable accidents be thought beforehand, whose actuation or removal requires force. Therefore motive force is the ground on account of which a certain unknown force is present successively in different places. But if the force of presence in any place whatever is again called motive, the same is explained by the same. It is evident therefore that we cognize in bodies nothing but determinations of space and time, but do not cognize intellectually the absolute marks of the subjects.",["The passage is Latin. *perdurabilis*: enduring; *ratio*: ground; *vis motrix*: motive force.","*idem per idem explicatur*: the same is explained by the same.","*cognoscere*: cognize; *notas*: marks."]],"reflm:4085":["The idea of the world is not arbitrary. For just as I must finally think a |\npart that is no further a whole, so I must think a whole that is no part.",["endlich: finally, at last; the German can also be read as finite (think a part as finite)."]],"reflm:4086":["* There exists a world, i.e. a whole that is no part of any actual (possible) whole. From this it can be concluded:\n1. The world is a *compositum substantiale*. Thus does not consist of necessary beings. Its parts also do not hang together through themselves. [After] For this reason, because they are through one cause. Space is the *phaenomenon* of the existence of all things through one. Infinity. The *commercium* of substances is possible thereby.\n2. By its having its boundary (s of subordination and of coordination) *a priori*, it has an origin. whether also a beginning? The beginning of the world is considered only with respect to the world, but not to something outside the world.\n(g 3. On the order of nature. 4. Ladder of beings. texture, figure, mixture.)\nThe whole of the world and of its state.\nIt depends on the supreme cause, but is not *in commercio* with it. There are no more worlds besides this one, although possible instead of this one, because there is only one cause. This holds with respect to the things themselves; but with respect to the supreme cause only one is hypothetically possible.\nWe are thus to infer from a world which, how great and how perfect it may be, is undetermined, to a supreme cause that is infinitely |\ngreater than the ground of proof of its existence. From this it is evident that the existence of this supreme and sole cause and its all-sufficiency must already be known otherwise, and the existence of the world must give proof only of the existence of a necessary one.\nThe composition of a *totius substantiarum* is a proof of contingency;\nThe limitation of each thing is a proof of contingency.\nThe alteration of each thing is a proof of contingency.\nWhether from space as the ground of the possibility of the *commercii* of the *substantiarum* according to laws of sensibility one could not infer to the intuition of a cause valid for all things that can stand *in commercio*. And from infinite time as the ground of the possibility of the duration of things to the eternity and necessity of the cause.\n(g A *compositum* that is no *totum absolute tale* is, *tanqvam pars*, possible only through the limitation of the *totius*; thus everything that holds of a thing only as *parte* presupposes the concept of the whole.)\n*(g In every *composito actuali* no part is *complete* determined but through all the rest together, consequently the thoroughgoing determination goes from all coordinated substances to one, thus it is at all times a part of a *toto absoluto*, i.e. of a world. But in a *composito potentiali* every part is *complete* determined, because the rest are posited in addition to it and have no influence on the existence of the former; thus the sequence [therefore not com] is not necessarily complete. But if in a series of things following upon one another there is a last, then there is also a first.)",["eines nothwendigen: the adjective (necessary) has no noun in the German, and none is supplied.","Beweisgrund: ground of proof; Grentze: boundary; Einschränkung: limitation; Veränderung: alteration.","kein Theil complete determinirt wie durch alle übrige: wie read as other than (but); *complete*: completely. *totum absolute tale*: a whole absolutely as such; *tanqvam pars*: as a part."]],"reflm:4087":["Dependent is a ring in the chain; and that on which others depend |\nand which is itself independent is the uppermost ring. According to laws of gravity that one too must depend, because the lower links are a ground of the sinking of the upper ones. but in itself a thing could be with its consequences without any foreign ground.",["abhängen / abhängend: depend, dependent; the German also means hang (down), which the image of the chain and of gravity turns on.","Folgen: consequences (Grund and Folge, ground and consequence)."]],"reflm:4088":["Relation of the world to the empty intuition outside the world.\nThe world's unity or plurality.\n- -\n- -\nContingency or necessity. Dependence.\n- -\n- -\nFiniteness or infinity.\nConnection (g contingency) and unconnectedness.\n(s Composition.* Community.)\nmetaphysical perfection or imperfection.\n(s Natural and supernatural.)\n(s Egoist)\n(s *Partes materiales et immateriales* (material and immaterial parts).)\n( s *partes repraesentativae* (representative parts): whether simple? whether they fill space? )\nTogether with the sensitive. Of eternity, of immensity, of the beginning, of continuity.\n*(s *Systemata commercii substantiarum* (systems of the community of substances). If the cause of the harmony is external and merely arbitrary, then the state of the one does not necessarily agree with that of the others, but everything comes down to choice; thus there is no necessary ground that would be universally valid for the relation of the things; thus they do not constitute a whole; or the harmony does not follow sufficiently from the things, is thus no *influxus*.)",["contingentz (in the insertion) and Zufälligkeit are both rendered contingency; contingentz is the Latinate word.","Verhaltnis and Beziehung are both rendered relation; Beziehung is the second (of the things).","willkührlich: arbitrary; Willkühr: choice."]],"reflm:4089":["[*Multitudo Complexus*]\nA multitude of subordinated things is a series. A series is of things subordinated either sensitively or intellectually.",["The passage is Latin. *Multitudo Complexus*: multitude, complex (struck through by Kant).","*subordinatorum*: of subordinated things; *sensitive vel intellectualiter*: sensitively or intellectually."]],"reflm:4090":["Over, beside and \nThe impossibility of a *regressus* in a series of the *successionum* (not *causatorum*) comes from this, that the series is regarded as an *absolutum*, completed, and we, who can determine magnitudes only successively, yet can never cognize it [full] *complet*. This, however, does not prove its impossibility.\nBut whether it be a *causatum* of another is not asked at all thereby.",["The first line breaks off in the source after und.","solche / ihre: plural in the German, referring either to the magnitudes or to the series (die Reihe); the English it/its follows the series, and the reference is open.","*successionum*: of successions; *causatorum*: of things caused; *complet*: completely."]],"reflm:4091":["To blind necessity is opposed the absolute (s natural) (rational).\nTo blind chance is opposed freedom (intellectual).\n(s are both contrary to the order which makes cognitions of experience possible, thus impossible in regard to the objects thereof.)",["rationale, intellectuale: Latinate forms in the German (rational, intellectual).","Ohngefehr: chance; Gegenstände: objects."]],"reflm:4092":["In the series of marks subordinated to one another no two are the nearest; all differences are divisible to infinity.",[]],"reflm:4093":["All alteration happens in a time. For between the two |\nmoments of the one state and of the following there is a time, thus the alteration has happened in an infinite multitude of moments between both states. Let us posit that the substance persisted through a time under certain conditions in the same state: then it will persist therein eternally under the same conditions.\nBetween two states are two moments, and between two moments is a time, and in this time the subject is at all times in another state.",["Veränderung: alteration; Augenblik: moment; beharren: persist."]],"reflm:4094":["Egoism can be refuted only by this, that no alteration is possible in a single substance alone.\nThe idealist regards all his outer (g actual) representations as *spontaneas*. The egoist [as if also no outwardly] all outer possible ones.",["*spontaneas*: spontaneous ones; the last sentence has no verb, and the construction of the first is understood.","äußere: outer; wirkliche: actual."]],"reflm:4095":["Essence is the first logical ground of the inner determinations;\nNature is the first real ground of the inner determinations.\nThe first: the ground of that which is [constantly] necessary in a thing;\nThe second: the ground of that which is possible through a thing.\n(s 1. That through which a thing is possible.\n2. What is possible through a thing.\nNature we know through experience.)",["Wesen: essence; kennen: know."]],"reflm:4096":["The real essence is nature.",["realwesen: real essence (Wesen)."]],"reflm:4097":["The necessary of the concept constitutes the essence.\nThe necessary of actuality makes nature. is the permanent.\nNature is the *principium* of contingencies.\nWe cognize through experience nature sooner than essence, e.g. the hardness of steel. Essence and nature are both permanent.\nThe first of that which a thing is, is called essence.\nThe first of that which happens through a thing is called nature. The essence of bodies consists in impenetrable extension; to the nature of bodies belongs *inertia*. The cognition of nature is grounded on experience or at least contingent relation of the thing itself.",["zufallig Verhältnis: the adjective is uninflected in the German; the construction (on contingent relation) is left as loose as it stands.","Wirklichkeit: actuality; Zufälligkeiten: contingencies."]],"reflm:4098":["progression.\nThe infinity of the series as such is possible, but not the infinity of the *aggregat*. The former is an infinite possibility (of additions), the latter an infinite (actual) taking-together.",["Hinzuthuungen: additions; Zusammennehmung: taking-together, comprehension.","*aggregat*: aggregate."]],"reflm:4099":["The greatest of an *aggregat* is impossible; for it would be an *apposition* to which none would be possible.",["*apposition*: a placing-beside, addition; none (keine) refers back to apposition."]],"reflm:4100":["The incompetence of reason to set limitations to sensibility itself under the condition of this intuition, both of expansion and of division, and of both according to time and to space. Reason can set limitations to the empirical with regard to the rational [e.g. if one that] and restrict empiricism, e.g. that everything be extended, that before every series in the world another always precede and there be no first origin; but it cannot restrict sensualism.",["The first sentence has no verb in the German.","Schranken: limitations; einschränken: restrict; Ausbreitung: expansion (not Ausdehnung, extension)."]],"reflm:4101":["In the *harmonia generaliter stabilita* the dancing master, for example, is also apt for walking at the same time instead of dancing; in the *harmonia praestabilita*, however, there is no [ground] general ground at all, but all determination is individual.",["*harmonia generaliter stabilita*: generally established harmony; *harmonia praestabilita*: preestablished harmony."]],"reflm:4102":["Opposite and \n*effectus vel naturae vel libertatis* (effect either of nature or of freedom).\n*actio vel naturalis vel libera* (action either natural or free).",["The first line breaks off in the source after und; Gegenüber: opposite, over against."]],"reflm:4103":["What is in different places admits of no cognition through one [outer] inner sense. hence no selfhood.",["einen: one or an (inner sense); the German does not decide between them."]],"reflm:4104":["*Systema emanationis (e Deo vel parentibus)*; *systema creationis*.\n*Materialismus*: *systema [propagationis] generationis naturalis, epigenesis psychologica*.\n(s Bad habit or naughtiness, e.g. violent heat.)\nWhether everything in the character of the child depends solely on the man or the woman. The system of the *ovulorum* presupposes that, if the woman had had another man, she would nevertheless have begotten the same children; that of the *animalculorum*: if the man had had another woman, he would have gotten the same children. This is very practical; for in the first case a [father] man has to look very much to the constitution of the woman and her race, in the second he has not to look to it at all, but only the woman to the race of the man. According to the *epigenesi* one must look to both: 1. because of the alternative, secondly because of the mixture. With free *praeexistentz* to neither of the two.\nBefore birth was the soul a pure spirit? Did it find itself in the spiritual life? Has it already lived on earth, or does it live in two worlds, the pneumatic and the mechanical, at the same time?",["*Systema emanationis (e Deo vel parentibus)*: system of emanation (from God or the parents); *systema creationis*: system of creation; *systema [propagationis] generationis naturalis*: system [of propagation] of natural generation.","*ovulorum*: of the little eggs; *animalculorum*: of the animalcules; *epigenesi*, *praeexistentz*: epigenesis, preexistence.","Unart oder Ungezogenheit: bad habit or naughtiness (ill-breeding); Hitze: heat, also hot temper."]],"reflm:4105":["The perpetual duration of substances, i.e. the equal age of each with the whole world, cannot so much be proved as that it must lie at the basis in the method of philosophizing.",["nicht so wohl ... als daß: not so much ... as that; the comparison is kept as loose as the German."]],"reflm:4106":["All proofs of the immortality of the soul* must be conducted in such a way that they hold not merely for some but for all (g and not merely for a short existence after death, but for an eternal duration). They can therefore not be inferred from the necessity that the world must at all times have spectators who connect its epochs, because from this it does not yet follow that we, who have once begun to live, must always live.\n1. Not from divine justice. For firstly this would hold only of some, whose virtue and vice have not been sufficiently requited in this world. And 2ndly it would prove only a short existence, which would cease after the requital.\n2. Not from the cognized immortal nature of the soul. Here the grounds of the opposite are preponderant.\n(s not from empirical but from rational grounds.)\n3. Only from the analogy with other things of nature. Human capacities have no correct relation to the post in this world.\n*(s *Imo* they are either derived from the nature of the soul from empirical principles or concepts *a priori* of the thinking being in general. The first is psychological, the second properly metaphysical;\nor *IIdo* they are teleological, either of the analogy of the physical ends or of the moral ones. According to the order of nature.)",["*Imo*, *IIdo*: firstly, secondly.","Principien: principles (the Latinate word, not Grundsatz); Fahigkeiten: capacities; Posten: post, station.","verknüpfen: connect; Vergeltung / vergolten: requital / requited."]],"reflm:4107":["Life cannot derive from the combination with the lifeless; rather, this must diminish a degree of life. Birth can thus be no beginning of life in general, but only of animal life, and death the end of the same.",["Verbindung: combination; herrühren: derive, stem from."]],"reflm:4634":["M XXII:\nWe know every object only through predicates that we say or think of it. Before that, what is encountered of representations in us is to be counted only as materials, but not as cognition. Hence an object is only a something in general, which we think to ourselves through certain predicates that make up its concept. In every judgment there are accordingly two predicates, which we compare with one another. Of which the one, [through which to us the] which makes up the given cognition of the object, is called the logical *subiect*, the second, which is compared with it, the logical *praedicat*. If I say: a body is divisible, then it signifies as much as: Something [which] x, which I know under the predicates that together make|up a concept of body,\nI also think through the predicate of divisibility. x  is the same as xb. Now a as well as b belongs to x. Only |\nM XXIII: |\nin different ways: either that b already lies in that which makes up the concept a, and thus can be found through analysis of it, or that b belongs to the x without being included and comprehended in a. In the first case the judgment is analytic, in the second synthetic. The case cited is of an analytic judgment, but the proposition: Every body is heavy, is a *synthesis*; the one predicate is not involved in the subject, but added. Now all analytic judgments can be seen into a priori; and what can be cognized only a posteriori is synthetic. Hence the properly empirical judgments are synthetic. But there are nevertheless judgments, [which a] whose validity seems to stand firm a priori, which are nonetheless synthetic, e.g. Everything alterable has a cause; whence does one come to these judgments? [through which ha] whence do we take it, to associate with one concept another of the very same object, which no observation and experience shows in it. Nonetheless all proper *axiomata* are such synthetic propositions, *e.g.* between two points there can be only one straight line. On the other hand the proposition: [Every magnitude is itself If one Every part is smal] Every magnitude is equal to itself, is an analytic proposition. The *principium* or the norm of all analytic propositions is the principle of contradiction and of identity. It is (if I take them both together) no *axioma*, but a formula, i.e. a universal model of analytic propositions; for it contains no *medium terminum*.\nWe have accordingly judgments a posteriori which are synthetic, but also judgments a priori which are nevertheless synthetic and for that reason cannot be derived from any experience, because they contain a true universality, hence necessity, as well as comprehend in themselves nothing but concepts which could not have been drawn from experience. [Whence] These concepts may dwell in us from wherever they will: whence do we take this connection of them. Are they revelations, prejudices etc etc.\nIf certain concepts in us contain nothing other than that through which all experiences are possible on our side, then they can be said a priori before experience and yet with complete validity for everything that may ever come before us. They then hold, to be sure, not of things in general, but yet of everything that can ever be given to us through experience, because they contain the conditions through which these experiences are possible. Such propositions will thus contain the condition of the possibility not of things, but of experience. |\nM XXIV: |\nBut things that cannot be given to us through any experience are nothing for us; thus we can very well use such propositions as universal with a practical aim, only not as *principia* of speculation about objects in general.\nNow in order to settle what sort of concepts those are which must necessarily precede all experience and through which this is alone possible, which are thus given a priori and also contain the ground for judgments a priori, we must analyze an experience in general. In every experience there is something through which an object is given to us, and something through which it is thought. If we take the conditions that lie in the activities of the mind through which alone it can be given, then one can cognize something of the objects a priori. If we take that through which alone it can be thought, then one can also cognize something a priori of all possible objects. For through this alone does something become for us an object or a cognition of it.\nWe will investigate the former. That through which an object (g of experience) is given to us is called appearance. The possibility of appearances is, on the side of the human mind, sensibility. In sensibility there is a matter, which is called sensation, and in regard to which and its diversity we are merely passive, and the manifoldness of impressions brings it about that we find nothing a priori in us which we could know from ourselves a priori before the impressions. One cannot ever in thought |\nrepresent to oneself any [new] impression of a new kind. But appearances also have a form, a ground lying in our subject, through which we order either the impressions themselves or that which corresponds to them and give to each part of them its place. This [is] can be nothing other than an activity which is indeed naturally excited by the impressions, but yet can be cognized by itself.\n(When we posit something in space and in time, we act; when we posit it next to and after one another, we connect. These actions are only means to bring about those places; but one can take them separately; when we posit the same thing several times or in the one action at the same time posit the other, this is a kind of actions through which we posit something in accordance with the rule of appearances, whereby this positing must have its particular rules, which are distinguished from the conditions of the form, as they are to be placed with regard to the appearance.)",["“know” renders kennen, “cognize” erkennen, “analysis” Zergliederung; “principle of contradiction” renders Satz des Wiederspruchs.","“x  is the same as xb” is kept as printed: a letter appears to be missing before the double space (perhaps “xa”).","“cannot ever … any” renders the German double negative “keinen … niemals”; “places” (Stellen) and “to be placed” (zu stellen) keep Kant's wordplay."]],"reflm:4635":["Not every object is a thing (Sache). That in the representation through which it has its peculiar function represents [the thing itself] the *Obiect* (real function); its function in the judgment is the respective function, through which it has a manifold validity with regard to other representations. The first function goes to that which lies in the representation; the second, what can be cognized through it.",["“object” renders obiect / Obiect (Kant's Latinate spelling), not Gegenstand; “thing” renders Sache.","“manifold validity” renders vielgültigkeit, literally “many-validity”."]],"reflm:4636":["Cognitions a priori must not go at all to determinate things (because these are not yet given), but to universal representations of |\nthings in general and thus to intuitions (g not sensations, for these are that through which something determinate is given) or to thoughts in general. (One can intuit something without thinking anything thereby or under it.)\n(g All cognitions come about in us through thinking, i.e. through concepts; they are not intuitions.)",["“determinate” renders bestimmt (also “determined”).","“must not go at all” renders müssen gar nicht … gehen, which can also be read “need not go at all”."]],"reflm:4637":["That belonging to our cognitions through which the objects are [given] thought (for space is only the way in which they are given to us) is either the thinking of an object in general: *possibile*[it may be given or not], or how it can be given in general by ourselves: *qvantitas*, or how it must be given to us through experience.",["“in general” after “given” renders überhaupt, which may attach to “can be given” or to “by ourselves”."]],"reflm:4638":["All cognition consists in judgments. The judgments may now be immediate or mediate (syllogisms); [a determinate] Thinking is called judging. Even the concepts are predicates. Accordingly [cognitions] concepts to which no object is given, but which are nevertheless supposed to express the ways of thinking objects in general, will contain in themselves that which in judgments is thought relatively of two concepts to one another.\nCommonly valid concepts have a logical [function] place [the place] among concepts. The place in a judgment is a logical function; it is bricht ab.\nThe determinate logical function of a representation in general is the [real] pure concept of the understanding.",["“syllogisms” renders Vernunftschlüsse; “commonly valid” renders Gemeingültige.","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note that the sentence is unfinished, kept as printed."]],"reflm:4639":["That the necessity of mere truths of reason involves a kind of existence is to be judged from the little word “is”. E.g. one cannot say: all triangles will be three-cornered.",["“triangles” renders the Latinate triangel, “three-cornered” the German dreyeckigt; “will be” renders the future werden … werden."]],"reflm:4640":["Cause and effect seems to be for [fantasy] *praevision*, whole and part for fantasy, substance and accident for the senses, possibility for the faculty of invention, actuality for [intuition] sensation, magnitude to intuition, necessity for the w bricht ab.\nIn all these items the constant combination makes the ground of an abstract concept of the understanding. For those powers are combining, and from this arises the possibility of judgments a priori and also of these concepts.\nEverything subjective relates ultimately to sensation, hence every objective to substance.\nSince all form (g of the subjective) is grounded on one determination being combined together with the other, inherence is grounded on intuition, the connection of the effect with the cause on imitation, conversely on anticipation, whole and part on imagination.",["“fantasy” renders phantasie, “faculty of invention” Dichtungsvermögen; “imitation”, “anticipation” and “imagination” render Nachbildung, Vorbildung and Einbildung (not Einbildungskraft).","“magnitude to intuition” keeps Kant's shift from vor to auf; “the w” and “bricht ab” (breaks off, the AA's note) mark the broken-off sentence."]],"reflm:4641":["The distinct representation of that which one\n[sees] intuits or which one thinks.\n*synthesis*\n*analysis*\nOne can still transform a sensible concept or concept of appearance into a concept of reason.\nDistinctness is opposed to indistinctness, order to confusion. [Through the understanding] Order depends on one's giving each partial representation its place, thus it is not merely the consciousness of this partial representation (distinctness), but also the [co-or] co- or subordination. Distinctness of the understanding depends on analysis and receives order through the logical places.",["“analysis” in the last sentence renders Zergliederung; “partial representation” renders Theilvorstellung."]],"reflm:4642":["Everything that a priori makes a law of our representations, [every *actus*] hence the relation of the representations to a (g a priori established) universal law, is the object. That shall be thought under it, represents the thing as it is, i.e. as it holds for all appearances. But for that reason it must be something determinable a priori, because only thereby can we think; for thinking signifies nothing other than determining representations from the universal.\nThe concepts of the understanding express all *actus* of the powers of the mind insofar as representations are possible according to their universal laws, and indeed [that] this their possibility a priori.",["“object” renders obiect; “thing” renders Sache; “powers of the mind” renders Gemüthskräfte.","“That shall be thought under it, represents” keeps an elliptical construction: Das may be demonstrative or stand for “that which”."]],"reflm:4643":["Transcendental is every pure cognition a priori in which, therefore, no sensation is given.\n1. The transcendental aesthetic.\n2. The transcendental logic.\n3. The transcendental critique.\n4. —\n—\n—\n—\nArchitectonic.\nOf that which one knows only through inner experience one cannot say the predicates of space (hence the soul a spirit).",["“knows” renders kennt; “spirit” renders Geist."]],"reflm:4644":["If one uses of a thing in general [what] a priori predicates of space and time, then this is a *petitio phaenomenorum*.\nIf one uses of things that one knows only under the appearance of space universal [concepts] real concepts of the understanding (of the kind through which the things themselves are given to the understanding, and their quality, not the magnitude or possibility), then one commits a *petitionem noumeni*. The former is *synthesis subrepticia*, the latter *analysis subrepticia*.\nQuantity presents no thing itself, but indeed *substantia*, *causa*, *compositum*.",["“thing” renders Ding in the first sentence and Sache/Sachen thereafter.","*petitionem noumeni* is kept in the accusative as Kant wrote it."]],"reflm:4645":["In all cognitions is in the object\n1. the matter and the form of them, i.e. the quality.\n2. The difference, how it is given.\n3. The quantity to be noted.\nIn a judgment the matter is subject and predicate, but the form of each consists precisely in this, that the one is called subject etc etc.\nThe positing is copula: *est*. Quantity concerns both, either that the subject is a universal concept in itself etc etc.\nHere what is weighed is not what is logical in the judgment, but what is real in the concepts. It is the matter of the concepts.\nTo the matter belong objective *realdata*, e.g. cause, inherence, composition.\nMagnitude concerns only the division of the representation, as it can arise from repetition, but not the real composition and the origin from it with antecedent simple.",["“them” (derselben) and “it” (sie) in items 1–2 may refer to the cognitions or to the object; the list hangs on “is … to be noted”, as in the German.","“what is logical … what is real” renders das Logische … das reale; “with antecedent simple” keeps mit vorhergegangenem Einfachen without a noun."]],"reflm:4646":["We would have no concept of magnitude at all if we did not repeat one several times; thus time and its intuiting counter-image, space, are here wholly effective.\nMagnitude is for pure intuition in general without data.\nQuality for the difference of sensation in general.\nThe categories belonging under it relate to sensations of the relations which are posited through the activity of the soul.\nPosition to the faculty of invention, the [Int] sensation and the bricht ab.\n(g Categories of quantity, quality and position.)",["“intuiting counter-image” renders Anschauendes Gegenbild; “faculty of invention” renders Dichtungsvermögen.","“[Int]” renders the struck-out beginning “[Ans]” (Anschauung); “bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as printed."]],"reflm:4647":["Among the maxims of reason belongs that even that which contains the most distinct relation to ends has nevertheless arisen according to the order of nature.\nMy ontotheological proof, which is correct in this, that according to the order of nature under the same creator we become aware of relations to ends.",["The second sentence has no main verb in the German and is left incomplete."]],"reflm:4648":["Am Innenrand quer:\nMotion is something that happens, thus belongs to the actual appearances and not to the mere sensible form, also presupposes something mo|vable\ni.e. alterable with regard to place, which cannot be cognized a priori, but presupposes empirical concepts, among them also concepts of the understanding.",["“Am Innenrand quer” (on the inner margin, crosswise) is the AA's locus note, kept as printed; “actual” renders wirklichen."]],"reflm:4649":["Am Innenrand quer:\nThe understanding represents things as they are, but not in *intuition*, but in *reflexion*, i.e. not as they are given in general, but as they are thought. That which the appearances of the object for the same sense among themselves bricht ab.",["*intuition* and *reflexion* are Kant's Latin terms; “Am Innenrand quer” (on the inner margin, crosswise) and “bricht ab” (breaks off) are the AA's notes, kept as printed."]]};
